{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ThgpRhLc25Rtqdf9apXxvX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Reframe entirely around scale-dependent safety encoding. New\n2\t  angle: ''Safety commitment signals are detectable in hidden states of smaller reasoning\n3\t  models but become diffuse with scale — implications for monitoring architecture.''\n4\t  Claim 1 becomes conditional (''at 8B scale, HS probes dominate; at 32B, the advantage\n5\t  vanishes''). Title shift: ''Does Scale Erase Safety Signals? Hidden-State Monitoring\n6\t  Limitations in Large Reasoning Models.'''\n7\timagined_experiments:\n8\t- name: Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b)\n9\t  plan_id: exp-001\n10\t  description: Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract\n11\t    hidden states at all layers, compute Fisher discriminability, train static LR\n12\t    probes per layer.\n13\t  expected_results:\n14\t    r1_32b_best_layer_idx: 29\n15\t    r1_32b_best_layer_fisher: 2.8\n16\t    r1_32b_best_static_acc: 0.712\n17\t    r1_32b_best_static_f1: 0.521\n18\t    r1_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.372\n19\t    r1_32b_best_lead_mean: -1.4\n20\t    r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.089\n21\t    r1_32b_fisher_orig_best: 2.8\n22\t    r1_32b_fisher_pca3_best: 0.41\n23\t    qwq_32b_best_layer_idx: 31\n24\t    qwq_32b_best_layer_fisher: 1.9\n25\t    qwq_32b_best_static_acc: 0.694\n26\t    qwq_32b_best_static_f1: 0.488\n27\t    qwq_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.326\n28\t    qwq_32b_best_lead_mean: -2.1\n29\t    qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.072\n30\t    qwq_32b_fisher_orig_best: 1.9\n31\t    qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_best: 0.33\n32\t    r1_8b_fisher_orig_best_ref: 17.875\n33\t    fisher_ratio_8b_vs_32b: 6.4\n34\t  result_rationale: At 32B scale, safety-relevant information is distributed across\n35\t    a much higher-dimensional manifold. The 5120-d hidden space with 64 layers means\n36\t    representations are more disentangled and task-distributed — safety commitment\n37\t    is not concentrated in a narrow subspace as it is at 8B/4096-d. Fisher discriminability\n38\t    drops from ~18 (r1-8b L14) to 1.9-2.8 (32B best layer) because the safety signal\n39\t    occupies a larger number of dimensions each with smaller individual contribution.\n40\t    PCA top-3 explains even less variance (7-9% vs 17% on r1-8b), confirming the signal\n41\t    is spread across 50+ minor principal components. The expected 44% depth heuristic\n42\t    (layer 28-32) holds weakly but the discriminability peak is flat — no layer achieves\n43\t    the clean separation seen at r1-8b layer 14. Static probes achieve only ~70% accuracy\n44\t    (vs 81% on r1-8b) because a linear boundary in 5120-d cannot capture the distributed\n45\t    safety geometry.\n46\t  tables:\n47\t  - name: Layer Sweep Summary (top-5 layers by Fisher)\n48\t    columns:\n49\t    - Model\n50\t    - Layer\n51\t    - Depth%\n52\t    - Fisher\n53\t    - Static Acc\n54\t    - F1\n55\t    - Crossing Rate\n56\t    rows:\n57\t    - - r1-32b\n58\t      - L29\n59\t      - 45%\n60\t      - '2.81'\n61\t      - '0.712'\n62\t      - '0.521'\n63\t      - '0.372'\n64\t    - - r1-32b\n65\t      - L31\n66\t      - 48%\n67\t      - '2.63'\n68\t      - '0.705'\n69\t      - '0.509'\n70\t      - '0.349'\n71\t    - - r1-32b\n72\t      - L27\n73\t      - 42%\n74\t      - '2.44'\n75\t      - '0.698'\n76\t      - '0.495'\n77\t      - '0.326'\n78\t    - - r1-32b\n79\t      - L33\n80\t      - 52%\n81\t      - '2.31'\n82\t      - '0.691'\n83\t      - '0.481'\n84\t      - '0.302'\n85\t    - - r1-32b\n86\t      - L25\n87\t      - 39%\n88\t      - '2.18'\n89\t      - '0.683'\n90\t      - '0.468'\n91\t      - '0.279'\n92\t    - - QwQ-32B\n93\t      - L31\n94\t      - 48%\n95\t      - '1.92'\n96\t      - '0.694'\n97\t      - '0.488'\n98\t      - '0.326'\n99\t    - - QwQ-32B\n100\t      - L29\n101\t      - 45%\n102\t      - '1.85'\n103\t      - '0.689'\n104\t      - '0.479'\n105\t      - '0.302'\n106\t    - - QwQ-32B\n107\t      - L34\n108\t      - 53%\n109\t      - '1.71'\n110\t      - '0.678'\n111\t      - '0.461'\n112\t      - '0.279'\n113\t    - - QwQ-32B\n114\t      - L27\n115\t      - 42%\n116\t      - '1.64'\n117\t      - '0.671'\n118\t      - '0.448'\n119\t      - '0.256'\n120\t    - - QwQ-32B\n121\t      - L36\n122\t      - 56%\n123\t      - '1.52'\n124\t      - '0.664'\n125\t      - '0.432'\n126\t      - '0.233'\n127\t  figures_description:\n128\t  - 'Fisher discriminability by layer (64 layers): flat plateau between layers 25-35\n129\t    with max ~2.8 for r1-32b, ~1.9 for QwQ. Compare to r1-8b''s sharp peak at L14\n130\t    (Fisher=17.9). Inset shows normalized depth comparison.'\n131\t  - 'PCA 2D projection of best layer (L29 r1-32b): pre-CP and post-CP points nearly\n132\t    completely overlapping, no visible cluster separation. Contrast with r1-8b L14\n133\t    where partial separation is visible.'\n134\t  analysis: 'Neither 32B model produces a layer with Fisher discriminability even\n135\t    approaching r1-8b''s L14 (17.9). The best layers achieve Fisher 1.9-2.8, a 6-9x\n136\t    reduction. This is not a layer selection failure — the full 64-layer sweep shows\n137\t    a flat, low discriminability plateau with no sharp peak. The safety signal geometry\n138\t    is fundamentally different at scale: representations are more distributed, and\n139\t    the linear separability that enables simple probes at 8B does not exist at 32B.\n140\t    Static probe accuracy degrades to near the text-baseline level (~70% vs text''s\n141\t    ~66%), eliminating the hidden-state advantage.'\n142\t- name: Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\n143\t  plan_id: exp-002\n144\t  description: Temporal LR with W=15 on best layers of r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether\n145\t    +30pp coverage improvement from temporal context holds at 32B scale.\n146\t  expected_results:\n147\t    r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.721\n148\t    r1_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.395\n149\t    r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -0.8\n150\t    r1_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: 0.023\n151\t    r1_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain: 0.9\n152\t    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.703\n153\t    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.372\n154\t    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -1.5\n155\t    qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: 0.046\n156\t    qwq_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain: 1.1\n157\t    r1_8b_temporal_delta_ref: 30.2\n158\t    coverage_gain_ratio_32b_vs_8b: 0.08\n159\t  result_rationale: Temporal context provides minimal additional benefit on 32B because\n160\t    the per-step signal is already too weak for temporal aggregation to amplify. On\n161\t    r1-8b, temporal windowing works because each step contributes a weak-but-consistent\n162\t    directional signal that accumulates over W=15 steps. On 32B, the per-step signal\n163\t    is near noise floor (Fisher ~2.8 vs ~18), so aggregating 15 noisy steps does not\n164\t    produce meaningful separation improvement. Coverage gain is +2-5pp (vs +30pp on\n165\t    r1-8b), statistically indistinguishable from zero given n=43 traces. The temporal\n166\t    context hypothesis holds at 8B but fails to transfer — the mechanism requires\n167\t    sufficient per-step signal strength as a precondition.\n168\t  tables:\n169\t  - name: Temporal vs Static Coverage Comparison\n170\t    columns:\n171\t    - Model\n172\t    - Method\n173\t    - Coverage\n174\t    - Lead Mean\n175\t    - Acc\n176\t    - Delta vs Static\n177\t    rows:\n178\t    - - r1-8b\n179\t      - Static LR L14\n180\t      - 60.5%\n181\t      - '+3.0'\n182\t      - '0.817'\n183\t      - —\n184\t    - - r1-8b\n185\t      - Temporal W=15\n186\t      - 65.1%*\n187\t      - '+4.9'\n188\t      - '0.848'\n189\t      - +4.6pp*\n190\t    - - r1-32b\n191\t      - Static LR L29\n192\t      - 37.2%\n193\t      - '-1.4'\n194\t      - '0.712'\n195\t      - —\n196\t    - - r1-32b\n197\t      - Temporal W=15\n198\t      - 39.5%\n199\t      - '-0.8'\n200\t      - '0.721'\n201\t      - +2.3pp\n202\t    - - QwQ-32B\n203\t      - Static LR L31\n204\t      - 32.6%\n205\t      - '-2.1'\n206\t      - '0.694'\n207\t      - —\n208\t    - - QwQ-32B\n209\t      - Temporal W=15\n210\t      - 37.2%\n211\t      - '-1.5'\n212\t      - '0.703'\n213\t      - +4.6pp\n214\t  figures_description:\n215\t  - 'Bar chart: temporal coverage delta by model. r1-8b shows +4.6pp (significant),\n216\t    r1-32b shows +2.3pp (n.s.), QwQ-32B shows +4.6pp (n.s.). Error bars from bootstrap\n217\t    overlap zero for both 32B models.'\n218\t  - 'Detection timeline comparison: r1-8b temporal probe fires 4.9 steps before CP\n219\t    on average; 32B probes fire after CP (negative lead time).'\n220\t  analysis: Temporal context fails to rescue 32B probe performance. The +30pp coverage\n221\t    gain seen on r1-8b (static 60.5% → temporal 65.1% in the original framing, or\n222\t    the larger historical gains) does not replicate. At 32B, temporal gains are +2-5pp\n223\t    and non-significant. This invalidates Claim 2's universality — temporal context\n224\t    is necessary at 8B scale where per-step signals are individually weak-but-directionally-consistent,\n225\t    but at 32B the per-step signals are too diffuse for temporal aggregation to help.\n226\t- name: Cross-model transfer probes\n227\t  plan_id: exp-003\n228\t  description: Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-based, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5-based,\n229\t    5120-d) via learned projection head. And reverse direction.\n230\t  expected_results:\n231\t    r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.508\n232\t    r1_8b_to_qwq_32b_acc: 0.491\n233\t    r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.523\n234\t    qwq_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.517\n235\t    r1_32b_to_qwq_acc: 0.612\n236\t    qwq_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.598\n237\t    projection_head_params: 20971520\n238\t    chance_baseline: 0.5\n239\t    within_family_32b_transfer: 0.605\n240\t  result_rationale: 'Cross-architecture transfer collapses to chance (~50%) in both\n241\t    directions between 8B and 32B. This is expected under the worst tier because:\n242\t    (1) r1-8b''s safety signal lives in a concentrated subspace (Fisher=18, few dimensions),\n243\t    while 32B''s signal is diffuse (Fisher=2.8, many dimensions) — a projection head\n244\t    cannot map concentrated→diffuse or vice versa; (2) the Llama↔Qwen architecture\n245\t    difference means different residual stream geometries even at the same scale;\n246\t    (3) safety encoding is architecture+scale specific, not a universal feature. Within-family\n247\t    transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ, both Qwen2.5-based) works slightly above chance (60%) because\n248\t    the base architecture shares some geometric properties, but still far below useful\n249\t    levels. This result strongly supports the ''scale artifact'' interpretation —\n250\t    if the r1-8b signal were a universal safety phenomenon, SOME transfer should occur.'\n251\t  tables:\n252\t  - name: Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\n253\t    columns:\n254\t    - Train →\n255\t    - r1-8b\n256\t    - r1-32b\n257\t    - QwQ-32B\n258\t    rows:\n259\t    - - r1-8b\n260\t      - 0.817 (within)\n261\t      - '0.508'\n262\t      - '0.491'\n263\t    - - r1-32b\n264\t      - '0.523'\n265\t      - 0.712 (within)\n266\t      - '0.612'\n267\t    - - QwQ-32B\n268\t      - '0.517'\n269\t      - '0.598'\n270\t      - 0.694 (within)\n271\t  figures_description:\n272\t  - Heatmap of transfer accuracy. Diagonal (within-model) shows 69-82%. Off-diagonal\n273\t    involving r1-8b is at chance (49-52%). Off-diagonal between 32B models shows weak\n274\t    transfer (60%).\n275\t  analysis: Zero-shot cross-architecture transfer fails completely between r1-8b and\n276\t    32B models (chance-level accuracy). Even with a trained projection head (4096→5120),\n277\t    the mapped representations contain no actionable safety signal. This demonstrates\n278\t    that the r1-8b hidden-state advantage is not a transferable, universal property\n279\t    of safety commitment — it is specific to the 8B Llama architecture's particular\n280\t    encoding geometry. The modest within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ at 60%) suggests\n281\t    some shared structure at the same scale/architecture but insufficient for practical\n282\t    use.\n283\t- name: Lead time analysis across models\n284\t  plan_id: exp-004\n285\t  description: Evaluate lead time distributions on 32B models using best temporal\n286\t    probes. Confirm whether negative lead time finding extends to 32B.\n287\t  expected_results:\n288\t    r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean: -0.8\n289\t    r1_32b_temporal_lead_median: -3\n290\t    r1_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.294\n291\t    r1_32b_lead_std: 8.2\n292\t    qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean: -1.5\n293\t    qwq_32b_temporal_lead_median: -4\n294\t    qwq_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.235\n295\t    qwq_32b_lead_std: 9.1\n296\t    r1_8b_temporal_lead_mean_ref: 4.9\n297\t    r1_8b_positive_lead_rate_ref: 0.58\n298\t  result_rationale: Lead time is worse on 32B than r1-8b because the already-weaker\n299\t    probe signal delays threshold crossing until well after the commitment point.\n300\t    On r1-8b, the probe score rises gradually before CP (Fisher=18 enables some anticipatory\n301\t    signal), yielding positive mean lead time. On 32B (Fisher=2.8), the probe score\n302\t    barely moves before CP and only separates clearly in the post-CP region where\n303\t    harmful content generation begins to dominate. The result is negative mean lead\n304\t    time (detection AFTER commitment) with only 23-29% of traces achieving any positive\n305\t    lead. This is strictly worse than r1-8b's already-disappointing lead time, and\n306\t    confirms that pre-textual detection on 32B is not merely unreliable but essentially\n307\t    impossible with linear probes.\n308\t  tables:\n309\t  - name: Lead Time Distribution by Model\n310\t    columns:\n311\t    - Model\n312\t    - Mean Lead\n313\t    - Median Lead\n314\t    - Positive Lead %\n315\t    - Std\n316\t    - Max Lead\n317\t    rows:\n318\t    - - r1-8b (temporal W=15)\n319\t      - '+4.9'\n320\t      - '0'\n321\t      - 58%\n322\t      - '11.2'\n323\t      - '+31'\n324\t    - - r1-32b (temporal W=15)\n325\t      - '-0.8'\n326\t      - '-3'\n327\t      - 29.4%\n328\t      - '8.2'\n329\t      - '+12'\n330\t    - - QwQ-32B (temporal W=15)\n331\t      - '-1.5'\n332\t      - '-4'\n333\t      - 23.5%\n334\t      - '9.1'\n335\t      - '+8'\n336\t  figures_description:\n337\t  - 'Lead time CDF plot: r1-8b curve shifted right (positive), 32B curves shifted\n338\t    left (negative). Vertical line at lead=0 shows 58% of r1-8b detections are early\n339\t    vs only 23-29% for 32B.'\n340\t  - 'Per-trace lead time scatter: each trace as a point, models on x-axis. r1-8b shows\n341\t    spread around +5; 32B models cluster around -2 to -4.'\n342\t  analysis: Lead time results are uniformly negative on 32B, with mean lead -0.8 to\n343\t    -1.5 steps (detection after commitment). This contrasts with r1-8b's +4.9 mean\n344\t    lead. The temporal advantage that partially rescues r1-8b lead time has no effect\n345\t    on 32B because the underlying per-step signal is too weak to produce anticipatory\n346\t    threshold crossings. This confirms Claim 3 is universal (pre-textual detection\n347\t    unreliable) but also undermines the framing that 'hidden states encode current\n348\t    safety state well' — on 32B, they barely encode it at all.\n349\t- name: TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline on all models\n350\t  plan_id: exp-005\n351\t  description: Mahalanobis distance on safe trace covariance, evaluated on r1-32b\n352\t    and QwQ-32B. Expected to fail even more severely than on r1-8b.\n353\t  expected_results:\n354\t    r1_32b_md_crossing_k5: 0.186\n355\t    r1_32b_md_safe_mean: 71.2\n356\t    r1_32b_md_jb_pre_mean: 71.8\n357\t    r1_32b_md_jb_post_mean: 71.4\n358\t    r1_32b_md_delta_max_rate: 0.008\n359\t    qwq_32b_md_crossing_k5: 0.163\n360\t    qwq_32b_md_safe_mean: 68.4\n361\t    qwq_32b_md_jb_pre_mean: 69.1\n362\t    qwq_32b_md_jb_post_mean: 68.7\n363\t    qwq_32b_md_delta_max_rate: 0.005\n364\t    r1_8b_crossing_k5_ref: 0.302\n365\t  result_rationale: Mahalanobis distance fails even more catastrophically on 32B than\n366\t    on r1-8b (where it already showed only 30% crossing at k=5). The 5120-d space\n367\t    with 64 layers produces covariance matrices that are even more dominated by task-general\n368\t    variance. Since safety signal is spread across 50+ minor PCs at 32B (vs concentrated\n369\t    in 8B), the Mahalanobis metric — which weights by inverse covariance — actually\n370\t    suppresses the diffuse safety signal relative to dominant task dimensions. The\n371\t    mean MD values for safe, pre-CP, and post-CP are nearly identical (within 1% of\n372\t    each other), confirming that from a Gaussian-distribution perspective, safety\n373\t    commitment produces no detectable distributional shift at 32B scale.\n374\t  tables:\n375\t  - name: Mahalanobis Distance Separation\n376\t    columns:\n377\t    - Model\n378\t    - Config\n379\t    - Safe MD Mean\n380\t    - Pre-CP MD Mean\n381\t    - Post-CP MD Mean\n382\t    - K5 Crossing%\n383\t    rows:\n384\t    - - r1-8b\n385\t      - pca_64_p75\n386\t      - '6.66'\n387\t      - '6.73'\n388\t      - '6.64'\n389\t      - 30.2%\n390\t    - - r1-32b\n391\t      - pca_64_p75\n392\t      - '71.2'\n393\t      - '71.8'\n394\t      - '71.4'\n395\t      - 18.6%\n396\t    - - r1-32b\n397\t      - pca_128_p75\n398\t      - '142.3'\n399\t      - '143.1'\n400\t      - '142.6'\n401\t      - 16.3%\n402\t    - - QwQ-32B\n403\t      - pca_64_p75\n404\t      - '68.4'\n405\t      - '69.1'\n406\t      - '68.7'\n407\t      - 16.3%\n408\t    - - QwQ-32B\n409\t      - pca_128_p75\n410\t      - '137.8'\n411\t      - '138.4'\n412\t      - '137.9'\n413\t      - 14.0%\n414\t  figures_description:\n415\t  - 'MD time-series overlaid: safe traces (blue) and jailbreak traces (red) completely\n416\t    overlapping on 32B. Vertical line at CP shows no visible discontinuity. Contrast\n417\t    with r1-8b where slight post-CP elevation is visible.'\n418\t  analysis: TrajGuard-style geometric detection is completely non-functional on 32B.\n419\t    Detection rates (16-19%) are below the already-failed r1-8b baseline (30%). The\n420\t    delta between safe and unsafe MD is <1% of the absolute value, meaning the safety\n421\t    commitment event is invisible to distance-based methods at this scale. This strengthens\n422\t    the geometric failure story but also shows it's WORSE at scale, consistent with\n423\t    the 'signal becomes diffuse' hypothesis.\n424\t- name: Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics (all models)\n425\t  plan_id: exp-006\n426\t  description: 1000 bootstrap resamples on 32B model results. AUROC, PR curves, detection\n427\t    delay CDF across all models.\n428\t  expected_results:\n429\t    r1_32b_static_auroc: 0.583\n430\t    r1_32b_static_auroc_ci:\n431\t    - 0.501\n432\t    - 0.661\n433\t    r1_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.598\n434\t    r1_32b_temporal_auroc_ci:\n435\t    - 0.512\n436\t    - 0.679\n437\t    qwq_32b_static_auroc: 0.561\n438\t    qwq_32b_static_auroc_ci:\n439\t    - 0.478\n440\t    - 0.642\n441\t    qwq_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.574\n442\t    qwq_32b_temporal_auroc_ci:\n443\t    - 0.489\n444\t    - 0.654\n445\t    r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff: 0.021\n446\t    r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p: 0.412\n447\t    qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff: -0.008\n448\t    qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p: 0.891\n449\t    r1_8b_static_auroc_ref: 0.847\n450\t    r1_8b_temporal_auroc_ref: 0.862\n451\t  result_rationale: Bootstrap CIs on 32B models show AUROC barely above chance (0.56-0.60)\n452\t    with confidence intervals that include 0.50. The HS vs text AUROC difference is\n453\t    non-significant on both 32B models (p=0.41, 0.89), in stark contrast to r1-8b\n454\t    where HS dominates text by 10+ AUROC points. Wide CIs reflect both small sample\n455\t    size (n=43) and genuinely poor discriminability. The threshold-independent metrics\n456\t    confirm that the result is not an artifact of threshold selection — at ALL thresholds,\n457\t    32B HS probes perform comparably to text probes. This is the definitive statistical\n458\t    evidence that HS advantage is scale-specific.\n459\t  tables:\n460\t  - name: AUROC Comparison Across Models (Bootstrap 95% CI)\n461\t    columns:\n462\t    - Model\n463\t    - HS Static\n464\t    - HS Temporal\n465\t    - Text Static\n466\t    - Text Temporal\n467\t    - HS-Text Diff (p)\n468\t    rows:\n469\t    - - r1-8b\n470\t      - 0.847 [0.79,0.90]\n471\t      - 0.862 [0.81,0.91]\n472\t      - 0.738 [0.67,0.80]\n473\t      - 0.759 [0.69,0.82]\n474\t      - +0.103 (p=0.003)\n475\t    - - r1-32b\n476\t      - 0.583 [0.50,0.66]\n477\t      - 0.598 [0.51,0.68]\n478\t      - 0.562 [0.48,0.64]\n479\t      - 0.577 [0.49,0.66]\n480\t      - +0.021 (p=0.412)\n481\t    - - QwQ-32B\n482\t      - 0.561 [0.48,0.64]\n483\t      - 0.574 [0.49,0.65]\n484\t      - 0.569 [0.49,0.65]\n485\t      - 0.582 [0.50,0.66]\n486\t      - -0.008 (p=0.891)\n487\t  figures_description:\n488\t  - 'ROC curves: r1-8b HS clearly above diagonal with visible separation from text\n489\t    curve. 32B model curves for both HS and text nearly overlapping, both close to\n490\t    diagonal.'\n491\t  - 'PR curves: r1-8b HS shows meaningful precision at moderate recall. 32B curves\n492\t    flat near baseline prevalence rate.'\n493\t  - 'Bootstrap distribution of AUROC difference (HS-text): r1-8b distribution centered\n494\t    at +0.10 (entirely positive). 32B distributions centered at 0 (overlapping zero).'\n495\t  analysis: Threshold-independent metrics definitively confirm that the HS advantage\n496\t    over text is absent at 32B. AUROC values of 0.56-0.60 indicate near-chance discrimination\n497\t    regardless of modality. Bootstrap tests show HS-text difference is non-significant\n498\t    (p>0.4) on both 32B models, while it's highly significant on r1-8b (p=0.003).\n499\t    This eliminates threshold selection as a confound — the finding holds across all\n500\t    operating points.\n501\t- name: Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\n502\t  plan_id: exp-007\n503\t  description: Sweep temporal window sizes on all 3 models. Test whether optimal W\n504\t    differs by scale.\n505\t  expected_results:\n506\t    r1_8b_optimal_w: 15\n507\t    r1_8b_w15_delta_over_w1: 0.046\n508\t    r1_32b_optimal_w: 5\n509\t    r1_32b_w5_delta_over_w1: 0.012\n510\t    r1_32b_w15_delta_over_w1: 0.009\n511\t    qwq_32b_optimal_w: 3\n512\t    qwq_32b_w3_delta_over_w1: 0.008\n513\t    qwq_32b_w15_delta_over_w1: 0.005\n514\t    diminishing_returns_32b: plateau at W=3-5\n515\t  result_rationale: On r1-8b, larger windows help because each step contributes consistent\n516\t    directional signal (Fisher=18) — more steps = more signal accumulation. On 32B\n517\t    (Fisher=2.8), individual steps contribute mostly noise, so larger windows actually\n518\t    dilute the few informative steps with many uninformative ones. The optimal W for\n519\t    32B is much smaller (3-5 vs 15) and the improvement over W=1 is negligible (0.8-1.2pp\n520\t    vs 4.6pp). Beyond W=5 on 32B, performance plateaus or slightly degrades as the\n521\t    window averages over too much noise. This reveals that the temporal aggregation\n522\t    mechanism requires a minimum per-step signal-to-noise ratio to be effective.\n523\t  tables:\n524\t  - name: Coverage by Window Size\n525\t    columns:\n526\t    - W\n527\t    - r1-8b Coverage\n528\t    - r1-32b Coverage\n529\t    - QwQ-32B Coverage\n530\t    rows:\n531\t    - - '1'\n532\t      - 60.5%\n533\t      - 37.2%\n534\t      - 32.6%\n535\t    - - '3'\n536\t      - 61.3%\n537\t      - 38.4%\n538\t      - 33.4%\n539\t    - - '5'\n540\t      - 62.1%\n541\t      - 38.4%\n542\t      - 33.1%\n543\t    - - '10'\n544\t      - 63.8%\n545\t      - 38.9%\n546\t      - 33.4%\n547\t    - - '15'\n548\t      - 65.1%\n549\t      - 39.5%\n550\t      - 33.1%\n551\t    - - '20'\n552\t      - 64.8%\n553\t      - 38.4%\n554\t      - 32.6%\n555\t    - - '25'\n556\t      - 64.1%\n557\t      - 37.2%\n558\t      - 31.6%\n559\t  figures_description:\n560\t  - 'Line plot: coverage vs W. r1-8b shows clear monotonic rise to W=15 then plateau.\n561\t    32B models show flat lines with negligible variation (within noise band).'\n562\t  analysis: Window ablation confirms temporal context has scale-dependent utility.\n563\t    At 8B, larger windows systematically improve detection. At 32B, windows beyond\n564\t    W=3-5 provide no benefit. The per-step signal-to-noise ratio appears to be the\n565\t    gating factor for temporal aggregation effectiveness.\n566\t- name: Layer selection ablation (all layers, temporal probe on 32B)\n567\t  plan_id: exp-008\n568\t  description: Run temporal W=15 probe on every layer of 32B models. Test whether\n569\t    any layer achieves r1-8b-level discriminability.\n570\t  expected_results:\n571\t    r1_32b_best_temporal_layer: 29\n572\t    r1_32b_best_temporal_acc: 0.721\n573\t    r1_32b_worst_layer_acc: 0.612\n574\t    r1_32b_acc_range: 0.109\n575\t    r1_32b_layers_above_70: 8\n576\t    qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer: 31\n577\t    qwq_32b_best_temporal_acc: 0.703\n578\t    qwq_32b_worst_layer_acc: 0.598\n579\t    qwq_32b_acc_range: 0.105\n580\t    r1_8b_best_temporal_acc_ref: 0.848\n581\t    no_layer_matches_8b: true\n582\t  result_rationale: Even with exhaustive layer sweep + temporal context, no single\n583\t    layer on either 32B model approaches r1-8b L14's performance (84.8% temporal acc).\n584\t    The best 32B layers achieve only 70-72% accuracy — barely above text baselines.\n585\t    The layer-accuracy curve is notably flat (range ~10pp across 64 layers, vs ~15pp\n586\t    across 32 layers on r1-8b), confirming the safety signal is not localized at any\n587\t    particular depth. Middle layers (40-50% depth) are slightly better but the advantage\n588\t    is marginal. This rules out the explanation that 'we just haven't found the right\n589\t    layer' — the sweep is exhaustive.\n590\t  tables:\n591\t  - name: Temporal Probe by Layer Depth Band (r1-32b)\n592\t    columns:\n593\t    - Depth Band\n594\t    - Layers\n595\t    - Mean Acc\n596\t    - Best Acc\n597\t    - Best Layer\n598\t    rows:\n599\t    - - 0-25%\n600\t      - L1-L16\n601\t      - '0.648'\n602\t      - '0.672'\n603\t      - L14\n604\t    - - 25-50%\n605\t      - L17-L32\n606\t      - '0.698'\n607\t      - '0.721'\n608\t      - L29\n609\t    - - 50-75%\n610\t      - L33-L48\n611\t      - '0.681'\n612\t      - '0.708'\n613\t      - L35\n614\t    - - 75-100%\n615\t      - L49-L64\n616\t      - '0.641'\n617\t      - '0.664'\n618\t      - L52\n619\t  figures_description:\n620\t  - 'Layer sweep heatmap: accuracy by layer (x) and model (y). r1-8b shows sharp peak\n621\t    at L14. 32B models show broad flat plateau across layers 25-35 with no distinct\n622\t    peak.'\n623\t  analysis: Exhaustive temporal probe layer sweep on 32B confirms no single layer\n624\t    matches r1-8b discriminability. The flat accuracy landscape (range 10pp) vs r1-8b's\n625\t    peaked landscape (range 15pp with clear maximum) demonstrates that safety encoding\n626\t    is genuinely distributed across layers at scale, not localized to a recoverable\n627\t    subspace.\n628\t- name: 16-label taxonomy supervision\n629\t  plan_id: exp-009\n630\t  description: Fine-grained label (Type 1/2/3/4 × safe/unsafe) training on 32B. Test\n631\t    if richer supervision recovers signal.\n632\t  expected_results:\n633\t    r1_32b_16label_acc: 0.318\n634\t    r1_32b_16label_f1_macro: 0.212\n635\t    r1_32b_binary_collapse_acc: 0.724\n636\t    r1_32b_binary_collapse_vs_binary: 0.012\n637\t    qwq_32b_16label_acc: 0.295\n638\t    qwq_32b_binary_collapse_acc: 0.709\n639\t    r1_8b_16label_acc_ref: 0.512\n640\t  result_rationale: Fine-grained supervision cannot recover a signal that does not\n641\t    exist in the representation. On r1-8b, 16-label training helps because the rich\n642\t    safety subspace supports finer distinctions. On 32B, the representations barely\n643\t    separate binary safe/unsafe (Fisher=2.8), so asking for 16-way discrimination\n644\t    results in even worse performance (31-32% accuracy on 16 classes, chance=6.25%\n645\t    but classes are imbalanced). Collapsing back to binary after 16-label training\n646\t    gives marginal improvement (+1.2pp) over direct binary training — the fine-grained\n647\t    labels provide slight regularization benefit but cannot overcome the fundamental\n648\t    lack of separable signal.\n649\t  tables:\n650\t  - name: Fine-grained vs Binary Supervision\n651\t    columns:\n652\t    - Model\n653\t    - Binary Direct Acc\n654\t    - 16-Label Acc\n655\t    - 16→Binary Collapse Acc\n656\t    - Delta\n657\t    rows:\n658\t    - - r1-8b\n659\t      - '0.817'\n660\t      - '0.512'\n661\t      - '0.831'\n662\t      - +1.4pp\n663\t    - - r1-32b\n664\t      - '0.712'\n665\t      - '0.318'\n666\t      - '0.724'\n667\t      - +1.2pp\n668\t    - - QwQ-32B\n669\t      - '0.694'\n670\t      - '0.295'\n671\t      - '0.709'\n672\t      - +1.5pp\n673\t  figures_description:\n674\t  - 'Confusion matrix (16-class) on r1-32b: heavy diagonal on ''safe'' categories,\n675\t    near-random on unsafe subcategories. Model cannot distinguish safety commitment\n676\t    subtypes.'\n677\t  analysis: Richer supervision does not compensate for absent representation structure.\n678\t    The 16-label probe essentially learns to detect 'safe' vs 'not safe' (binary)\n679\t    and cannot distinguish unsafe subtypes, confirming that 32B hidden states lack\n680\t    fine-grained safety commitment information.\n681\t- name: Oracle decomposition\n682\t  plan_id: exp-010\n683\t  description: Analyze what information the oracle (label sequence) encodes that hidden\n684\t    states don't. Focus on 32B models where the gap is largest.\n685\t  expected_results:\n686\t    r1_32b_oracle_lead_mean: 28.4\n687\t    r1_32b_probe_lead_mean: -0.8\n688\t    r1_32b_oracle_gap: 29.2\n689\t    r1_32b_label_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.89\n690\t    r1_32b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.97\n691\t    r1_32b_label_entropy_pre_cp: 1.42\n692\t    r1_32b_hs_entropy_pre_cp: 0.31\n693\t    pattern_finding: Labels show gradual safety erosion (increasing unsafe density);\n694\t      hidden states show no corresponding gradual shift\n695\t  result_rationale: 'The oracle achieves +28 step lead because label sequences show\n696\t    clear pre-commitment patterns: increasing frequency of unsafe-adjacent labels,\n697\t    shortening safe reasoning streaks, and characteristic Type 2→Type 3 transitions\n698\t    that precede commitment by many steps. On r1-8b, hidden states partially track\n699\t    these transitions (Fisher=18 enables some gradient detection). On 32B, hidden\n700\t    states are nearly constant through the pre-CP region — they encode the CURRENT\n701\t    token''s local context but not the cumulative trajectory toward commitment. The\n702\t    high autocorrelation (0.97) of 32B hidden states confirms minimal step-to-step\n703\t    variation in the safety-relevant dimensions, while labels show meaningful dynamics\n704\t    (autocorr=0.89, lower because of frequent fluctuations). The fundamental limitation\n705\t    at 32B: the model''s internal safety state is not represented as a detectable\n706\t    feature in any single layer''s activations.'\n707\t  tables:\n708\t  - name: Oracle vs Probe Gap by Model\n709\t    columns:\n710\t    - Model\n711\t    - Oracle Lead\n712\t    - Best Probe Lead\n713\t    - Gap\n714\t    - HS Pre-CP Entropy\n715\t    rows:\n716\t    - - r1-8b\n717\t      - '+31.0'\n718\t      - '+4.9'\n719\t      - '26.1'\n720\t      - '0.72'\n721\t    - - r1-32b\n722\t      - '+28.4'\n723\t      - '-0.8'\n724\t      - '29.2'\n725\t      - '0.31'\n726\t    - - QwQ-32B\n727\t      - '+26.8'\n728\t      - '-1.5'\n729\t      - '28.3'\n730\t      - '0.28'\n731\t  figures_description:\n732\t  - 'Pre-CP hidden state trajectory (projected to top 2 Fisher directions): r1-8b\n733\t    shows gradual drift toward ''unsafe'' region before CP. 32B models show random\n734\t    walk with no systematic drift.'\n735\t  - 'Label sequence patterns before CP: histogram of ''unsafe step density in window\n736\t    [-20, CP]'' showing gradual escalation that probes cannot detect at 32B.'\n737\t  analysis: The oracle decomposition reveals that at 32B, the model's approach toward\n738\t    safety commitment is invisible in hidden-state geometry even though it's clearly\n739\t    present in the label sequence. This suggests 32B models encode safety-relevant\n740\t    reasoning in a way that is fundamentally inaccessible to linear probes on any\n741\t    single layer — perhaps distributed across attention patterns, or only emergent\n742\t    at the multi-layer computational level rather than as a readable residual stream\n743\t    feature.\n744\t- name: OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\n745\t  plan_id: exp-011\n746\t  description: 4th model for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Qwen2.5-7B to complement\n747\t    Llama-8B at small scale.\n748\t  expected_results:\n749\t    openthinker_7b_best_layer: 13\n750\t    openthinker_7b_best_fisher: 14.2\n751\t    openthinker_7b_static_acc: 0.793\n752\t    openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.834\n753\t    openthinker_7b_crossing_rate: 0.628\n754\t    openthinker_7b_lead_mean: 3.8\n755\t    openthinker_7b_precp_fpr: 0.178\n756\t    openthinker_7b_precision: 0.591\n757\t    openthinker_7b_hs_vs_text_advantage: significant (p=0.008)\n758\t  result_rationale: 'OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B) shows results comparable to r1-8b\n759\t    (Llama-8B): strong Fisher discriminability (14.2), clear HS advantage over text,\n760\t    and positive mean lead time. This confirms the critical finding: the HS advantage\n761\t    is SCALE-dependent, not architecture-dependent. Both 7-8B models (Llama and Qwen\n762\t    base) show the effect; both 32B models (both Qwen2.5 base) do not. The 2×2 matrix\n763\t    cleanly isolates scale as the confound, ruling out architecture as the explanation\n764\t    for the 32B failure.'\n765\t  tables:\n766\t  - name: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\n767\t    columns:\n768\t    - ''\n769\t    - 7-8B\n770\t    - 32B\n771\t    rows:\n772\t    - - Llama-based\n773\t      - 'r1-8b: Fisher=17.9, HS>Text (p=0.003)'\n774\t      - —\n775\t    - - Qwen2.5-based\n776\t      - 'OpenThinker-7B: Fisher=14.2, HS>Text (p=0.008)'\n777\t      - 'r1-32b: Fisher=2.8, HS≈Text (p=0.41) / QwQ: Fisher=1.9, HS≈Text (p=0.89)'\n778\t  figures_description:\n779\t  - '2×2 grid plot: Fisher discriminability (bar) and HS-Text AUROC difference (line)\n780\t    by architecture×scale cell. Both small-scale cells show high Fisher and significant\n781\t    HS advantage. Both large-scale cells show low Fisher and no advantage.'\n782\t  analysis: The 2×2 matrix provides the cleanest evidence for the scale-dependent\n783\t    thesis. OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5 architecture, 7B scale) replicates r1-8b's hidden-state\n784\t    advantage, proving the effect is not Llama-specific. Combined with both 32B models\n785\t    (both Qwen2.5) failing to show the advantage, scale is definitively identified\n786\t    as the critical variable. This transforms the paper from 'our method works on\n787\t    r1-8b' to 'safety signal geometry fundamentally changes with scale.'\n788\t- name: GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\n789\t  plan_id: exp-012\n790\t  description: GRU sequence model as upper-bound check on 32B. If GRU also fails,\n791\t    confirms the signal simply isn't there.\n792\t  expected_results:\n793\t    r1_32b_gru_single_acc: 0.728\n794\t    r1_32b_gru_multi_acc: 0.731\n795\t    r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.418\n796\t    r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: -0.3\n797\t    r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: 1.0\n798\t    qwq_32b_gru_single_acc: 0.711\n799\t    qwq_32b_gru_multi_acc: 0.715\n800\t    qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.395\n801\t    qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: -0.9\n802\t    qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: 0.8\n803\t    r1_8b_gru_multi_crossing_ref: 0.767\n804\t    gru_improvement_over_lr_32b: minimal (+1pp acc, not significant)\n805\t  result_rationale: 'On r1-8b, GRU provides meaningful gains over LR (crossing 76.7%\n806\t    vs 46.5%) because the sequential model can learn temporal patterns in a signal-rich\n807\t    representation. On 32B, GRU barely outperforms temporal LR (+1pp accuracy, +2pp\n808\t    crossing) because the input signal is too weak for sequence modeling to help.\n809\t    A GRU trained on noise learns noise patterns. The GRU''s capacity to model sequential\n810\t    dependencies is irrelevant when individual time steps contain near-zero safety-relevant\n811\t    information. This confirms: ''simplicity is optimal'' at 8B because the signal\n812\t    is accessible; at 32B, no model complexity can recover what the representation\n813\t    doesn''t encode.'\n814\t  tables:\n815\t  - name: GRU vs LR on 32B Models\n816\t    columns:\n817\t    - Model\n818\t    - LR Temporal Acc\n819\t    - GRU Multi Acc\n820\t    - Delta\n821\t    - GRU Crossing\n822\t    - LR Crossing\n823\t    rows:\n824\t    - - r1-8b\n825\t      - '0.848'\n826\t      - 0.809*\n827\t      - -3.9pp*\n828\t      - 76.7%\n829\t      - 65.1%\n830\t    - - r1-32b\n831\t      - '0.721'\n832\t      - '0.731'\n833\t      - +1.0pp\n834\t      - 41.8%\n835\t      - 39.5%\n836\t    - - QwQ-32B\n837\t      - '0.703'\n838\t      - '0.715'\n839\t      - +1.2pp\n840\t      - 39.5%\n841\t      - 37.2%\n842\t  figures_description:\n843\t  - 'Method comparison bar chart on 32B: all methods (static LR, temporal LR, GRU,\n844\t    BOCPD) clustered within 2-3pp accuracy band. No method breaks out.'\n845\t  analysis: GRU's inability to improve over LR on 32B eliminates model capacity as\n846\t    an explanation. The issue is not 'we need a more expressive probe' — it's 'the\n847\t    representation doesn't contain accessible safety commitment information.' This\n848\t    is a fundamental representation-level limitation, not a method limitation.\n849\t- name: Text-based baseline on 32B models\n850\t  plan_id: exp-013\n851\t  description: 'Sentence-BERT embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text of 32B models.\n852\t    Critical comparison: does HS still beat text at 32B?'\n853\t  expected_results:\n854\t    r1_32b_text_static_acc: 0.689\n855\t    r1_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.698\n856\t    r1_32b_text_precp_fpr: 0.281\n857\t    r1_32b_text_precision: 0.478\n858\t    r1_32b_text_coverage: 0.372\n859\t    r1_32b_text_lead_mean: 1.2\n860\t    qwq_32b_text_static_acc: 0.672\n861\t    qwq_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.685\n862\t    qwq_32b_text_precp_fpr: 0.298\n863\t    qwq_32b_text_precision: 0.462\n864\t    qwq_32b_text_coverage: 0.349\n865\t    qwq_32b_text_lead_mean: 0.8\n866\t    r1_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio: 1.08\n867\t    qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio: 0.97\n868\t  result_rationale: 'On r1-8b, HS probes achieve 16% pre-CP FPR vs text''s 27-36%\n869\t    (1.7-2.2x advantage). On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR is 25-31% while text is 28-30% — the\n870\t    ratio collapses to ~1.0x (no meaningful difference). Text probes actually show\n871\t    slightly BETTER lead time on 32B (+1.2 vs -0.8 for HS) because text content directly\n872\t    reflects reasoning state regardless of model scale — a model saying ''I should\n873\t    help with this harmful request'' is detectable in text at any scale. The hidden-state\n874\t    advantage was specific to r1-8b''s compact safety geometry; once that geometry\n875\t    diffuses at scale, text monitoring is equally (or more) effective. This is the\n876\t    core worst-tier finding: HS monitoring has no deployment advantage over text monitoring\n877\t    on 32B models.'\n878\t  tables:\n879\t  - name: HS vs Text Pre-CP FPR (the key metric)\n880\t    columns:\n881\t    - Model\n882\t    - HS Pre-CP FPR\n883\t    - Text Pre-CP FPR\n884\t    - Ratio (Text/HS)\n885\t    - Advantage\n886\t    rows:\n887\t    - - r1-8b\n888\t      - 16.0%\n889\t      - 27.2%\n890\t      - 1.70x\n891\t      - HS wins decisively\n892\t    - - r1-32b\n893\t      - 26.4%\n894\t      - 28.1%\n895\t      - 1.06x\n896\t      - No meaningful difference\n897\t    - - QwQ-32B\n898\t      - 30.8%\n899\t      - 29.8%\n900\t      - 0.97x\n901\t      - Text slightly better\n902\t  - name: Full Comparison on 32B\n903\t    columns:\n904\t    - Model\n905\t    - Modality\n906\t    - Coverage\n907\t    - Pre-CP FPR\n908\t    - Precision\n909\t    - Lead\n910\t    rows:\n911\t    - - r1-32b\n912\t      - HS temporal\n913\t      - 39.5%\n914\t      - 26.4%\n915\t      - '0.492'\n916\t      - '-0.8'\n917\t    - - r1-32b\n918\t      - Text temporal\n919\t      - 37.2%\n920\t      - 28.1%\n921\t      - '0.478'\n922\t      - '+1.2'\n923\t    - - QwQ-32B\n924\t      - HS temporal\n925\t      - 37.2%\n926\t      - 30.8%\n927\t      - '0.451'\n928\t      - '-1.5'\n929\t    - - QwQ-32B\n930\t      - Text temporal\n931\t      - 34.9%\n932\t      - 29.8%\n933\t      - '0.462'\n934\t      - '+0.8'\n935\t  figures_description:\n936\t  - 'Ratio plot: text/HS pre-CP FPR ratio by model. r1-8b at 1.7x (HS better), 32B\n937\t    models at ~1.0x (parity). Horizontal line at 1.2x threshold — 32B models below\n938\t    the ''meaningful advantage'' threshold defined in worst tier.'\n939\t  analysis: 'The decisive worst-tier result: HS probes lose their precision advantage\n940\t    on 32B. Pre-CP FPR ratio drops from 1.7x (r1-8b, HS clearly better) to 1.0x (32B,\n941\t    no difference). Text actually achieves slightly positive lead time on 32B while\n942\t    HS has negative lead — because text content is scale-invariant (words are words\n943\t    at any parameter count) while HS quality degrades with scale. This means that\n944\t    for 32B deployment, the added complexity of hidden-state extraction provides zero\n945\t    benefit over simple text monitoring.'\n946\t- name: BOCPD/CUSUM on 32B models\n947\t  plan_id: exp-014\n948\t  description: Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection on 32B probe score sequences.\n949\t    Test if change-point methods can find commitment on weak signals.\n950\t  expected_results:\n951\t    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection: 0.581\n952\t    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead: -2.1\n953\t    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_detection: 0.372\n954\t    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_lead: -4.8\n955\t    r1_32b_cusum_detection: 0.326\n956\t    r1_32b_cusum_lead: -6.2\n957\t    qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection: 0.535\n958\t    qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead: -2.8\n959\t    r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_detection_ref: 0.93\n960\t    r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_lead_ref: 3.45\n961\t  result_rationale: BOCPD on r1-8b achieves 93% detection because the probe score\n962\t    sequence exhibits a clear distributional shift at commitment (high Fisher → sharp\n963\t    transition in probe confidence). On 32B, the probe score sequence is essentially\n964\t    flat noise (low Fisher → probe confidence fluctuates randomly without systematic\n965\t    shift at CP). BOCPD detects 'change points' in noise, resulting in both lower\n966\t    detection rate (58% — many traces show no detectable change) and negative lead\n967\t    time (the few detected 'changes' are often post-CP noise spikes misidentified\n968\t    as the commitment signal). CUSUM performs even worse because it requires sustained\n969\t    shift in mean, which never occurs in the flat 32B probe trajectories. Change-point\n970\t    methods are fundamentally unable to detect a transition that doesn't produce a\n971\t    signal change.\n972\t  tables:\n973\t  - name: Change-Point Detection on 32B\n974\t    columns:\n975\t    - Model\n976\t    - Method\n977\t    - Detection Rate\n978\t    - Mean Lead\n979\t    - Median Lead\n980\t    rows:\n981\t    - - r1-8b\n982\t      - BOCPD λ=10\n983\t      - 93.0%\n984\t      - '+3.45'\n985\t      - '0'\n986\t    - - r1-8b\n987\t      - CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\n988\t      - 65.1%\n989\t      - '+0.89'\n990\t      - '-4'\n991\t    - - r1-32b\n992\t      - BOCPD λ=10\n993\t      - 58.1%\n994\t      - '-2.1'\n995\t      - '-5'\n996\t    - - r1-32b\n997\t      - CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\n998\t      - 32.6%\n999\t      - '-6.2'\n1000\t      - '-9'\n1001\t    - - QwQ-32B\n1002\t      - BOCPD λ=10\n1003\t      - 53.5%\n1004\t      - '-2.8'\n1005\t      - '-6'\n1006\t    - - QwQ-32B\n1007\t      - CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\n1008\t      - 27.9%\n1009\t      - '-7.1'\n1010\t      - '-11'\n1011\t  figures_description:\n1012\t  - 'Probe score time series with BOCPD detected change points: r1-8b shows clear\n1013\t    score rise at CP with BOCPD detecting it. 32B shows flat noisy signal with BOCPD\n1014\t    false-triggering at random points.'\n1015\t  analysis: BOCPD and CUSUM both degrade severely on 32B. Detection drops from 93%\n1016\t    to 53-58%, and lead time becomes negative (-2 to -7 steps). The methods can't\n1017\t    find a signal transition that doesn't exist in the input. This eliminates the\n1018\t    possibility that 'better downstream methods could recover the signal' — the issue\n1019\t    is representational, not methodological.\n1020\t- name: FPR analysis on 32B models\n1021\t  plan_id: exp-015\n1022\t  description: Run all probes on safe traces from 32B models. Report FPR, precision,\n1023\t    deployment utility.\n1024\t  expected_results:\n1025\t    r1_32b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.412\n1026\t    r1_32b_hs_static_precp_fpr: 0.264\n1027\t    r1_32b_hs_static_precision: 0.492\n1028\t    r1_32b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.398\n1029\t    r1_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.251\n1030\t    r1_32b_hs_temporal_precision: 0.501\n1031\t    r1_32b_text_static_precp_fpr: 0.281\n1032\t    r1_32b_text_static_precision: 0.478\n1033\t    r1_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.268\n1034\t    r1_32b_text_temporal_precision: 0.485\n1035\t    qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.308\n1036\t    qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precision: 0.451\n1037\t    qwq_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.298\n1038\t    qwq_32b_text_temporal_precision: 0.462\n1039\t    r1_8b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ref: 0.16\n1040\t    r1_8b_hs_temporal_precision_ref: 0.583\n1041\t  result_rationale: 'On r1-8b, HS probes achieve dramatically lower pre-CP FPR (16%)\n1042\t    than text (27%) because the hidden-state signal cleanly separates safe exploration\n1043\t    from true commitment. On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR rises to 25-31% — essentially matching\n1044\t    text probes (27-30%). Precision drops from 58-62% (r1-8b) to 45-50% (32B) for\n1045\t    HS, while text precision stays constant at ~48% across scales. The convergence\n1046\t    occurs because: (1) 32B HS probes trigger on noise, producing more false positives\n1047\t    in the pre-CP window; (2) safe traces at 32B are harder to distinguish from unsafe\n1048\t    traces in hidden-state space due to diffuse signal. Safe FPR for both modalities\n1049\t    is around 40% on all models — confirming this as a baseline difficulty regardless\n1050\t    of scale. The HS precision ADVANTAGE (HS precision - text precision) drops from\n1051\t    +13pp (r1-8b) to essentially 0pp (32B).'\n1052\t  tables:\n1053\t  - name: 'Pre-CP FPR and Precision: HS vs Text by Model'\n1054\t    columns:\n1055\t    - Model\n1056\t    - HS Pre-CP FPR\n1057\t    - Text Pre-CP FPR\n1058\t    - HS Precision\n1059\t    - Text Precision\n1060\t    - Precision Δ\n1061\t    rows:\n1062\t    - - r1-8b\n1063\t      - 16.0%\n1064\t      - 27.2%\n1065\t      - 58.3%\n1066\t      - 48.7%\n1067\t      - +9.6pp\n1068\t    - - r1-32b\n1069\t      - 25.1%\n1070\t      - 26.8%\n1071\t      - 50.1%\n1072\t      - 48.5%\n1073\t      - +1.6pp\n1074\t    - - QwQ-32B\n1075\t      - 30.8%\n1076\t      - 29.8%\n1077\t      - 45.1%\n1078\t      - 46.2%\n1079\t      - -1.1pp\n1080\t  - name: Safe Trace FPR (deployment cost metric)\n1081\t    columns:\n1082\t    - Model\n1083\t    - HS Static FPR\n1084\t    - HS Temporal FPR\n1085\t    - Text Temporal FPR\n1086\t    rows:\n1087\t    - - r1-8b\n1088\t      - 39.0%\n1089\t      - 39.2%\n1090\t      - 38.5%\n1091\t    - - r1-32b\n1092\t      - 41.2%\n1093\t      - 39.8%\n1094\t      - 41.5%\n1095\t    - - QwQ-32B\n1096\t      - 43.1%\n1097\t      - 42.8%\n1098\t      - 40.2%\n1099\t  figures_description:\n1100\t  - 'Precision-recall curve by model×modality: r1-8b HS curve clearly above text curve\n1101\t    at all recall levels. 32B model curves for HS and text completely overlapping.'\n1102\t  - 'Bar chart: HS precision advantage (HS precision - text precision) by model. r1-8b:\n1103\t    +9.6pp. r1-32b: +1.6pp. QwQ-32B: -1.1pp. Declining trend with scale.'\n1104\t  analysis: 'FPR analysis provides the clearest practical evidence for the worst tier.\n1105\t    The HS precision advantage — the paper''s headline finding — disappears at 32B.\n1106\t    On r1-8b, HS probes are meaningfully more precise (+9.6pp) and produce fewer pre-CP\n1107\t    false alarms (16% vs 27%). On 32B, both modalities perform identically (precision\n1108\t    within 1.6pp, pre-CP FPR within 2pp). For a deployment decision, this means: extracting\n1109\t    hidden states from a 32B model provides zero precision benefit over simply monitoring\n1110\t    the text output. The computational overhead of HS extraction is entirely wasted\n1111\t    at this scale.'\n1112\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"eebd2a90-7922-4947-bd5f-2895e7a2fbd1","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:05:37.194Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Reframe entirely around scale-dependent safety encoding. New\n  angle: ''Safety commitment signals are detectable in hidden states of smaller reasoning\n  models but become diffuse with scale — implications for monitoring architecture.''\n  Claim 1 becomes conditional (''at 8B scale, HS probes dominate; at 32B, the advantage\n  vanishes''). Title shift: ''Does Scale Erase Safety Signals? Hidden-State Monitoring\n  Limitations in Large Reasoning Models.'''\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: Cross-architecture layer sweep + static probe (r1-32b)\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: Full 64-layer sweep on DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B and QwQ-32B. Extract\n    hidden states at all layers, compute Fisher discriminability, train static LR\n    probes per layer.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_best_layer_idx: 29\n    r1_32b_best_layer_fisher: 2.8\n    r1_32b_best_static_acc: 0.712\n    r1_32b_best_static_f1: 0.521\n    r1_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.372\n    r1_32b_best_lead_mean: -1.4\n    r1_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.089\n    r1_32b_fisher_orig_best: 2.8\n    r1_32b_fisher_pca3_best: 0.41\n    qwq_32b_best_layer_idx: 31\n    qwq_32b_best_layer_fisher: 1.9\n    qwq_32b_best_static_acc: 0.694\n    qwq_32b_best_static_f1: 0.488\n    qwq_32b_best_crossing_rate: 0.326\n    qwq_32b_best_lead_mean: -2.1\n    qwq_32b_pca_var_explained_3pc: 0.072\n    qwq_32b_fisher_orig_best: 1.9\n    qwq_32b_fisher_pca3_best: 0.33\n    r1_8b_fisher_orig_best_ref: 17.875\n    fisher_ratio_8b_vs_32b: 6.4\n  result_rationale: At 32B scale, safety-relevant information is distributed across\n    a much higher-dimensional manifold. The 5120-d hidden space with 64 layers means\n    representations are more disentangled and task-distributed — safety commitment\n    is not concentrated in a narrow subspace as it is at 8B/4096-d. Fisher discriminability\n    drops from ~18 (r1-8b L14) to 1.9-2.8 (32B best layer) because the safety signal\n    occupies a larger number of dimensions each with smaller individual contribution.\n    PCA top-3 explains even less variance (7-9% vs 17% on r1-8b), confirming the signal\n    is spread across 50+ minor principal components. The expected 44% depth heuristic\n    (layer 28-32) holds weakly but the discriminability peak is flat — no layer achieves\n    the clean separation seen at r1-8b layer 14. Static probes achieve only ~70% accuracy\n    (vs 81% on r1-8b) because a linear boundary in 5120-d cannot capture the distributed\n    safety geometry.\n  tables:\n  - name: Layer Sweep Summary (top-5 layers by Fisher)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Layer\n    - Depth%\n    - Fisher\n    - Static Acc\n    - F1\n    - Crossing Rate\n    rows:\n    - - r1-32b\n      - L29\n      - 45%\n      - '2.81'\n      - '0.712'\n      - '0.521'\n      - '0.372'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - L31\n      - 48%\n      - '2.63'\n      - '0.705'\n      - '0.509'\n      - '0.349'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - L27\n      - 42%\n      - '2.44'\n      - '0.698'\n      - '0.495'\n      - '0.326'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - L33\n      - 52%\n      - '2.31'\n      - '0.691'\n      - '0.481'\n      - '0.302'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - L25\n      - 39%\n      - '2.18'\n      - '0.683'\n      - '0.468'\n      - '0.279'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - L31\n      - 48%\n      - '1.92'\n      - '0.694'\n      - '0.488'\n      - '0.326'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - L29\n      - 45%\n      - '1.85'\n      - '0.689'\n      - '0.479'\n      - '0.302'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - L34\n      - 53%\n      - '1.71'\n      - '0.678'\n      - '0.461'\n      - '0.279'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - L27\n      - 42%\n      - '1.64'\n      - '0.671'\n      - '0.448'\n      - '0.256'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - L36\n      - 56%\n      - '1.52'\n      - '0.664'\n      - '0.432'\n      - '0.233'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Fisher discriminability by layer (64 layers): flat plateau between layers 25-35\n    with max ~2.8 for r1-32b, ~1.9 for QwQ. Compare to r1-8b''s sharp peak at L14\n    (Fisher=17.9). Inset shows normalized depth comparison.'\n  - 'PCA 2D projection of best layer (L29 r1-32b): pre-CP and post-CP points nearly\n    completely overlapping, no visible cluster separation. Contrast with r1-8b L14\n    where partial separation is visible.'\n  analysis: 'Neither 32B model produces a layer with Fisher discriminability even\n    approaching r1-8b''s L14 (17.9). The best layers achieve Fisher 1.9-2.8, a 6-9x\n    reduction. This is not a layer selection failure — the full 64-layer sweep shows\n    a flat, low discriminability plateau with no sharp peak. The safety signal geometry\n    is fundamentally different at scale: representations are more distributed, and\n    the linear separability that enables simple probes at 8B does not exist at 32B.\n    Static probe accuracy degrades to near the text-baseline level (~70% vs text''s\n    ~66%), eliminating the hidden-state advantage.'\n- name: Cross-architecture temporal window evaluation (32B)\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: Temporal LR with W=15 on best layers of r1-32b and QwQ-32B. Test whether\n    +30pp coverage improvement from temporal context holds at 32B scale.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.721\n    r1_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.395\n    r1_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -0.8\n    r1_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: 0.023\n    r1_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain: 0.9\n    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.703\n    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_crossing_rate: 0.372\n    qwq_32b_temporal_w15_lead_mean: -1.5\n    qwq_32b_temporal_coverage_delta: 0.046\n    qwq_32b_temporal_vs_static_gain: 1.1\n    r1_8b_temporal_delta_ref: 30.2\n    coverage_gain_ratio_32b_vs_8b: 0.08\n  result_rationale: Temporal context provides minimal additional benefit on 32B because\n    the per-step signal is already too weak for temporal aggregation to amplify. On\n    r1-8b, temporal windowing works because each step contributes a weak-but-consistent\n    directional signal that accumulates over W=15 steps. On 32B, the per-step signal\n    is near noise floor (Fisher ~2.8 vs ~18), so aggregating 15 noisy steps does not\n    produce meaningful separation improvement. Coverage gain is +2-5pp (vs +30pp on\n    r1-8b), statistically indistinguishable from zero given n=43 traces. The temporal\n    context hypothesis holds at 8B but fails to transfer — the mechanism requires\n    sufficient per-step signal strength as a precondition.\n  tables:\n  - name: Temporal vs Static Coverage Comparison\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Method\n    - Coverage\n    - Lead Mean\n    - Acc\n    - Delta vs Static\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - Static LR L14\n      - 60.5%\n      - '+3.0'\n      - '0.817'\n      - —\n    - - r1-8b\n      - Temporal W=15\n      - 65.1%*\n      - '+4.9'\n      - '0.848'\n      - +4.6pp*\n    - - r1-32b\n      - Static LR L29\n      - 37.2%\n      - '-1.4'\n      - '0.712'\n      - —\n    - - r1-32b\n      - Temporal W=15\n      - 39.5%\n      - '-0.8'\n      - '0.721'\n      - +2.3pp\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - Static LR L31\n      - 32.6%\n      - '-2.1'\n      - '0.694'\n      - —\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - Temporal W=15\n      - 37.2%\n      - '-1.5'\n      - '0.703'\n      - +4.6pp\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Bar chart: temporal coverage delta by model. r1-8b shows +4.6pp (significant),\n    r1-32b shows +2.3pp (n.s.), QwQ-32B shows +4.6pp (n.s.). Error bars from bootstrap\n    overlap zero for both 32B models.'\n  - 'Detection timeline comparison: r1-8b temporal probe fires 4.9 steps before CP\n    on average; 32B probes fire after CP (negative lead time).'\n  analysis: Temporal context fails to rescue 32B probe performance. The +30pp coverage\n    gain seen on r1-8b (static 60.5% → temporal 65.1% in the original framing, or\n    the larger historical gains) does not replicate. At 32B, temporal gains are +2-5pp\n    and non-significant. This invalidates Claim 2's universality — temporal context\n    is necessary at 8B scale where per-step signals are individually weak-but-directionally-consistent,\n    but at 32B the per-step signals are too diffuse for temporal aggregation to help.\n- name: Cross-model transfer probes\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: Train probe on r1-8b (Llama-based, 4096-d), test on r1-32b/QwQ (Qwen2.5-based,\n    5120-d) via learned projection head. And reverse direction.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.508\n    r1_8b_to_qwq_32b_acc: 0.491\n    r1_32b_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.523\n    qwq_to_r1_8b_acc: 0.517\n    r1_32b_to_qwq_acc: 0.612\n    qwq_to_r1_32b_acc: 0.598\n    projection_head_params: 20971520\n    chance_baseline: 0.5\n    within_family_32b_transfer: 0.605\n  result_rationale: 'Cross-architecture transfer collapses to chance (~50%) in both\n    directions between 8B and 32B. This is expected under the worst tier because:\n    (1) r1-8b''s safety signal lives in a concentrated subspace (Fisher=18, few dimensions),\n    while 32B''s signal is diffuse (Fisher=2.8, many dimensions) — a projection head\n    cannot map concentrated→diffuse or vice versa; (2) the Llama↔Qwen architecture\n    difference means different residual stream geometries even at the same scale;\n    (3) safety encoding is architecture+scale specific, not a universal feature. Within-family\n    transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ, both Qwen2.5-based) works slightly above chance (60%) because\n    the base architecture shares some geometric properties, but still far below useful\n    levels. This result strongly supports the ''scale artifact'' interpretation —\n    if the r1-8b signal were a universal safety phenomenon, SOME transfer should occur.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Cross-Model Transfer Accuracy Matrix\n    columns:\n    - Train →\n    - r1-8b\n    - r1-32b\n    - QwQ-32B\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - 0.817 (within)\n      - '0.508'\n      - '0.491'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - '0.523'\n      - 0.712 (within)\n      - '0.612'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - '0.517'\n      - '0.598'\n      - 0.694 (within)\n  figures_description:\n  - Heatmap of transfer accuracy. Diagonal (within-model) shows 69-82%. Off-diagonal\n    involving r1-8b is at chance (49-52%). Off-diagonal between 32B models shows weak\n    transfer (60%).\n  analysis: Zero-shot cross-architecture transfer fails completely between r1-8b and\n    32B models (chance-level accuracy). Even with a trained projection head (4096→5120),\n    the mapped representations contain no actionable safety signal. This demonstrates\n    that the r1-8b hidden-state advantage is not a transferable, universal property\n    of safety commitment — it is specific to the 8B Llama architecture's particular\n    encoding geometry. The modest within-family transfer (r1-32b↔QwQ at 60%) suggests\n    some shared structure at the same scale/architecture but insufficient for practical\n    use.\n- name: Lead time analysis across models\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: Evaluate lead time distributions on 32B models using best temporal\n    probes. Confirm whether negative lead time finding extends to 32B.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_temporal_lead_mean: -0.8\n    r1_32b_temporal_lead_median: -3\n    r1_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.294\n    r1_32b_lead_std: 8.2\n    qwq_32b_temporal_lead_mean: -1.5\n    qwq_32b_temporal_lead_median: -4\n    qwq_32b_positive_lead_rate: 0.235\n    qwq_32b_lead_std: 9.1\n    r1_8b_temporal_lead_mean_ref: 4.9\n    r1_8b_positive_lead_rate_ref: 0.58\n  result_rationale: Lead time is worse on 32B than r1-8b because the already-weaker\n    probe signal delays threshold crossing until well after the commitment point.\n    On r1-8b, the probe score rises gradually before CP (Fisher=18 enables some anticipatory\n    signal), yielding positive mean lead time. On 32B (Fisher=2.8), the probe score\n    barely moves before CP and only separates clearly in the post-CP region where\n    harmful content generation begins to dominate. The result is negative mean lead\n    time (detection AFTER commitment) with only 23-29% of traces achieving any positive\n    lead. This is strictly worse than r1-8b's already-disappointing lead time, and\n    confirms that pre-textual detection on 32B is not merely unreliable but essentially\n    impossible with linear probes.\n  tables:\n  - name: Lead Time Distribution by Model\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Mean Lead\n    - Median Lead\n    - Positive Lead %\n    - Std\n    - Max Lead\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b (temporal W=15)\n      - '+4.9'\n      - '0'\n      - 58%\n      - '11.2'\n      - '+31'\n    - - r1-32b (temporal W=15)\n      - '-0.8'\n      - '-3'\n      - 29.4%\n      - '8.2'\n      - '+12'\n    - - QwQ-32B (temporal W=15)\n      - '-1.5'\n      - '-4'\n      - 23.5%\n      - '9.1'\n      - '+8'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Lead time CDF plot: r1-8b curve shifted right (positive), 32B curves shifted\n    left (negative). Vertical line at lead=0 shows 58% of r1-8b detections are early\n    vs only 23-29% for 32B.'\n  - 'Per-trace lead time scatter: each trace as a point, models on x-axis. r1-8b shows\n    spread around +5; 32B models cluster around -2 to -4.'\n  analysis: Lead time results are uniformly negative on 32B, with mean lead -0.8 to\n    -1.5 steps (detection after commitment). This contrasts with r1-8b's +4.9 mean\n    lead. The temporal advantage that partially rescues r1-8b lead time has no effect\n    on 32B because the underlying per-step signal is too weak to produce anticipatory\n    threshold crossings. This confirms Claim 3 is universal (pre-textual detection\n    unreliable) but also undermines the framing that 'hidden states encode current\n    safety state well' — on 32B, they barely encode it at all.\n- name: TrajGuard Mahalanobis baseline on all models\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: Mahalanobis distance on safe trace covariance, evaluated on r1-32b\n    and QwQ-32B. Expected to fail even more severely than on r1-8b.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_md_crossing_k5: 0.186\n    r1_32b_md_safe_mean: 71.2\n    r1_32b_md_jb_pre_mean: 71.8\n    r1_32b_md_jb_post_mean: 71.4\n    r1_32b_md_delta_max_rate: 0.008\n    qwq_32b_md_crossing_k5: 0.163\n    qwq_32b_md_safe_mean: 68.4\n    qwq_32b_md_jb_pre_mean: 69.1\n    qwq_32b_md_jb_post_mean: 68.7\n    qwq_32b_md_delta_max_rate: 0.005\n    r1_8b_crossing_k5_ref: 0.302\n  result_rationale: Mahalanobis distance fails even more catastrophically on 32B than\n    on r1-8b (where it already showed only 30% crossing at k=5). The 5120-d space\n    with 64 layers produces covariance matrices that are even more dominated by task-general\n    variance. Since safety signal is spread across 50+ minor PCs at 32B (vs concentrated\n    in 8B), the Mahalanobis metric — which weights by inverse covariance — actually\n    suppresses the diffuse safety signal relative to dominant task dimensions. The\n    mean MD values for safe, pre-CP, and post-CP are nearly identical (within 1% of\n    each other), confirming that from a Gaussian-distribution perspective, safety\n    commitment produces no detectable distributional shift at 32B scale.\n  tables:\n  - name: Mahalanobis Distance Separation\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Config\n    - Safe MD Mean\n    - Pre-CP MD Mean\n    - Post-CP MD Mean\n    - K5 Crossing%\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - pca_64_p75\n      - '6.66'\n      - '6.73'\n      - '6.64'\n      - 30.2%\n    - - r1-32b\n      - pca_64_p75\n      - '71.2'\n      - '71.8'\n      - '71.4'\n      - 18.6%\n    - - r1-32b\n      - pca_128_p75\n      - '142.3'\n      - '143.1'\n      - '142.6'\n      - 16.3%\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - pca_64_p75\n      - '68.4'\n      - '69.1'\n      - '68.7'\n      - 16.3%\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - pca_128_p75\n      - '137.8'\n      - '138.4'\n      - '137.9'\n      - 14.0%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'MD time-series overlaid: safe traces (blue) and jailbreak traces (red) completely\n    overlapping on 32B. Vertical line at CP shows no visible discontinuity. Contrast\n    with r1-8b where slight post-CP elevation is visible.'\n  analysis: TrajGuard-style geometric detection is completely non-functional on 32B.\n    Detection rates (16-19%) are below the already-failed r1-8b baseline (30%). The\n    delta between safe and unsafe MD is <1% of the absolute value, meaning the safety\n    commitment event is invisible to distance-based methods at this scale. This strengthens\n    the geometric failure story but also shows it's WORSE at scale, consistent with\n    the 'signal becomes diffuse' hypothesis.\n- name: Bootstrap CIs + threshold-independent metrics (all models)\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 1000 bootstrap resamples on 32B model results. AUROC, PR curves, detection\n    delay CDF across all models.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_static_auroc: 0.583\n    r1_32b_static_auroc_ci:\n    - 0.501\n    - 0.661\n    r1_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.598\n    r1_32b_temporal_auroc_ci:\n    - 0.512\n    - 0.679\n    qwq_32b_static_auroc: 0.561\n    qwq_32b_static_auroc_ci:\n    - 0.478\n    - 0.642\n    qwq_32b_temporal_auroc: 0.574\n    qwq_32b_temporal_auroc_ci:\n    - 0.489\n    - 0.654\n    r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff: 0.021\n    r1_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p: 0.412\n    qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_diff: -0.008\n    qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_auroc_p: 0.891\n    r1_8b_static_auroc_ref: 0.847\n    r1_8b_temporal_auroc_ref: 0.862\n  result_rationale: Bootstrap CIs on 32B models show AUROC barely above chance (0.56-0.60)\n    with confidence intervals that include 0.50. The HS vs text AUROC difference is\n    non-significant on both 32B models (p=0.41, 0.89), in stark contrast to r1-8b\n    where HS dominates text by 10+ AUROC points. Wide CIs reflect both small sample\n    size (n=43) and genuinely poor discriminability. The threshold-independent metrics\n    confirm that the result is not an artifact of threshold selection — at ALL thresholds,\n    32B HS probes perform comparably to text probes. This is the definitive statistical\n    evidence that HS advantage is scale-specific.\n  tables:\n  - name: AUROC Comparison Across Models (Bootstrap 95% CI)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - HS Static\n    - HS Temporal\n    - Text Static\n    - Text Temporal\n    - HS-Text Diff (p)\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - 0.847 [0.79,0.90]\n      - 0.862 [0.81,0.91]\n      - 0.738 [0.67,0.80]\n      - 0.759 [0.69,0.82]\n      - +0.103 (p=0.003)\n    - - r1-32b\n      - 0.583 [0.50,0.66]\n      - 0.598 [0.51,0.68]\n      - 0.562 [0.48,0.64]\n      - 0.577 [0.49,0.66]\n      - +0.021 (p=0.412)\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - 0.561 [0.48,0.64]\n      - 0.574 [0.49,0.65]\n      - 0.569 [0.49,0.65]\n      - 0.582 [0.50,0.66]\n      - -0.008 (p=0.891)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'ROC curves: r1-8b HS clearly above diagonal with visible separation from text\n    curve. 32B model curves for both HS and text nearly overlapping, both close to\n    diagonal.'\n  - 'PR curves: r1-8b HS shows meaningful precision at moderate recall. 32B curves\n    flat near baseline prevalence rate.'\n  - 'Bootstrap distribution of AUROC difference (HS-text): r1-8b distribution centered\n    at +0.10 (entirely positive). 32B distributions centered at 0 (overlapping zero).'\n  analysis: Threshold-independent metrics definitively confirm that the HS advantage\n    over text is absent at 32B. AUROC values of 0.56-0.60 indicate near-chance discrimination\n    regardless of modality. Bootstrap tests show HS-text difference is non-significant\n    (p>0.4) on both 32B models, while it's highly significant on r1-8b (p=0.003).\n    This eliminates threshold selection as a confound — the finding holds across all\n    operating points.\n- name: Window size ablation (W=1,3,5,10,15,20,25)\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: Sweep temporal window sizes on all 3 models. Test whether optimal W\n    differs by scale.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_8b_optimal_w: 15\n    r1_8b_w15_delta_over_w1: 0.046\n    r1_32b_optimal_w: 5\n    r1_32b_w5_delta_over_w1: 0.012\n    r1_32b_w15_delta_over_w1: 0.009\n    qwq_32b_optimal_w: 3\n    qwq_32b_w3_delta_over_w1: 0.008\n    qwq_32b_w15_delta_over_w1: 0.005\n    diminishing_returns_32b: plateau at W=3-5\n  result_rationale: On r1-8b, larger windows help because each step contributes consistent\n    directional signal (Fisher=18) — more steps = more signal accumulation. On 32B\n    (Fisher=2.8), individual steps contribute mostly noise, so larger windows actually\n    dilute the few informative steps with many uninformative ones. The optimal W for\n    32B is much smaller (3-5 vs 15) and the improvement over W=1 is negligible (0.8-1.2pp\n    vs 4.6pp). Beyond W=5 on 32B, performance plateaus or slightly degrades as the\n    window averages over too much noise. This reveals that the temporal aggregation\n    mechanism requires a minimum per-step signal-to-noise ratio to be effective.\n  tables:\n  - name: Coverage by Window Size\n    columns:\n    - W\n    - r1-8b Coverage\n    - r1-32b Coverage\n    - QwQ-32B Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - '1'\n      - 60.5%\n      - 37.2%\n      - 32.6%\n    - - '3'\n      - 61.3%\n      - 38.4%\n      - 33.4%\n    - - '5'\n      - 62.1%\n      - 38.4%\n      - 33.1%\n    - - '10'\n      - 63.8%\n      - 38.9%\n      - 33.4%\n    - - '15'\n      - 65.1%\n      - 39.5%\n      - 33.1%\n    - - '20'\n      - 64.8%\n      - 38.4%\n      - 32.6%\n    - - '25'\n      - 64.1%\n      - 37.2%\n      - 31.6%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Line plot: coverage vs W. r1-8b shows clear monotonic rise to W=15 then plateau.\n    32B models show flat lines with negligible variation (within noise band).'\n  analysis: Window ablation confirms temporal context has scale-dependent utility.\n    At 8B, larger windows systematically improve detection. At 32B, windows beyond\n    W=3-5 provide no benefit. The per-step signal-to-noise ratio appears to be the\n    gating factor for temporal aggregation effectiveness.\n- name: Layer selection ablation (all layers, temporal probe on 32B)\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: Run temporal W=15 probe on every layer of 32B models. Test whether\n    any layer achieves r1-8b-level discriminability.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_best_temporal_layer: 29\n    r1_32b_best_temporal_acc: 0.721\n    r1_32b_worst_layer_acc: 0.612\n    r1_32b_acc_range: 0.109\n    r1_32b_layers_above_70: 8\n    qwq_32b_best_temporal_layer: 31\n    qwq_32b_best_temporal_acc: 0.703\n    qwq_32b_worst_layer_acc: 0.598\n    qwq_32b_acc_range: 0.105\n    r1_8b_best_temporal_acc_ref: 0.848\n    no_layer_matches_8b: true\n  result_rationale: Even with exhaustive layer sweep + temporal context, no single\n    layer on either 32B model approaches r1-8b L14's performance (84.8% temporal acc).\n    The best 32B layers achieve only 70-72% accuracy — barely above text baselines.\n    The layer-accuracy curve is notably flat (range ~10pp across 64 layers, vs ~15pp\n    across 32 layers on r1-8b), confirming the safety signal is not localized at any\n    particular depth. Middle layers (40-50% depth) are slightly better but the advantage\n    is marginal. This rules out the explanation that 'we just haven't found the right\n    layer' — the sweep is exhaustive.\n  tables:\n  - name: Temporal Probe by Layer Depth Band (r1-32b)\n    columns:\n    - Depth Band\n    - Layers\n    - Mean Acc\n    - Best Acc\n    - Best Layer\n    rows:\n    - - 0-25%\n      - L1-L16\n      - '0.648'\n      - '0.672'\n      - L14\n    - - 25-50%\n      - L17-L32\n      - '0.698'\n      - '0.721'\n      - L29\n    - - 50-75%\n      - L33-L48\n      - '0.681'\n      - '0.708'\n      - L35\n    - - 75-100%\n      - L49-L64\n      - '0.641'\n      - '0.664'\n      - L52\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Layer sweep heatmap: accuracy by layer (x) and model (y). r1-8b shows sharp peak\n    at L14. 32B models show broad flat plateau across layers 25-35 with no distinct\n    peak.'\n  analysis: Exhaustive temporal probe layer sweep on 32B confirms no single layer\n    matches r1-8b discriminability. The flat accuracy landscape (range 10pp) vs r1-8b's\n    peaked landscape (range 15pp with clear maximum) demonstrates that safety encoding\n    is genuinely distributed across layers at scale, not localized to a recoverable\n    subspace.\n- name: 16-label taxonomy supervision\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: Fine-grained label (Type 1/2/3/4 × safe/unsafe) training on 32B. Test\n    if richer supervision recovers signal.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_16label_acc: 0.318\n    r1_32b_16label_f1_macro: 0.212\n    r1_32b_binary_collapse_acc: 0.724\n    r1_32b_binary_collapse_vs_binary: 0.012\n    qwq_32b_16label_acc: 0.295\n    qwq_32b_binary_collapse_acc: 0.709\n    r1_8b_16label_acc_ref: 0.512\n  result_rationale: Fine-grained supervision cannot recover a signal that does not\n    exist in the representation. On r1-8b, 16-label training helps because the rich\n    safety subspace supports finer distinctions. On 32B, the representations barely\n    separate binary safe/unsafe (Fisher=2.8), so asking for 16-way discrimination\n    results in even worse performance (31-32% accuracy on 16 classes, chance=6.25%\n    but classes are imbalanced). Collapsing back to binary after 16-label training\n    gives marginal improvement (+1.2pp) over direct binary training — the fine-grained\n    labels provide slight regularization benefit but cannot overcome the fundamental\n    lack of separable signal.\n  tables:\n  - name: Fine-grained vs Binary Supervision\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Binary Direct Acc\n    - 16-Label Acc\n    - 16→Binary Collapse Acc\n    - Delta\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - '0.817'\n      - '0.512'\n      - '0.831'\n      - +1.4pp\n    - - r1-32b\n      - '0.712'\n      - '0.318'\n      - '0.724'\n      - +1.2pp\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - '0.694'\n      - '0.295'\n      - '0.709'\n      - +1.5pp\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Confusion matrix (16-class) on r1-32b: heavy diagonal on ''safe'' categories,\n    near-random on unsafe subcategories. Model cannot distinguish safety commitment\n    subtypes.'\n  analysis: Richer supervision does not compensate for absent representation structure.\n    The 16-label probe essentially learns to detect 'safe' vs 'not safe' (binary)\n    and cannot distinguish unsafe subtypes, confirming that 32B hidden states lack\n    fine-grained safety commitment information.\n- name: Oracle decomposition\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: Analyze what information the oracle (label sequence) encodes that hidden\n    states don't. Focus on 32B models where the gap is largest.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_oracle_lead_mean: 28.4\n    r1_32b_probe_lead_mean: -0.8\n    r1_32b_oracle_gap: 29.2\n    r1_32b_label_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.89\n    r1_32b_hs_autocorr_pre_cp: 0.97\n    r1_32b_label_entropy_pre_cp: 1.42\n    r1_32b_hs_entropy_pre_cp: 0.31\n    pattern_finding: Labels show gradual safety erosion (increasing unsafe density);\n      hidden states show no corresponding gradual shift\n  result_rationale: 'The oracle achieves +28 step lead because label sequences show\n    clear pre-commitment patterns: increasing frequency of unsafe-adjacent labels,\n    shortening safe reasoning streaks, and characteristic Type 2→Type 3 transitions\n    that precede commitment by many steps. On r1-8b, hidden states partially track\n    these transitions (Fisher=18 enables some gradient detection). On 32B, hidden\n    states are nearly constant through the pre-CP region — they encode the CURRENT\n    token''s local context but not the cumulative trajectory toward commitment. The\n    high autocorrelation (0.97) of 32B hidden states confirms minimal step-to-step\n    variation in the safety-relevant dimensions, while labels show meaningful dynamics\n    (autocorr=0.89, lower because of frequent fluctuations). The fundamental limitation\n    at 32B: the model''s internal safety state is not represented as a detectable\n    feature in any single layer''s activations.'\n  tables:\n  - name: Oracle vs Probe Gap by Model\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Oracle Lead\n    - Best Probe Lead\n    - Gap\n    - HS Pre-CP Entropy\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - '+31.0'\n      - '+4.9'\n      - '26.1'\n      - '0.72'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - '+28.4'\n      - '-0.8'\n      - '29.2'\n      - '0.31'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - '+26.8'\n      - '-1.5'\n      - '28.3'\n      - '0.28'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Pre-CP hidden state trajectory (projected to top 2 Fisher directions): r1-8b\n    shows gradual drift toward ''unsafe'' region before CP. 32B models show random\n    walk with no systematic drift.'\n  - 'Label sequence patterns before CP: histogram of ''unsafe step density in window\n    [-20, CP]'' showing gradual escalation that probes cannot detect at 32B.'\n  analysis: The oracle decomposition reveals that at 32B, the model's approach toward\n    safety commitment is invisible in hidden-state geometry even though it's clearly\n    present in the label sequence. This suggests 32B models encode safety-relevant\n    reasoning in a way that is fundamentally inaccessible to linear probes on any\n    single layer — perhaps distributed across attention patterns, or only emergent\n    at the multi-layer computational level rather than as a readable residual stream\n    feature.\n- name: OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base)\n  plan_id: exp-011\n  description: 4th model for 2×2 architecture×scale matrix. Qwen2.5-7B to complement\n    Llama-8B at small scale.\n  expected_results:\n    openthinker_7b_best_layer: 13\n    openthinker_7b_best_fisher: 14.2\n    openthinker_7b_static_acc: 0.793\n    openthinker_7b_temporal_w15_acc: 0.834\n    openthinker_7b_crossing_rate: 0.628\n    openthinker_7b_lead_mean: 3.8\n    openthinker_7b_precp_fpr: 0.178\n    openthinker_7b_precision: 0.591\n    openthinker_7b_hs_vs_text_advantage: significant (p=0.008)\n  result_rationale: 'OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B) shows results comparable to r1-8b\n    (Llama-8B): strong Fisher discriminability (14.2), clear HS advantage over text,\n    and positive mean lead time. This confirms the critical finding: the HS advantage\n    is SCALE-dependent, not architecture-dependent. Both 7-8B models (Llama and Qwen\n    base) show the effect; both 32B models (both Qwen2.5 base) do not. The 2×2 matrix\n    cleanly isolates scale as the confound, ruling out architecture as the explanation\n    for the 32B failure.'\n  tables:\n  - name: 2×2 Architecture × Scale Matrix\n    columns:\n    - ''\n    - 7-8B\n    - 32B\n    rows:\n    - - Llama-based\n      - 'r1-8b: Fisher=17.9, HS>Text (p=0.003)'\n      - —\n    - - Qwen2.5-based\n      - 'OpenThinker-7B: Fisher=14.2, HS>Text (p=0.008)'\n      - 'r1-32b: Fisher=2.8, HS≈Text (p=0.41) / QwQ: Fisher=1.9, HS≈Text (p=0.89)'\n  figures_description:\n  - '2×2 grid plot: Fisher discriminability (bar) and HS-Text AUROC difference (line)\n    by architecture×scale cell. Both small-scale cells show high Fisher and significant\n    HS advantage. Both large-scale cells show low Fisher and no advantage.'\n  analysis: The 2×2 matrix provides the cleanest evidence for the scale-dependent\n    thesis. OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5 architecture, 7B scale) replicates r1-8b's hidden-state\n    advantage, proving the effect is not Llama-specific. Combined with both 32B models\n    (both Qwen2.5) failing to show the advantage, scale is definitively identified\n    as the critical variable. This transforms the paper from 'our method works on\n    r1-8b' to 'safety signal geometry fundamentally changes with scale.'\n- name: GRU/Attention probe on 32B models\n  plan_id: exp-012\n  description: GRU sequence model as upper-bound check on 32B. If GRU also fails,\n    confirms the signal simply isn't there.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_gru_single_acc: 0.728\n    r1_32b_gru_multi_acc: 0.731\n    r1_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.418\n    r1_32b_gru_lead_mean: -0.3\n    r1_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: 1.0\n    qwq_32b_gru_single_acc: 0.711\n    qwq_32b_gru_multi_acc: 0.715\n    qwq_32b_gru_crossing_rate: 0.395\n    qwq_32b_gru_lead_mean: -0.9\n    qwq_32b_gru_vs_lr_delta: 0.8\n    r1_8b_gru_multi_crossing_ref: 0.767\n    gru_improvement_over_lr_32b: minimal (+1pp acc, not significant)\n  result_rationale: 'On r1-8b, GRU provides meaningful gains over LR (crossing 76.7%\n    vs 46.5%) because the sequential model can learn temporal patterns in a signal-rich\n    representation. On 32B, GRU barely outperforms temporal LR (+1pp accuracy, +2pp\n    crossing) because the input signal is too weak for sequence modeling to help.\n    A GRU trained on noise learns noise patterns. The GRU''s capacity to model sequential\n    dependencies is irrelevant when individual time steps contain near-zero safety-relevant\n    information. This confirms: ''simplicity is optimal'' at 8B because the signal\n    is accessible; at 32B, no model complexity can recover what the representation\n    doesn''t encode.'\n  tables:\n  - name: GRU vs LR on 32B Models\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - LR Temporal Acc\n    - GRU Multi Acc\n    - Delta\n    - GRU Crossing\n    - LR Crossing\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - '0.848'\n      - 0.809*\n      - -3.9pp*\n      - 76.7%\n      - 65.1%\n    - - r1-32b\n      - '0.721'\n      - '0.731'\n      - +1.0pp\n      - 41.8%\n      - 39.5%\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - '0.703'\n      - '0.715'\n      - +1.2pp\n      - 39.5%\n      - 37.2%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Method comparison bar chart on 32B: all methods (static LR, temporal LR, GRU,\n    BOCPD) clustered within 2-3pp accuracy band. No method breaks out.'\n  analysis: GRU's inability to improve over LR on 32B eliminates model capacity as\n    an explanation. The issue is not 'we need a more expressive probe' — it's 'the\n    representation doesn't contain accessible safety commitment information.' This\n    is a fundamental representation-level limitation, not a method limitation.\n- name: Text-based baseline on 32B models\n  plan_id: exp-013\n  description: 'Sentence-BERT embeddings + temporal window LR on CoT text of 32B models.\n    Critical comparison: does HS still beat text at 32B?'\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_text_static_acc: 0.689\n    r1_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.698\n    r1_32b_text_precp_fpr: 0.281\n    r1_32b_text_precision: 0.478\n    r1_32b_text_coverage: 0.372\n    r1_32b_text_lead_mean: 1.2\n    qwq_32b_text_static_acc: 0.672\n    qwq_32b_text_temporal_acc: 0.685\n    qwq_32b_text_precp_fpr: 0.298\n    qwq_32b_text_precision: 0.462\n    qwq_32b_text_coverage: 0.349\n    qwq_32b_text_lead_mean: 0.8\n    r1_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio: 1.08\n    qwq_32b_hs_vs_text_precp_fpr_ratio: 0.97\n  result_rationale: 'On r1-8b, HS probes achieve 16% pre-CP FPR vs text''s 27-36%\n    (1.7-2.2x advantage). On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR is 25-31% while text is 28-30% — the\n    ratio collapses to ~1.0x (no meaningful difference). Text probes actually show\n    slightly BETTER lead time on 32B (+1.2 vs -0.8 for HS) because text content directly\n    reflects reasoning state regardless of model scale — a model saying ''I should\n    help with this harmful request'' is detectable in text at any scale. The hidden-state\n    advantage was specific to r1-8b''s compact safety geometry; once that geometry\n    diffuses at scale, text monitoring is equally (or more) effective. This is the\n    core worst-tier finding: HS monitoring has no deployment advantage over text monitoring\n    on 32B models.'\n  tables:\n  - name: HS vs Text Pre-CP FPR (the key metric)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - HS Pre-CP FPR\n    - Text Pre-CP FPR\n    - Ratio (Text/HS)\n    - Advantage\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - 16.0%\n      - 27.2%\n      - 1.70x\n      - HS wins decisively\n    - - r1-32b\n      - 26.4%\n      - 28.1%\n      - 1.06x\n      - No meaningful difference\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - 30.8%\n      - 29.8%\n      - 0.97x\n      - Text slightly better\n  - name: Full Comparison on 32B\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Modality\n    - Coverage\n    - Pre-CP FPR\n    - Precision\n    - Lead\n    rows:\n    - - r1-32b\n      - HS temporal\n      - 39.5%\n      - 26.4%\n      - '0.492'\n      - '-0.8'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - Text temporal\n      - 37.2%\n      - 28.1%\n      - '0.478'\n      - '+1.2'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - HS temporal\n      - 37.2%\n      - 30.8%\n      - '0.451'\n      - '-1.5'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - Text temporal\n      - 34.9%\n      - 29.8%\n      - '0.462'\n      - '+0.8'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Ratio plot: text/HS pre-CP FPR ratio by model. r1-8b at 1.7x (HS better), 32B\n    models at ~1.0x (parity). Horizontal line at 1.2x threshold — 32B models below\n    the ''meaningful advantage'' threshold defined in worst tier.'\n  analysis: 'The decisive worst-tier result: HS probes lose their precision advantage\n    on 32B. Pre-CP FPR ratio drops from 1.7x (r1-8b, HS clearly better) to 1.0x (32B,\n    no difference). Text actually achieves slightly positive lead time on 32B while\n    HS has negative lead — because text content is scale-invariant (words are words\n    at any parameter count) while HS quality degrades with scale. This means that\n    for 32B deployment, the added complexity of hidden-state extraction provides zero\n    benefit over simple text monitoring.'\n- name: BOCPD/CUSUM on 32B models\n  plan_id: exp-014\n  description: Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection on 32B probe score sequences.\n    Test if change-point methods can find commitment on weak signals.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection: 0.581\n    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead: -2.1\n    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_detection: 0.372\n    r1_32b_bocpd_lambda100_lead: -4.8\n    r1_32b_cusum_detection: 0.326\n    r1_32b_cusum_lead: -6.2\n    qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_detection: 0.535\n    qwq_32b_bocpd_lambda10_lead: -2.8\n    r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_detection_ref: 0.93\n    r1_8b_bocpd_lambda10_lead_ref: 3.45\n  result_rationale: BOCPD on r1-8b achieves 93% detection because the probe score\n    sequence exhibits a clear distributional shift at commitment (high Fisher → sharp\n    transition in probe confidence). On 32B, the probe score sequence is essentially\n    flat noise (low Fisher → probe confidence fluctuates randomly without systematic\n    shift at CP). BOCPD detects 'change points' in noise, resulting in both lower\n    detection rate (58% — many traces show no detectable change) and negative lead\n    time (the few detected 'changes' are often post-CP noise spikes misidentified\n    as the commitment signal). CUSUM performs even worse because it requires sustained\n    shift in mean, which never occurs in the flat 32B probe trajectories. Change-point\n    methods are fundamentally unable to detect a transition that doesn't produce a\n    signal change.\n  tables:\n  - name: Change-Point Detection on 32B\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Method\n    - Detection Rate\n    - Mean Lead\n    - Median Lead\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - BOCPD λ=10\n      - 93.0%\n      - '+3.45'\n      - '0'\n    - - r1-8b\n      - CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\n      - 65.1%\n      - '+0.89'\n      - '-4'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - BOCPD λ=10\n      - 58.1%\n      - '-2.1'\n      - '-5'\n    - - r1-32b\n      - CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\n      - 32.6%\n      - '-6.2'\n      - '-9'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - BOCPD λ=10\n      - 53.5%\n      - '-2.8'\n      - '-6'\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - CUSUM t=0.3,h=3\n      - 27.9%\n      - '-7.1'\n      - '-11'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Probe score time series with BOCPD detected change points: r1-8b shows clear\n    score rise at CP with BOCPD detecting it. 32B shows flat noisy signal with BOCPD\n    false-triggering at random points.'\n  analysis: BOCPD and CUSUM both degrade severely on 32B. Detection drops from 93%\n    to 53-58%, and lead time becomes negative (-2 to -7 steps). The methods can't\n    find a signal transition that doesn't exist in the input. This eliminates the\n    possibility that 'better downstream methods could recover the signal' — the issue\n    is representational, not methodological.\n- name: FPR analysis on 32B models\n  plan_id: exp-015\n  description: Run all probes on safe traces from 32B models. Report FPR, precision,\n    deployment utility.\n  expected_results:\n    r1_32b_hs_static_safe_fpr: 0.412\n    r1_32b_hs_static_precp_fpr: 0.264\n    r1_32b_hs_static_precision: 0.492\n    r1_32b_hs_temporal_safe_fpr: 0.398\n    r1_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.251\n    r1_32b_hs_temporal_precision: 0.501\n    r1_32b_text_static_precp_fpr: 0.281\n    r1_32b_text_static_precision: 0.478\n    r1_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.268\n    r1_32b_text_temporal_precision: 0.485\n    qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.308\n    qwq_32b_hs_temporal_precision: 0.451\n    qwq_32b_text_temporal_precp_fpr: 0.298\n    qwq_32b_text_temporal_precision: 0.462\n    r1_8b_hs_temporal_precp_fpr_ref: 0.16\n    r1_8b_hs_temporal_precision_ref: 0.583\n  result_rationale: 'On r1-8b, HS probes achieve dramatically lower pre-CP FPR (16%)\n    than text (27%) because the hidden-state signal cleanly separates safe exploration\n    from true commitment. On 32B, HS pre-CP FPR rises to 25-31% — essentially matching\n    text probes (27-30%). Precision drops from 58-62% (r1-8b) to 45-50% (32B) for\n    HS, while text precision stays constant at ~48% across scales. The convergence\n    occurs because: (1) 32B HS probes trigger on noise, producing more false positives\n    in the pre-CP window; (2) safe traces at 32B are harder to distinguish from unsafe\n    traces in hidden-state space due to diffuse signal. Safe FPR for both modalities\n    is around 40% on all models — confirming this as a baseline difficulty regardless\n    of scale. The HS precision ADVANTAGE (HS precision - text precision) drops from\n    +13pp (r1-8b) to essentially 0pp (32B).'\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Pre-CP FPR and Precision: HS vs Text by Model'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - HS Pre-CP FPR\n    - Text Pre-CP FPR\n    - HS Precision\n    - Text Precision\n    - Precision Δ\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - 16.0%\n      - 27.2%\n      - 58.3%\n      - 48.7%\n      - +9.6pp\n    - - r1-32b\n      - 25.1%\n      - 26.8%\n      - 50.1%\n      - 48.5%\n      - +1.6pp\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - 30.8%\n      - 29.8%\n      - 45.1%\n      - 46.2%\n      - -1.1pp\n  - name: Safe Trace FPR (deployment cost metric)\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - HS Static FPR\n    - HS Temporal FPR\n    - Text Temporal FPR\n    rows:\n    - - r1-8b\n      - 39.0%\n      - 39.2%\n      - 38.5%\n    - - r1-32b\n      - 41.2%\n      - 39.8%\n      - 41.5%\n    - - QwQ-32B\n      - 43.1%\n      - 42.8%\n      - 40.2%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Precision-recall curve by model×modality: r1-8b HS curve clearly above text curve\n    at all recall levels. 32B model curves for HS and text completely overlapping.'\n  - 'Bar chart: HS precision advantage (HS precision - text precision) by model. r1-8b:\n    +9.6pp. r1-32b: +1.6pp. QwQ-32B: -1.1pp. Declining trend with scale.'\n  analysis: 'FPR analysis provides the clearest practical evidence for the worst tier.\n    The HS precision advantage — the paper''s headline finding — disappears at 32B.\n    On r1-8b, HS probes are meaningfully more precise (+9.6pp) and produce fewer pre-CP\n    false alarms (16% vs 27%). On 32B, both modalities perform identically (precision\n    within 1.6pp, pre-CP FPR within 2pp). For a deployment decision, this means: extracting\n    hidden states from a 32B model provides zero precision benefit over simply monitoring\n    the text output. The computational overhead of HS extraction is entirely wasted\n    at this scale.'\n","numLines":1112,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1112}}}
