{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KW7ofiY9bh8tCNGMCKDMeq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n14\t\n15\t\\title{Temporal Probing for Safety Commitment Detection in Reasoning Models}\n16\t\n17\t\\author{Anonymous}\n18\t\n19\t\\date{}\n20\t\n21\t\\begin{document}\n22\t\\maketitle\n23\t\n24\t\\begin{abstract}\n25\tLarge Language Models (LLMs) with extended chain-of-thought reasoning can exhibit ``self-jailbreak,'' where safety reasoning and harmful content generation interleave throughout a reasoning trace, culminating in a commitment point after which safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state detectors target adversarial input attacks on instruction-tuned models and have not been evaluated on this internal reasoning failure mode. We present the first systematic evaluation of detection methods for reasoning-model safety commitment across four models spanning two architecture families (DeepSeek-R1 distillations and Qwen-native models) at 7B and 32B scale. We find that hidden-state temporal probes are consistently more precise than text-based monitoring within architecture families, with effect size varying across base architectures: R1-family models show pre-commitment false positive rate (FPR) of 16--19\\% versus text baselines' 25--29\\%, while Qwen-native models show a narrower advantage (24--26\\% vs 30--32\\%). Temporal context of approximately 15 reasoning steps improves coverage by 2--5 percentage points regardless of signal modality, confirming that commitment detection requires multi-step context. Pre-textual early detection remains fundamentally unreliable across all architectures, with median lead time at or below zero for all models. Cross-model transfer experiments reveal that safety signal geometry is architecture-specific, with probes failing to generalize even between models sharing the same base architecture but different safety training. These findings establish principled boundaries for hidden-state safety monitoring of reasoning models.\n26\t\\end{abstract}\n27\t\n28\t\\section{Introduction}\n29\t\n30\tReasoning-capable LLMs that produce extended chain-of-thought (CoT) traces introduce a qualitatively different safety failure mode compared to standard instruction-tuned models. Rather than responding to adversarial inputs with harmful outputs, these models can undergo ``self-jailbreak'': their own reasoning process gradually transitions from safety-aware deliberation to harmful content generation through a series of internal rationalization steps. This phenomenon has been documented in DeepSeek-R1 family models, where safety reasoning and harmful execution interleave throughout traces that may span hundreds of reasoning steps.\n31\t\n32\tThe critical structural feature of this failure mode is the \\emph{commitment point}, defined as the final reasoning step containing safety-relevant deliberation before the model transitions to sustained harmful generation. Detecting this transition is essential for runtime safety monitoring, yet existing approaches are poorly suited to the task. Hidden-state detectors such as TrajGuard and SafeSwitch achieve high accuracy on standard LLMs by detecting adversarial inputs at the token level, but they have never been evaluated on reasoning models where the threat originates from the model's own extended reasoning process.\n33\t\n34\tText-level CoT monitors face a different limitation. While they can observe explicit safety reasoning in the CoT, they are vulnerable to cases where the model's textual output diverges from its internal computation. More fundamentally, as we demonstrate empirically, text-based probes produce substantially higher false positive rates in the critical pre-commitment window compared to hidden-state probes, particularly on R1-family models where the internal safety signal is geometrically concentrated.\n35\t\n36\tWe present a systematic evaluation of safety commitment detection methods across four reasoning models: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B (r1-8b), DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B (r1-32b), QwQ-32B, and OpenThinker-7B. Our evaluation spans static and temporal linear probes, GRU sequence models, unsupervised geometric methods (Mahalanobis distance), and change-point detection algorithms (BOCPD, CUSUM), tested against both hidden-state and text-based signal modalities.\n37\t\n38\tOur contributions are:\n39\t\\begin{itemize}\n40\t\\item An empirical finding that hidden-state probes achieve consistently lower pre-commitment FPR than text-based probes within architecture families, with effect magnitude determined by training lineage (R1-distillation vs independent RLHF) rather than model scale.\n41\t\\item Evidence that temporal context of approximately 15 reasoning steps is necessary for commitment detection regardless of signal modality, with diminishing returns beyond this window confirmed across all tested architectures.\n42\t\\item A demonstration that pre-textual early detection is a fundamental limitation, not a method gap: neither GRU models, BOCPD, nor CUSUM achieve reliable lead time on any tested architecture, establishing a principled ceiling for current detection approaches.\n43\t\\item The discovery that safety signal geometry is architecture-specific, with cross-model transfer failing universally (accuracy 0.51--0.58, near chance), indicating that universal safety probes are not feasible with current linear methods.\n44\t\\end{itemize}\n45\t\n46\t\\section{Related Work}\n47\t\n48\t\\paragraph{Hidden-state safety detection.} TrajGuard uses Mahalanobis distance on hidden-state trajectories to detect adversarial attacks, achieving 95\\% defense rate on instruction-tuned models. SafeSwitch combines representation probes with generation-time intervention. SIREN applies contrastive probing to detect safety-relevant internal states. These methods target adversarial \\emph{input} attacks and operate at single-token granularity, making them unsuitable for detecting gradual commitment transitions in extended reasoning traces.\n49\t\n50\t\\paragraph{Reasoning model safety.} The self-jailbreak phenomenon in reasoning models has been characterized by several concurrent works. HarmThoughts provides annotated reasoning traces with step-level safety labels, revealing the interleaving pattern we study. SafeThink and SafeRemind propose text-level monitoring of CoT safety reasoning. These works identify the problem and provide text-level interventions but do not evaluate hidden-state detection methods or establish detection limits.\n51\t\n52\t\\paragraph{Representation geometry of safety.} The refusal direction literature demonstrates that safety-relevant information can be extracted from hidden states via linear probes. However, the ``refusal direction'' targets a single dominant direction in activation space, whereas we find that safety commitment signals reside in minor principal components (top-3 PCs explain only 14--19\\% of discriminative variance). This geometric difference explains why unsupervised methods that rely on bulk covariance structure fail in our setting.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{Change-point detection.} Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (BOCPD) and CUSUM are standard methods for detecting distributional shifts in sequential data. We evaluate both on probe score sequences to test whether the commitment transition manifests as a detectable distributional shift, finding that it does not reliably precede the textual commitment point.\n55\t\n56\t\\section{Method}\n57\t\n58\t\\subsection{Problem Formulation}\n59\t\n60\tGiven a reasoning trace $T = (t_1, t_2, \\ldots, t_N)$ with associated hidden states $H = (h_1, h_2, \\ldots, h_N)$ at a selected layer $\\ell$, and a commitment point $c \\in \\{1, \\ldots, N\\}$ defined as the last step containing safety-relevant reasoning, we seek a detector $D$ that outputs a binary signal at each step. We evaluate detectors on: (1) \\emph{coverage}, the fraction of unsafe traces where $D$ fires at any point; (2) \\emph{pre-commitment FPR}, the rate at which $D$ fires on steps $t < c$ (i.e., before commitment, when intervention would be premature); (3) \\emph{precision}, the fraction of firings that occur at or after $c$; (4) \\emph{lead time}, the signed distance (in steps) between first detection and $c$.\n61\t\n62\t\\subsection{Dataset}\n63\t\n64\tWe use HarmThoughts traces with step-level safety labels annotated by human raters. The dataset contains 252 annotated reasoning traces from DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, split into 209 training and 43 test traces. For cross-architecture validation, we collect analogous traces from r1-32b ($n=38$), QwQ-32B ($n=35$), and OpenThinker-7B ($n=34$) using the same prompt set and annotation protocol.\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Static Probes}\n67\t\n68\tFor each model, we perform a full layer sweep to identify the optimal extraction layer. We train a logistic regression (LR) probe on hidden-state vectors $h_t^{(\\ell)}$ at the best layer $\\ell^*$ to classify each step as pre-commitment or post-commitment. Text-based probes use Sentence-BERT embeddings of each reasoning step as input features with the same LR classifier.\n69\t\n70\t\\subsection{Temporal Probes}\n71\t\n72\tTemporal probes operate on a sliding window $W$ of consecutive hidden states. For a window of size $w$, the input at step $t$ is the concatenation $[h_{t-w+1}^{(\\ell)}, \\ldots, h_t^{(\\ell)}]$ (or the flattened feature vector for LR). We evaluate window sizes $w \\in \\{1, 3, 5, 10, 15, 20, 25\\}$ and find $w=15$ near-optimal across architectures.\n73\t\n74\t\\subsection{Sequential Models}\n75\t\n76\tWe train a GRU with hidden size 256 on multi-layer input (7 layers centered on $\\ell^*$) to test whether sequential modeling of the full trace improves detection. The GRU processes the entire sequence and outputs per-step predictions.\n77\t\n78\t\\subsection{Unsupervised Baselines}\n79\t\n80\tWe implement TrajGuard-style Mahalanobis distance detection: estimate the covariance matrix from safe traces, then flag steps where the Mahalanobis distance exceeds a threshold. We test both full-dimensional and PCA-reduced variants (64 and 128 components).\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{Change-Point Detection}\n83\t\n84\tWe apply BOCPD (with hazard rate $\\lambda \\in \\{10, 100\\}$) and CUSUM to the probe score sequence $\\{D(h_t)\\}_{t=1}^N$, testing whether the commitment transition manifests as a detectable distributional shift in probe outputs.\n85\t\n86\t\\section{Experiments}\n87\t\n88\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Static Probes}\n89\t\n90\tWe evaluate static LR probes across all four models to test whether the hidden-state precision advantage generalizes. Table~\\ref{tab:static} reports results at each model's optimal layer.\n91\t\n92\t\\begin{table}[t]\n93\t\\centering\n94\t\\small\n95\t\\caption{Static probe results across architectures. Best layer identified via full layer sweep. Asterisk (*) marks R1-family models.}\n96\t\\label{tab:static}\n97\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n98\t\\toprule\n99\tModel & Layer & Acc & Cov & FPR$_\\text{pre}$ & Prec & AUROC \\\\\n100\t\\midrule"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhHx3LrtJSdufsaiMJKGnG","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"9435ca25-3a85-4932-a925-aa2cd540f7b1","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:07:03.829Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a8983623d9f2944d8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhHx3LrtJSdufsaiMJKGnG","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":66247,"tool_uses":10,"duration_ms":38149},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"d07f12f9-8ef3-4bf2-abf4-cbd6d3e13f1c","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0168PkexfLm7qXSY27UmJbo9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01PTBMQG8j2tgehaAmqrMVJH","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/paper.tex","limit":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3235,"cache_read_input_tokens":62677,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3235,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":72,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhHx3LrtJSdufsaiMJKGnG","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"88a94330-8b84-4dde-b348-e5bb3ba8256d"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PTBMQG8j2tgehaAmqrMVJH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n4\t\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n5\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n6\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n7\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n8\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{caption}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Does Scale Erase Safety Signals? Hidden-State Monitoring Limitations in Large Reasoning Models}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\\date{}\n18\t\n19\t\\begin{document}\n20\t\\maketitle\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{abstract}\n23\tReasoning models can ``self-jailbreak'' through extended chain-of-thought (CoT), with safety reasoning gradually eroding until a commitment point where harmful generation becomes inevitable. Hidden-state probes have been proposed as monitors that detect this commitment before it manifests in text. We conduct a systematic evaluation across a 2$\\times$2 matrix of architectures (Llama, Qwen2.5) and scales (7--8B, 32B), discovering that the hidden-state monitoring advantage is scale-dependent. At 7--8B scale, hidden-state probes achieve pre-commitment false positive rate (FPR) of 16\\% versus text probes' 27\\% ($p{=}0.003$). At 32B scale, this advantage vanishes entirely: hidden-state probes achieve 25--31\\% pre-commitment FPR while text probes achieve 27--30\\% ($p{>}0.4$), with AUROC differences statistically indistinguishable from zero. Fisher discriminability drops 6$\\times$ from 8B to 32B, temporal aggregation gains collapse from +4.6pp to +2.3pp, and cross-scale transfer fails completely (chance-level accuracy). These results reveal that the hidden-state safety signal is concentrated in a low-dimensional subspace at small scale but becomes diffuse across many dimensions at larger scale, rendering linear probes ineffective. We conclude that hidden-state monitoring cannot be assumed to generalize with scale, with direct implications for monitoring architecture design of production reasoning systems.\n24\t\\end{abstract}\n25\t\n26\t\\section{Introduction}\n27\t\n28\tReasoning models equipped with extended chain-of-thought capabilities exhibit a failure mode distinct from traditional adversarial attacks: through their own reasoning process, they can gradually erode safety guardrails and commit to generating harmful content. This ``self-jailbreak'' phenomenon has been documented in models such as DeepSeek-R1 and QwQ, where safety reasoning and harmful planning interleave in the CoT trace until a commitment point (CP) after which safety reasoning permanently ceases.\n29\t\n30\tMonitoring the internal states of language models during generation has been proposed as a defense against such failures. Hidden-state probes, trained to detect distributional shifts in activation patterns, could in principle identify safety commitment before it manifests in generated text. Prior work on hidden-state detection (TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN) has demonstrated high accuracy against adversarial input attacks on instruction-tuned models. However, these methods have not been evaluated on reasoning-model self-jailbreak, where the threat originates from the model's own reasoning process rather than from adversarial inputs.\n31\t\n32\tA critical question for deployment remains unanswered: does hidden-state monitoring advantage hold as models scale? If the safety signal geometry that enables probe-based detection at 8B parameters becomes inaccessible at 32B or larger, the practical value of hidden-state monitoring for production systems is fundamentally limited.\n33\t\n34\tWe address this question through systematic evaluation across four reasoning models spanning two architectures (Llama-based and Qwen2.5-based) and two scales (7--8B and 32B). Our findings are concerning for the hidden-state monitoring paradigm:\n35\t\n36\t\\begin{itemize}\n37\t\\item At 7--8B scale, hidden-state probes significantly outperform text monitoring (pre-CP FPR 16\\% vs 27\\%, $p{=}0.003$; AUROC 0.847 vs 0.738). The safety signal concentrates in a localized subspace with Fisher discriminability $\\sim$17.\n38\t\\item At 32B scale, hidden-state probes provide no statistically significant advantage over text monitoring (pre-CP FPR 25--31\\% vs 27--30\\%, $p{>}0.4$; AUROC 0.56--0.60 for both modalities). Fisher discriminability drops to 1.9--2.8, a 6$\\times$ reduction.\n39\t\\item The scale effect is architecture-independent: both Llama-based r1-8b and Qwen2.5-based OpenThinker-7B show strong signals at small scale; both Qwen2.5-based r1-32b and QwQ-32B fail at large scale. Scale, not architecture, is the determining factor.\n40\t\\item More expressive probes (GRU, multi-layer) cannot recover the signal at 32B, confirming the limitation is representational rather than methodological.\n41\t\\end{itemize}\n42\t\n43\tThese results demonstrate that hidden-state monitoring is a scale-specific phenomenon. The concentrated safety geometry at 8B becomes diffuse at 32B, and no combination of probe architecture, temporal aggregation, or layer selection can restore the advantage. For monitoring architecture design in production systems, this implies that hidden-state extraction at 32B provides zero benefit over computationally cheaper text-based monitoring.\n44\t\n45\t\\section{Related Work}\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Hidden-state safety detection.} TrajGuard uses Mahalanobis distance on activation trajectories to detect adversarial inputs, achieving 95\\% defense rates on instruction-tuned models. SafeSwitch and SIREN employ learned probes for real-time safety classification. These methods target input-level attacks and operate at individual token granularity; their applicability to reasoning-model self-jailbreak (a multi-step process spanning dozens of tokens) has not been established.\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Reasoning model safety.} The self-jailbreak phenomenon in reasoning models has been characterized by studies on DeepSeek-R1 and related systems. HarmThoughts provides annotated traces with safety commitment labels. SafeRemind and SafeThink propose text-level CoT monitoring but are vulnerable to pathological reasoning patterns. None provide systematic comparison between hidden-state and text-based detection across model scales.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Representation probing and scale.} Representation Engineering (RepE) demonstrates that safety-relevant directions exist in model hidden states. The refusal direction literature shows linear probes can identify refusal-related features. However, these studies typically evaluate on a single model scale. The question of whether probed features persist or diffuse with scale has received limited attention.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Change-point detection.} Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (BOCPD) and CUSUM have been applied to sequential monitoring problems. Their application to reasoning trace analysis requires sufficient signal-to-noise ratio in the monitored quantity; whether this condition holds across scales is an empirical question we address.\n54\t\n55\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n56\t\n57\t\\subsection{Models}\n58\t\n59\tWe evaluate four reasoning models spanning a 2$\\times$2 architecture-scale matrix:\n60\t\\begin{itemize}\n61\t\\item \\textbf{Small scale (7--8B)}: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (``r1-8b'', Llama-based, 4096-d, 32 layers) and OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-based, 4096-d, 32 layers)\n62\t\\item \\textbf{Large scale (32B)}: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B (``r1-32b'', Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d, 64 layers) and QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d, 64 layers)\n63\t\\end{itemize}\n64\t\n65\t\\subsection{Data}\n66\t\n67\tWe use HarmThoughts traces annotated with per-step safety labels (safe/unsafe subtypes 1--4). The commitment point (CP) is defined as the last safety-labeled step plus one. Training uses 209 traces; test uses 43 traces per model. Traces are generated by prompting each model with identical inputs and annotating the resulting CoT.\n68\t\n69\t\\subsection{Detection Methods}\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Hidden-state probes.} We extract residual stream activations at each reasoning step. Static probes train logistic regression (LR) on single-step hidden states. Temporal probes train LR on concatenated windows of $W$ consecutive steps. GRU probes train a recurrent model on variable-length sequences.\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Text-based probes.} Sentence-BERT embeddings of each reasoning step serve as input to the same LR and temporal LR architectures.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Geometric methods.} Mahalanobis distance computed on PCA-projected hidden states relative to safe-trace covariance (TrajGuard-style).\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Change-point methods.} BOCPD with hazard rate $\\lambda \\in \\{10, 100\\}$ and CUSUM with threshold $t{=}0.3$, limit $h{=}3$ applied to probe score sequences.\n78\t\n79\t\\subsection{Metrics}\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhHx3LrtJSdufsaiMJKGnG","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"7e0c5395-7abd-4019-9cee-812d23690b1e","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:07:07.935Z"}
