{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01XrrAeRu5d5Yqj1oEkf8RYK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DExWFLDo9zP3VsLb3JCmWX","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/best/paper.tex","limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":278,"cache_read_input_tokens":21631,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":278,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":53,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"5f5a6a04-7f4a-4392-81ec-823d42142774"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DExWFLDo9zP3VsLb3JCmWX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n6\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n7\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n8\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n9\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{subcaption}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n14\t\n15\t\\newcommand{\\method}{ContrastiveTrace}\n16\t\\newcommand{\\dataset}{HarmThoughts}\n17\t\n18\t\\title{Hidden States Universally Encode Safety Commitment Better Than Text:\\\\A Cross-Architecture Evaluation of Reasoning Model Monitoring}\n19\t\n20\t\\author{Anonymous}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t\\begin{abstract}\n26\tReasoning models (e.g., DeepSeek-R1, QwQ) exhibit ``self-jailbreak,'' where extended chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning culminates in a commitment point at which safety reasoning permanently ceases and harmful content generation begins. Existing hidden-state detectors target adversarial inputs on standard language models and have never been evaluated on this internal reasoning failure mode. We present the first systematic cross-architecture evaluation of safety commitment detection, comparing hidden-state probes, text-based monitors, and unsupervised geometric methods across four reasoning models spanning two architecture families (Llama, Qwen2.5) and two scales (7--8B, 32B). Our pooled three-model analysis (p=0.003, $I^2$=0.12) establishes that hidden-state temporal probes achieve pre-commitment false positive rates of 14--16\\%, compared to 27--36\\% for text-based probes, a precision advantage that is consistent across all tested architectures. Temporal context (window size $W$=15) universally improves coverage by 5--7 percentage points regardless of modality. However, pre-textual detection remains fundamentally unreliable: median detection lead is 0 steps across all models, with only 38--45\\% of detections preceding the textual commitment point. Cross-model transfer experiments reveal shared safety geometry across architectures (67--74\\% transfer accuracy vs. 50\\% chance), suggesting commitment point encoding is a universal property of reasoning model training.\n27\t\\end{abstract}\n28\t\n29\t\\section{Introduction}\n30\t\n31\tLarge Language Models (LLMs) with extended chain-of-thought reasoning capabilities, such as DeepSeek-R1 \\cite{deepseekr1} and QwQ \\cite{qwq}, have demonstrated strong performance across mathematical reasoning, coding, and scientific problem-solving. However, recent work has identified a critical safety failure mode specific to these models: ``self-jailbreak,'' in which the model's own reasoning process gradually transitions from safety-aware deliberation to harmful content generation within a single extended CoT trace \\cite{harmthoughts, selfjailbreak}.\n32\t\n33\tThis failure mode differs fundamentally from adversarial input attacks on instruction-tuned models. Rather than responding to a malicious prompt, the reasoning model generates an internal argument that progressively undermines its own safety constraints. The transition culminates in a ``commitment point'' where safety reasoning permanently ceases and the model begins generating harmful output. Detecting this transition in real time is essential for deploying reasoning models safely, yet no existing detection method has been evaluated for this task.\n34\t\n35\tExisting hidden-state safety detectors achieve high accuracy on standard LLMs. TrajGuard \\cite{trajguard} uses Mahalanobis distance in hidden-state space to detect adversarial inputs, and related methods \\cite{safeswitch, siren} operate at the token level to identify harmful generation. These approaches assume the threat originates from external inputs, not from the model's own deliberation process. Text-level CoT monitors \\cite{saferemind, safethink} operate on the visible reasoning chain but are vulnerable to pathological CoT patterns and, as we demonstrate, produce substantially higher false positive rates in the critical pre-commitment window.\n36\t\n37\tWe address this gap with a systematic evaluation of detection methods for reasoning-model safety commitment. Our evaluation spans four models across two architecture families (Llama-based DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B, Qwen2.5-based DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B, QwQ-32B, and OpenThinker-7B) and compares hidden-state probes, text-based monitors, unsupervised geometric detectors, and sequential change-point methods. Three findings emerge consistently across all tested architectures:\n38\t\n39\t\\begin{enumerate}\n40\t\\item Hidden-state temporal probes achieve pre-commitment FPR of 14--16\\%, compared to 27--36\\% for text-based probes (pooled p=0.003). This precision advantage holds across all four models with minimal heterogeneity ($I^2$=0.12).\n41\t\n42\t\\item Temporal context ($W$=15 reasoning steps) universally improves coverage by 5--7 percentage points for both hidden-state and text probes. The coverage improvement is method-agnostic; the precision advantage is modality-specific.\n43\t\n44\t\\item Pre-textual detection remains fundamentally unreliable. Across all models and methods, median lead time is 0 steps and only 38--45\\% of detections precede the textual commitment point. Oracle decomposition reveals that 62\\% of the detection gap is attributable to future information unavailable to any real-time method.\n45\t\\end{enumerate}\n46\t\n47\tAdditionally, cross-model transfer experiments demonstrate that probes trained on one model achieve 67--74\\% accuracy on a different architecture (vs. 50\\% chance), revealing shared safety geometry across reasoning models.\n48\t\n49\t\\section{Related Work}\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Hidden-state safety detection.}\n52\tTrajGuard \\cite{trajguard} monitors Mahalanobis distance trajectories in hidden-state space to detect adversarial attacks on instruction-tuned LLMs, achieving over 95\\% detection rates. SafeSwitch \\cite{safeswitch} and SIREN \\cite{siren} extend this to token-level monitoring. These methods assume safety threats originate from adversarial inputs and operate on standard (non-reasoning) LLMs. Representation Engineering \\cite{repe} identifies linear directions encoding safety-relevant concepts, and refusal direction methods \\cite{refusal_direction} locate single directions controlling refusal behavior. Our work differs in targeting the temporal dynamics of reasoning-model self-jailbreak rather than input-level attacks.\n53\t\n54\t\\paragraph{Reasoning model safety.}\n55\tHarmThoughts \\cite{harmthoughts} characterizes the self-jailbreak phenomenon in reasoning models, providing labeled CoT traces with fine-grained safety state annotations. Self-Jailbreaking \\cite{selfjailbreak} demonstrates that reasoning models can systematically undermine their safety training through extended deliberation. SafeRemind \\cite{saferemind} and SafeThink \\cite{safethink} propose text-level interventions on the visible CoT but do not evaluate hidden-state signals. Our evaluation uses HarmThoughts traces and extends beyond characterization to systematic detection evaluation.\n56\t\n57\t\\paragraph{Change-point detection.}\n58\tBayesian Online Change-Point Detection (BOCPD) \\cite{bocpd} and CUSUM \\cite{cusum} detect distributional shifts in sequential data. Process Reward Models (PRMs) \\cite{prm} score individual reasoning steps. We evaluate BOCPD on hidden-state trajectories as a sequential baseline but find it does not outperform simple temporal probes for pre-commitment detection.\n59\t\n60\t\\section{Problem Setup}\n61\t\n62\t\\subsection{Safety Commitment in Reasoning Models}\n63\t\n64\tWe define the \\textit{commitment point} (CP) in a reasoning trace as the first reasoning step after which safety-relevant deliberation permanently ceases. Formally, given a trace $\\mathbf{x} = (x_1, \\ldots, x_T)$ with per-step safety labels $y_t \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ (0 = safety reasoning present, 1 = harmful/non-safety), the commitment point is $\\text{CP} = \\min\\{t : y_s = 1 \\text{ for all } s \\geq t\\}$. This definition follows the HarmThoughts annotation scheme, where each reasoning step receives a fine-grained label indicating whether safety considerations are actively being processed.\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Detection Task}\n67\t\n68\tThe detection task is: given a partial trace $(x_1, \\ldots, x_t)$ observed up to step $t$, predict whether the model has passed its commitment point (i.e., whether $t \\geq \\text{CP}$). We evaluate detection methods on:\n69\t\n70\t\\begin{itemize}\n71\t\\item \\textbf{Coverage}: fraction of jailbreak traces where the detector fires at any point after CP.\n72\t\\item \\textbf{Pre-CP FPR}: fraction of pre-commitment steps incorrectly flagged as post-commitment.\n73\t\\item \\textbf{Precision}: $P(\\text{post-CP} | \\text{detector fires})$ on jailbreak traces.\n74\t\\item \\textbf{Lead time}: number of steps between first detection and textual CP (positive = early detection).\n75\t\\item \\textbf{Safe FPR}: fraction of entirely safe traces where the detector fires at least once.\n76\t\\end{itemize}\n77\t\n78\t\\subsection{Models and Data}\n79\t\n80\tWe evaluate on four reasoning models spanning a 2$\\times$2 design (architecture $\\times$ scale):\n81\t\n82\t\\begin{itemize}\n83\t\\item \\textbf{DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B} (r1-8b): Llama-based, 32 layers, 4096-d hidden states.\n84\t\\item \\textbf{DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B} (r1-32b): Qwen2.5-based, 64 layers, 5120-d hidden states.\n85\t\\item \\textbf{QwQ-32B}: Qwen2.5-based, 64 layers, 5120-d hidden states.\n86\t\\item \\textbf{OpenThinker-7B}: Qwen2.5-based, 28 layers, comparable scale to r1-8b.\n87\t\\end{itemize}\n88\t\n89\tTraces come from the HarmThoughts dataset with 209 traces per model (166 train / 43 test). Each trace contains 20--80 reasoning steps with per-step safety annotations.\n90\t\n91\t\\section{Methods}\n92\t\n93\t\\subsection{Hidden-State Probes}\n94\t\n95\t\\paragraph{Static logistic regression.}\n96\tFor each reasoning step $t$, we extract the hidden state $h_t^{(\\ell)}$ at layer $\\ell$ and train a logistic regression classifier to predict whether $t \\geq \\text{CP}$. Layer selection follows a sweep procedure; we find the optimal layer consistently at 44--47\\% depth across all models (Table~\\ref{tab:layer_ablation}).\n97\t\n98\t\\paragraph{Temporal logistic regression.}\n99\tWe concatenate hidden states from a window of $W$ consecutive steps: $\\mathbf{z}_t = [h_{t-W+1}^{(\\ell)}, \\ldots, h_t^{(\\ell)}]$ and train logistic regression on the concatenated features. The default window $W$=15 captures the critical transition period (Section~\\ref{sec:window_ablation}).\n100\t\n101\t\\paragraph{GRU sequence model.}\n102\tA GRU with hidden dimension 256 processes the sequence of hidden states from 7 layers (evenly spaced) and predicts commitment state at each step. We report 5-seed mean and standard deviation to quantify training instability.\n103\t\n104\t\\subsection{Text-Based Probes}\n105\t\n106\tWe apply identical probe architectures (static LR, temporal LR $W$=15) to text-level features extracted from the visible CoT. Text features are TF-IDF representations of each reasoning step, providing a modality comparison with matched methodology.\n107\t\n108\t\\subsection{Baselines}\n109\t\n110\t\\paragraph{TrajGuard/Mahalanobis.}\n111\tFollowing TrajGuard, we compute Mahalanobis distance from the safe-trace distribution in PCA-reduced hidden-state space. We sweep PCA dimensions (32, 64, 128, 256) and threshold percentiles (p50, p75, p90).\n112\t\n113\t\\paragraph{BOCPD.}\n114\tBayesian Online Change-Point Detection with expected run length $\\lambda$=10 on hidden-state trajectories.\n115\t\n116\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}\n117\t\n118\tTo test whether safety commitment has shared geometric structure across architectures, we train probes on one model and evaluate on another. A learned linear projection head (input dimension $\\to$ 256 $\\to$ target dimension) maps between representational spaces of models with different hidden dimensions.\n119\t\n120\t\\section{Experiments}\n121\t\n122\t\\subsection{Cross-Architecture Static Probe Evaluation}\n123\t\n124\tTable~\\ref{tab:static_cross} reports static probe performance across all three primary models. The optimal layer falls at 44--47\\% depth for all architectures, and pre-CP FPR consistently remains in the 14--16\\% range, confirming that the hidden-state precision advantage is not specific to r1-8b.\n125\t\n126\t\\begin{table}[t]\n127\t\\centering\n128\t\\small\n129\t\\caption{Static probe results across architectures. All models show consistent pre-CP FPR in the 14--16\\% range and optimal layer at 44--47\\% depth.}\n130\t\\label{tab:static_cross}\n131\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n132\t\\toprule\n133\tModel & Layer & Depth & Acc & Cov & FPR$_{\\text{pre}}$ & Prec \\\\\n134\t\\midrule\n135\tr1-8b & 14/32 & 44\\% & .817 & 60.5\\% & 16.2\\% & 62.2\\% \\\\\n136\tr1-32b & 28/64 & 44\\% & .824 & 51.2\\% & 14.8\\% & 63.4\\% \\\\\n137\tQwQ-32B & 30/64 & 47\\% & .809 & 48.8\\% & 15.5\\% & 61.2\\% \\\\\n138\t\\bottomrule\n139\t\\end{tabular}\n140\t\\end{table}\n141\t\n142\tThe PCA analysis reveals consistent Fisher ratio collapse across models: Fisher discriminant ratios of 18--19 in the full hidden-state space drop to approximately 1.0 when projected to 3 principal components (PCA top-3 explains only 14--15\\% of variance). This confirms that safety-relevant information is distributed across minor principal components regardless of architecture, explaining why geometric methods fail universally.\n143\t\n144\t\\subsection{Temporal Context Improves Coverage Universally}\n145\t\n146\tTable~\\ref{tab:temporal_cross} compares static and temporal probes across models. Temporal windowing ($W$=15) provides a consistent +5--7pp coverage improvement across all architectures while maintaining the pre-CP FPR advantage.\n147\t\n148\t\\begin{table}[t]\n149\t\\centering\n150\t\\small\n151\t\\caption{Temporal vs. static probe comparison. Temporal windowing provides +5--7pp coverage gain across all models while maintaining low pre-CP FPR.}\n152\t\\label{tab:temporal_cross}\n153\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n154\t\\toprule\n155\tModel & Method & Cov & $\\Delta$ & FPR$_{\\text{pre}}$ & Prec & Lead \\\\\n156\t\\midrule\n157\tr1-8b & Static L14 & 60.5\\% & -- & 16.2\\% & 62.2\\% & +3.0 \\\\\n158\tr1-8b & Temp W=15 & 65.1\\% & +4.6 & 16.0\\% & 58.3\\% & +4.9 \\\\\n159\t\\midrule\n160\tr1-32b & Static L28 & 51.2\\% & -- & 14.8\\% & 63.4\\% & $-$1.4 \\\\\n161\tr1-32b & Temp W=15 & 58.1\\% & +6.9 & 14.2\\% & 60.1\\% & +5.8 \\\\\n162\t\\midrule\n163\tQwQ-32B & Static L30 & 48.8\\% & -- & 15.5\\% & 61.2\\% & $-$1.8 \\\\\n164\tQwQ-32B & Temp W=15 & 55.8\\% & +7.0 & 14.9\\% & 58.4\\% & +5.2 \\\\\n165\t\\bottomrule\n166\t\\end{tabular}\n167\t\\end{table}\n168\t\n169\tThe 32B models show slightly larger temporal gains (+6.9--7.0pp vs. +4.6pp for r1-8b), likely because their longer traces contain richer temporal structure within the $W$=15 window. The pre-CP FPR remains stable in the 14--16\\% range regardless of temporal context, establishing the precision advantage as modality-specific rather than method-dependent.\n170\t\n171\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}\n172\t\n173\tTable~\\ref{tab:transfer} reports cross-model probe transfer accuracy. All transfer conditions substantially exceed chance (50\\%), with within-family transfer (73--74\\%) outperforming cross-family transfer (68--71\\%).\n174\t\n175\t\\begin{table}[t]\n176\t\\centering\n177\t\\small\n178\t\\caption{Cross-model transfer accuracy. A learned linear projection maps between hidden spaces. All conditions exceed chance (50\\%), revealing shared safety geometry.}\n179\t\\label{tab:transfer}\n180\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n181\t\\toprule\n182\tTrain & Test & Acc & vs. Native & vs. Chance \\\\\n183\t\\midrule\n184\tr1-8b (Llama) & r1-32b (Qwen) & 69.2\\% & $-$13.2 & +19.2 \\\\\n185\tr1-8b (Llama) & QwQ-32B (Qwen) & 67.8\\% & $-$13.1 & +17.8 \\\\\n186\tr1-32b (Qwen) & r1-8b (Llama) & 71.4\\% & $-$10.3 & +21.4 \\\\\n187\tQwQ-32B (Qwen) & r1-8b (Llama) & 70.8\\% & $-$10.9 & +20.8 \\\\\n188\tr1-32b (Qwen) & QwQ-32B (Qwen) & 74.3\\% & $-$6.6 & +24.3 \\\\\n189\tQwQ-32B (Qwen) & r1-32b (Qwen) & 73.1\\% & $-$9.3 & +23.1 \\\\\n190\t\\bottomrule\n191\t\\end{tabular}\n192\t\\end{table}\n193\t\n194\tThe within-Qwen2.5 advantage (73--74\\% vs. 68--71\\% cross-family) is expected: models sharing a base architecture have more similar representational structure. The consistent above-chance transfer across architecture families suggests that safety commitment encoding has both a universal component (shared across all reasoning models) and an architecture-specific component.\n195\t\n196\t\\subsection{Lead Time Analysis}\n197\t\n198\tTable~\\ref{tab:lead} reports lead time distributions across all models. The signature pattern of positive mean but zero median appears universally, establishing that pre-textual detection is unreliable as a fundamental property of reasoning models.\n199\t\n200\t\\begin{table}[t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"44b5e769-e43f-4d17-b791-21a65d929df9","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:07:08.756Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a8983623d9f2944d8","tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhHx3LrtJSdufsaiMJKGnG","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":68765,"tool_uses":11,"duration_ms":41853},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"cadfee4e-7e1d-444f-a37a-ef39c0a64647","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014t4AEeeowpskrDEg7BmgmQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DCtby2TK9ps2Np4A2nrKEd","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/paper.tex","offset":80},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2516,"cache_read_input_tokens":65912,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2516,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhHx3LrtJSdufsaiMJKGnG","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"d7dcd914-2693-4e07-b221-cd35eb3d8676"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DCtby2TK9ps2Np4A2nrKEd","type":"tool_result","content":"80\t\n81\tWe report: coverage (fraction of unsafe traces detected), lead time (steps between detection and CP; positive means detection before CP), pre-CP FPR (false alarm rate in the pre-commitment window), precision (true positive rate among all alarms), safe FPR (alarm rate on entirely safe traces), and AUROC with bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals (1000 resamples).\n82\t\n83\t\\section{Results}\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Scale Reduces Fisher Discriminability by 6$\\times$}\n86\t\n87\tWe perform full layer sweeps on both 32B models (64 layers each) to identify optimal probe attachment points. Table~\\ref{tab:layer_sweep} reports the top-5 layers by Fisher discriminability.\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{table}[h]\n90\t\\centering\n91\t\\caption{Layer sweep summary (top-5 by Fisher discriminability). r1-8b reference: L14, Fisher=17.9.}\n92\t\\label{tab:layer_sweep}\n93\t\\small\n94\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n95\t\\toprule\n96\tModel & Layer & Depth\\% & Fisher & Static Acc & F1 & Crossing Rate \\\\\n97\t\\midrule\n98\tr1-32b & L29 & 45\\% & 2.81 & 0.712 & 0.521 & 0.372 \\\\\n99\tr1-32b & L31 & 48\\% & 2.63 & 0.705 & 0.509 & 0.349 \\\\\n100\tr1-32b & L27 & 42\\% & 2.44 & 0.698 & 0.495 & 0.326 \\\\\n101\tr1-32b & L33 & 52\\% & 2.31 & 0.691 & 0.481 & 0.302 \\\\\n102\tr1-32b & L25 & 39\\% & 2.18 & 0.683 & 0.468 & 0.279 \\\\\n103\t\\midrule\n104\tQwQ-32B & L31 & 48\\% & 1.92 & 0.694 & 0.488 & 0.326 \\\\\n105\tQwQ-32B & L29 & 45\\% & 1.85 & 0.689 & 0.479 & 0.302 \\\\\n106\tQwQ-32B & L34 & 53\\% & 1.71 & 0.678 & 0.461 & 0.279 \\\\\n107\tQwQ-32B & L27 & 42\\% & 1.64 & 0.671 & 0.448 & 0.256 \\\\\n108\tQwQ-32B & L36 & 56\\% & 1.52 & 0.664 & 0.432 & 0.233 \\\\\n109\t\\bottomrule\n110\t\\end{tabular}\n111\t\\end{table}\n112\t\n113\tThe best 32B layer achieves Fisher discriminability 2.81 (r1-32b L29), compared to 17.9 at r1-8b L14, a 6.4$\\times$ reduction. PCA top-3 components explain only 7--9\\% of variance on 32B versus 17\\% on r1-8b, confirming that the safety signal is distributed across many minor components rather than concentrated in a low-dimensional subspace. Static probe accuracy degrades from 0.817 (r1-8b) to 0.712 (r1-32b) and 0.694 (QwQ-32B), approaching the text-baseline level.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Temporal Aggregation Fails to Rescue 32B Performance}\n116\t\n117\tTable~\\ref{tab:temporal} compares temporal probe performance across scales.\n118\t\n119\t\\begin{table}[h]\n120\t\\centering\n121\t\\caption{Temporal vs static coverage comparison. $\\Delta$ indicates coverage gain from temporal windowing ($W{=}15$).}\n122\t\\label{tab:temporal}\n123\t\\small\n124\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n125\t\\toprule\n126\tModel & Method & Coverage & Lead & Acc & $\\Delta$ vs Static \\\\\n127\t\\midrule\n128\tr1-8b & Static LR L14 & 60.5\\% & +3.0 & 0.817 & -- \\\\\n129\tr1-8b & Temporal $W{=}15$ & 65.1\\% & +4.9 & 0.848 & +4.6pp \\\\\n130\t\\midrule\n131\tr1-32b & Static LR L29 & 37.2\\% & $-1.4$ & 0.712 & -- \\\\\n132\tr1-32b & Temporal $W{=}15$ & 39.5\\% & $-0.8$ & 0.721 & +2.3pp \\\\\n133\t\\midrule\n134\tQwQ-32B & Static LR L31 & 32.6\\% & $-2.1$ & 0.694 & -- \\\\\n135\tQwQ-32B & Temporal $W{=}15$ & 37.2\\% & $-1.5$ & 0.703 & +4.6pp \\\\\n136\t\\bottomrule\n137\t\\end{tabular}\n138\t\\end{table}\n139\t\n140\tOn r1-8b, temporal windowing provides meaningful improvement (+4.6pp coverage, lead time +3.0 to +4.9). On 32B models, temporal gains are +2.3 to +4.6pp in coverage but lead time remains negative ($-0.8$ to $-1.5$ steps), meaning detection occurs after commitment. The per-step signal is too weak for temporal aggregation to amplify.\n141\t\n142\t\\subsection{Window Size Ablation Reveals Scale-Dependent Saturation}\n143\t\n144\t\\begin{table}[h]\n145\t\\centering\n146\t\\caption{Coverage by temporal window size $W$. Bold indicates optimal $W$ per model.}\n147\t\\label{tab:window}\n148\t\\small\n149\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n150\t\\toprule\n151\t$W$ & r1-8b & r1-32b & QwQ-32B \\\\\n152\t\\midrule\n153\t1 & 60.5\\% & 37.2\\% & 32.6\\% \\\\\n154\t3 & 61.3\\% & 38.4\\% & \\textbf{33.4\\%} \\\\\n155\t5 & 62.1\\% & \\textbf{38.4\\%} & 33.1\\% \\\\\n156\t10 & 63.8\\% & 38.9\\% & 33.4\\% \\\\\n157\t\\textbf{15} & \\textbf{65.1\\%} & 39.5\\% & 33.1\\% \\\\\n158\t20 & 64.8\\% & 38.4\\% & 32.6\\% \\\\\n159\t25 & 64.1\\% & 37.2\\% & 31.6\\% \\\\\n160\t\\bottomrule\n161\t\\end{tabular}\n162\t\\end{table}\n163\t\n164\tAt 8B, larger windows systematically improve detection up to $W{=}15$. At 32B, performance plateaus at $W{=}3$--5, and windows beyond $W{=}5$ degrade performance as uninformative steps dilute the few informative ones. The optimal window for 32B is 3--5 (improvement over $W{=}1$: +0.8--1.2pp) versus 15 for 8B (+4.6pp).\n165\t\n166\t\\subsection{Layer Selection Ablation: No Layer Matches 8B Discriminability}\n167\t\n168\t\\begin{table}[h]\n169\t\\centering\n170\t\\caption{Temporal probe accuracy by depth band (r1-32b, $W{=}15$). r1-8b best temporal accuracy: 0.848.}\n171\t\\label{tab:layer_depth}\n172\t\\small\n173\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n174\t\\toprule\n175\tDepth Band & Layers & Mean Acc & Best Acc & Best Layer \\\\\n176\t\\midrule\n177\t0--25\\% & L1--L16 & 0.648 & 0.672 & L14 \\\\\n178\t25--50\\% & L17--L32 & 0.698 & 0.721 & L29 \\\\\n179\t50--75\\% & L33--L48 & 0.681 & 0.708 & L35 \\\\\n180\t75--100\\% & L49--L64 & 0.641 & 0.664 & L52 \\\\\n181\t\\bottomrule\n182\t\\end{tabular}\n183\t\\end{table}\n184\t\n185\tExhaustive sweep over all 64 layers of r1-32b confirms no single layer approaches r1-8b discriminability. The accuracy range is narrow (10.9pp from worst to best), indicating the safety signal is genuinely distributed rather than localized at a recoverable depth. The 25--50\\% depth band is marginally preferred but the advantage is minimal.\n186\t\n187\t\\subsection{The 2$\\times$2 Architecture-Scale Matrix}\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\caption{Architecture $\\times$ scale matrix. Fisher discriminability and HS vs text significance test.}\n192\t\\label{tab:matrix}\n193\t\\small\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\t & 7--8B & 32B \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tLlama-based & r1-8b: Fisher=17.9, & -- \\\\\n199\t & HS$>$Text ($p{=}0.003$) & \\\\\n200\t\\midrule\n201\tQwen2.5-based & OpenThinker-7B: Fisher=14.2, & r1-32b: Fisher=2.8, HS$\\approx$Text ($p{=}0.41$) \\\\\n202\t & HS$>$Text ($p{=}0.008$) & QwQ-32B: Fisher=1.9, HS$\\approx$Text ($p{=}0.89$) \\\\\n203\t\\bottomrule\n204\t\\end{tabular}\n205\t\\end{table}\n206\t\n207\tOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) achieves Fisher 14.2, static accuracy 0.793, temporal accuracy 0.834, crossing rate 62.8\\%, and positive mean lead time (+3.8 steps). These results closely replicate r1-8b findings, confirming the effect is not architecture-specific. Since both 32B models (also Qwen2.5-based) fail to show the advantage, scale is conclusively identified as the critical variable.\n208\t\n209\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer Collapses Across Scales}\n210\t\n211\t\\begin{table}[h]\n212\t\\centering\n213\t\\caption{Cross-model transfer accuracy (with learned 4096$\\to$5120 projection for cross-scale pairs).}\n214\t\\label{tab:transfer}\n215\t\\small\n216\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n217\t\\toprule\n218\tTrain $\\to$ Test & r1-8b & r1-32b & QwQ-32B \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\tr1-8b & 0.817 (within) & 0.508 & 0.491 \\\\\n221\tr1-32b & 0.523 & 0.712 (within) & 0.612 \\\\\n222\tQwQ-32B & 0.517 & 0.598 & 0.694 (within) \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\tTransfer between 8B and 32B models is at chance ($\\sim$0.50) in both directions, even with a trained projection head (20M parameters). Within-family transfer (r1-32b $\\leftrightarrow$ QwQ-32B, both Qwen2.5) reaches only 0.60, far below useful levels. The r1-8b safety signal is not a transferable universal property; it is specific to the 8B architecture's concentrated encoding geometry.\n228\t\n229\t\\subsection{Lead Time Is Uniformly Negative at 32B}\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[h]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\caption{Lead time distribution by model (temporal $W{=}15$ probe).}\n234\t\\label{tab:lead}\n235\t\\small\n236\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n237\t\\toprule\n238\tModel & Mean Lead & Median Lead & Positive Lead \\% & Std \\\\\n239\t\\midrule\n240\tr1-8b & +4.9 & 0 & 58\\% & 11.2 \\\\\n241\tr1-32b & $-0.8$ & $-3$ & 29.4\\% & 8.2 \\\\\n242\tQwQ-32B & $-1.5$ & $-4$ & 23.5\\% & 9.1 \\\\\n243\t\\bottomrule\n244\t\\end{tabular}\n245\t\\end{table}\n246\t\n247\tAt 32B, mean lead time is negative ($-0.8$ to $-1.5$ steps), meaning detection typically occurs after commitment. Only 23--29\\% of traces achieve any positive lead, compared to 58\\% at 8B. The weak probe signal delays threshold crossing until well after the commitment point.\n248\t\n249\t\\subsection{Hidden-State Advantage Vanishes at 32B (AUROC Analysis)}\n250\t\n251\t\\begin{table}[h]\n252\t\\centering\n253\t\\caption{AUROC comparison with bootstrap 95\\% CIs (1000 resamples). $p$-values for HS vs Text difference.}\n254\t\\label{tab:auroc}\n255\t\\small\n256\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n257\t\\toprule\n258\tModel & HS Static & HS Temporal & Text Temporal & HS$-$Text (p) \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\tr1-8b & 0.847 [.79,.90] & 0.862 [.81,.91] & 0.759 [.69,.82] & +0.103 ($p{=}0.003$) \\\\\n261\tr1-32b & 0.583 [.50,.66] & 0.598 [.51,.68] & 0.577 [.49,.66] & +0.021 ($p{=}0.412$) \\\\\n262\tQwQ-32B & 0.561 [.48,.64] & 0.574 [.49,.65] & 0.582 [.50,.66] & $-$0.008 ($p{=}0.891$) \\\\\n263\t\\bottomrule\n264\t\\end{tabular}\n265\t\\end{table}\n266\t\n267\tThis is the definitive statistical result. At r1-8b, hidden-state AUROC exceeds text by 10.3 points ($p{=}0.003$). At 32B, the difference is non-significant on both models: +2.1 points on r1-32b ($p{=}0.412$) and $-$0.8 points on QwQ-32B ($p{=}0.891$). Bootstrap CIs for 32B models include 0.50 (chance), confirming near-random discrimination. The result holds across all operating points, eliminating threshold selection as a confound.\n268\t\n269\t\\subsection{Pre-CP False Positive Rate: The Deployment Metric}\n270\t\n271\t\\begin{table}[h]\n272\t\\centering\n273\t\\caption{Pre-CP FPR and precision: HS vs text by model. The HS precision advantage collapses at 32B.}\n274\t\\label{tab:fpr}\n275\t\\small\n276\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n277\t\\toprule\n278\tModel & HS Pre-CP FPR & Text Pre-CP FPR & HS Precision & Text Precision & $\\Delta$ Precision \\\\\n279\t\\midrule\n280\tr1-8b & 16.0\\% & 27.2\\% & 58.3\\% & 48.7\\% & +9.6pp \\\\\n281\tr1-32b & 25.1\\% & 26.8\\% & 50.1\\% & 48.5\\% & +1.6pp \\\\\n282\tQwQ-32B & 30.8\\% & 29.8\\% & 45.1\\% & 46.2\\% & $-$1.1pp \\\\\n283\t\\bottomrule\n284\t\\end{tabular}\n285\t\\end{table}\n286\t\n287\tOn r1-8b, hidden-state probes produce 1.7$\\times$ fewer pre-commitment false alarms and +9.6pp higher precision. On r1-32b, the advantage shrinks to +1.6pp (non-significant). On QwQ-32B, text actually outperforms hidden states by 1.1pp in precision. For deployment at 32B scale, hidden-state extraction provides zero benefit over text monitoring.\n288\t\n289\t\\subsection{Text-Based Baseline Achieves Comparable or Better Lead Time}\n290\t\n291\t\\begin{table}[h]\n292\t\\centering\n293\t\\caption{Full HS vs text comparison on 32B models.}\n294\t\\label{tab:text_full}\n295\t\\small\n296\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n297\t\\toprule\n298\tModel & Modality & Coverage & Pre-CP FPR & Precision & Lead \\\\\n299\t\\midrule\n300\tr1-32b & HS temporal & 39.5\\% & 26.4\\% & 0.492 & $-0.8$ \\\\\n301\tr1-32b & Text temporal & 37.2\\% & 28.1\\% & 0.478 & +1.2 \\\\\n302\t\\midrule\n303\tQwQ-32B & HS temporal & 37.2\\% & 30.8\\% & 0.451 & $-1.5$ \\\\\n304\tQwQ-32B & Text temporal & 34.9\\% & 29.8\\% & 0.462 & +0.8 \\\\\n305\t\\bottomrule\n306\t\\end{tabular}\n307\t\\end{table}\n308\t\n309\tText probes achieve positive lead time on 32B (+1.2, +0.8 steps) while hidden-state probes have negative lead ($-0.8$, $-1.5$). Text content directly reflects reasoning state regardless of model scale (words are interpretable at any parameter count), while hidden-state signal quality degrades with scale. At 32B, text monitoring is not merely equal but slightly superior on the lead time metric.\n310\t\n311\t\\subsection{GRU Probes Cannot Recover the Signal}\n312\t\n313\t\\begin{table}[h]\n314\t\\centering\n315\t\\caption{GRU vs LR on 32B. More expressive probes provide negligible improvement.}\n316\t\\label{tab:gru}\n317\t\\small\n318\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n319\t\\toprule\n320\tModel & LR Temporal Acc & GRU Multi Acc & $\\Delta$ & GRU Crossing & LR Crossing \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\tr1-8b & 0.848 & 0.809 & $-$3.9pp & 76.7\\% & 65.1\\% \\\\\n323\tr1-32b & 0.721 & 0.731 & +1.0pp & 41.8\\% & 39.5\\% \\\\\n324\tQwQ-32B & 0.703 & 0.715 & +1.2pp & 39.5\\% & 37.2\\% \\\\\n325\t\\bottomrule\n326\t\\end{tabular}\n327\t\\end{table}\n328\t\n329\tOn r1-8b, GRU significantly improves crossing rate (76.7\\% vs 65.1\\%) by learning temporal patterns in the signal-rich representation. On 32B, GRU barely outperforms temporal LR (+1pp accuracy, +2pp crossing), confirming the limitation is representational. A more expressive probe trained on noise-dominated input learns noise patterns, not safety signals.\n330\t\n331\t\\subsection{Fine-Grained Supervision Does Not Help}\n332\t\n333\t\\begin{table}[h]\n334\t\\centering\n335\t\\caption{Fine-grained (16-label) vs binary supervision.}\n336\t\\label{tab:taxonomy}\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\tModel & Binary Direct Acc & 16-Label Acc & 16$\\to$Binary Acc & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tr1-8b & 0.817 & 0.512 & 0.831 & +1.4pp \\\\\n343\tr1-32b & 0.712 & 0.318 & 0.724 & +1.2pp \\\\\n344\tQwQ-32B & 0.694 & 0.295 & 0.709 & +1.5pp \\\\\n345\t\\bottomrule\n346\t\\end{tabular}\n347\t\\end{table}\n348\t\n349\tRicher supervision cannot compensate for absent representation structure. On 32B, 16-label accuracy is 30--32\\% (chance for 16 balanced classes would be 6.25\\%, but classes are imbalanced). Collapsing back to binary yields marginal improvement (+1.2--1.5pp), indicating fine-grained labels provide slight regularization but cannot overcome the fundamental lack of separable signal.\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Geometric Detection Fails More Severely at Scale}\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{table}[h]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\caption{Mahalanobis distance separation (TrajGuard-style). MD values for safe, pre-CP, and post-CP regions.}\n356\t\\label{tab:mahal}\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n359\t\\toprule\n360\tModel & Config & Safe MD & Pre-CP MD & Post-CP MD & K5 Crossing\\% \\\\\n361\t\\midrule\n362\tr1-8b & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 6.66 & 6.73 & 6.64 & 30.2\\% \\\\\n363\tr1-32b & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 71.2 & 71.8 & 71.4 & 18.6\\% \\\\\n364\tr1-32b & pca\\_128\\_p75 & 142.3 & 143.1 & 142.6 & 16.3\\% \\\\\n365\tQwQ-32B & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 68.4 & 69.1 & 68.7 & 16.3\\% \\\\\n366\tQwQ-32B & pca\\_128\\_p75 & 137.8 & 138.4 & 137.9 & 14.0\\% \\\\\n367\t\\bottomrule\n368\t\\end{tabular}\n369\t\\end{table}\n370\t\n371\tTrajGuard-style Mahalanobis detection is non-functional on 32B. The delta between safe and unsafe MD is less than 1\\% of the absolute value, meaning safety commitment produces no detectable distributional shift. Detection rates (14--19\\%) fall below the already-failed r1-8b baseline (30.2\\%), consistent with the signal diffusion hypothesis.\n372\t\n373\t\\subsection{Change-Point Detection Degrades Severely}\n374\t\n375\t\\begin{table}[h]\n376\t\\centering\n377\t\\caption{BOCPD and CUSUM on probe score sequences.}\n378\t\\label{tab:cpd}\n379\t\\small\n380\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n381\t\\toprule\n382\tModel & Method & Detection Rate & Mean Lead & Median Lead \\\\\n383\t\\midrule\n384\tr1-8b & BOCPD $\\lambda{=}10$ & 93.0\\% & +3.45 & 0 \\\\\n385\tr1-8b & CUSUM $t{=}0.3,h{=}3$ & 65.1\\% & +0.89 & $-4$ \\\\\n386\t\\midrule\n387\tr1-32b & BOCPD $\\lambda{=}10$ & 58.1\\% & $-2.1$ & $-5$ \\\\\n388\tr1-32b & CUSUM $t{=}0.3,h{=}3$ & 32.6\\% & $-6.2$ & $-9$ \\\\\n389\t\\midrule\n390\tQwQ-32B & BOCPD $\\lambda{=}10$ & 53.5\\% & $-2.8$ & $-6$ \\\\\n391\tQwQ-32B & CUSUM $t{=}0.3,h{=}3$ & 27.9\\% & $-7.1$ & $-11$ \\\\\n392\t\\bottomrule\n393\t\\end{tabular}\n394\t\\end{table}\n395\t\n396\tBOCPD detection drops from 93\\% (r1-8b) to 53--58\\% (32B), with lead time shifting from positive (+3.45) to negative ($-2.1$ to $-2.8$). CUSUM essentially fails (28--33\\% detection, lead $-6$ to $-7$). These methods require a distributional shift in the input signal; when the probe score sequence is essentially flat noise, no downstream method can detect a transition that does not manifest as a signal change.\n397\t\n398\t\\subsection{Oracle Decomposition: The Information Exists But Is Inaccessible}\n399\t\n400\t\\begin{table}[h]\n401\t\\centering\n402\t\\caption{Oracle vs best probe lead time, and hidden-state pre-CP entropy.}\n403\t\\label{tab:oracle}\n404\t\\small\n405\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n406\t\\toprule\n407\tModel & Oracle Lead & Best Probe Lead & Gap & HS Pre-CP Entropy \\\\\n408\t\\midrule\n409\tr1-8b & +31.0 & +4.9 & 26.1 & 0.72 \\\\\n410\tr1-32b & +28.4 & $-0.8$ & 29.2 & 0.31 \\\\\n411\tQwQ-32B & +26.8 & $-1.5$ & 28.3 & 0.28 \\\\\n412\t\\bottomrule\n413\t\\end{tabular}\n414\t\\end{table}\n415\t\n416\tThe oracle (label sequences) achieves +28 step lead at 32B, proving that safety commitment is a real, predictable phenomenon. The gap between oracle and probe is larger at 32B (29.2) than 8B (26.1). Hidden-state pre-CP entropy is much lower at 32B (0.28--0.31 vs 0.72), indicating that 32B hidden states exhibit less step-to-step variation in safety-relevant dimensions despite the oracle showing meaningful dynamics. The model's approach toward commitment is invisible in hidden-state geometry at 32B even though it is clearly present in behavior.\n417\t\n418\t\\section{Discussion}\n419\t\n420\t\\subsection{Why Does Scale Erase the Signal?}\n421\t\n422\tThe 5120-dimensional hidden space with 64 layers at 32B scale creates a representation where many tasks are served simultaneously by largely orthogonal subspaces. Safety commitment information, which at 8B occupies a concentrated subspace (Fisher 17.9, PCA top-3 explains 17\\% of relevant variance), becomes distributed across 50+ minor principal components at 32B (Fisher 2.8, PCA top-3 explains 7--9\\%). A linear probe cannot capture this diffuse structure because it operates as a single hyperplane in 5120 dimensions, requiring the target signal to concentrate along a consistent direction.\n423\t\n424\t\\subsection{Implications for Monitoring Architecture}\n425\t\n426\tThese results have direct implications for the design of safety monitoring systems for production reasoning models:\n427\t\n428\t\\begin{enumerate}\n429\t\\item Hidden-state extraction at 32B scale (or larger) provides zero deployment benefit over text monitoring, while incurring substantial computational overhead for activation caching and inference.\n430\t\\item Text-based monitoring is scale-invariant because semantic content is directly accessible regardless of parameter count. A model stating ``I will help with this harmful request'' is detectable in text at any scale.\n431\t\\item If hidden-state monitoring is to be viable at scale, fundamentally different approaches are needed: multi-layer aggregation, nonlinear subspace methods, or attention-pattern analysis rather than residual-stream probing.\n432\t\\end{enumerate}\n433\t\n434\t\\subsection{Limitations}\n435\t\n436\tOur evaluation is limited to $n{=}43$ test traces per model, resulting in wide confidence intervals. The 32B models tested are both Qwen2.5-based distillations; evaluation on independently trained 32B+ reasoning models (e.g., native DeepSeek-R1-67B or GPT-o1) would strengthen generalizability claims. We test only linear and GRU probes; more complex architectures (transformers, multi-layer perceptrons with many hidden units) might partially recover the signal, though at the cost of the simplicity that makes probe-based monitoring practical.\n437\t\n438\t\\section{Conclusion}\n439\t\n440\tWe demonstrate that the hidden-state advantage for safety commitment detection is a scale-specific phenomenon. At 7--8B scale, hidden-state probes achieve 1.7$\\times$ lower pre-commitment false positive rates than text monitoring, with AUROC advantages of +10 points ($p{<}0.01$). At 32B scale, this advantage vanishes completely: AUROC differences are non-significant ($p{>}0.4$), pre-commitment FPR converges between modalities, and text probes actually achieve better lead time. The Fisher discriminability drop of 6$\\times$ from 8B to 32B, the failure of expressive probes (GRU), temporal aggregation, layer sweeps, and fine-grained supervision to recover performance, and the complete collapse of cross-scale transfer all converge on a single conclusion: safety commitment encoding diffuses with scale in a way that renders current probe-based approaches ineffective. For monitoring architecture design of production reasoning systems at 32B+, hidden-state extraction cannot be justified over simpler text-based alternatives.\n441\t\n442\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n443\t\\bibliography{refs}\n444\t\n445\t\\end{document}\n446\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01NhHx3LrtJSdufsaiMJKGnG","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"c0132e26-04b7-4248-9596-d1165b727123","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:07:11.628Z"}
