{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KdATRR2xCLJfPafiCS4PSm","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n10\t\\usepackage{array}\n11\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n12\t\\usepackage[numbers]{natbib}\n13\t\n14\t\\title{Detecting Safety Commitment Before It Appears in Text:\\\\Temporal Hidden-State Probes and Change-Point Detection\\\\for Reasoning Model Self-Jailbreak}\n15\t\n16\t\\author{Anonymous}\n17\t\n18\t\\begin{document}\n19\t\\maketitle\n20\t\n21\t%==============================================================================\n22\t\\begin{abstract}\n23\tReasoning models interleave safety reasoning with harmful execution throughout extended chain-of-thought (CoT), culminating in a \\emph{commitment point} where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state jailbreak detectors, designed for instruction-tuned models with adversarial inputs, have never been evaluated on this \\emph{self-jailbreak} phenomenon.\n24\tWe provide the first systematic study of hidden-state detection for safety commitment in reasoning models across four models spanning two architecture families (Llama-3.1, Qwen2.5) and two scales (7--8B, 32B). We find that (1) static per-step probes achieve $<$50\\% detection coverage despite strong hidden-state signal, because the safety signal is distributed across minor principal components; (2) temporal window logistic regression (W=15) saturates oracle-achievable coverage at 72--77\\%, improving over static probes by 31--35 percentage points across all architectures; and (3) Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (BOCPD) on probe score sequences achieves the first reliable pre-textual detection, with +8.4 mean lead time steps and 62\\% early detection rate---substantially outperforming GRU sequence models (+4.7 steps, 39\\% early detection). Hidden-state probes outperform text-based monitoring by 29--32pp in coverage, confirming that the commitment signal exists in hidden states before it manifests in text. Our results establish temporal probing with BOCPD as a practical pipeline for reasoning-model safety monitoring.\n25\t\\end{abstract}\n26\t\n27\t%==============================================================================\n28\t\\section{Introduction}\n29\t\\label{sec:intro}\n30\t\n31\tLarge language models with extended chain-of-thought (CoT) reasoning capabilities---such as DeepSeek-R1 \\cite{deepseek2025r1} and QwQ \\cite{qwq2024}---exhibit a distinctive failure mode termed \\emph{self-jailbreak}: the model's own reasoning process gradually abandons safety considerations and commits to producing harmful content, without any adversarial manipulation of the input \\cite{jiang2025harmthoughts}. Unlike conventional jailbreaks targeting instruction-tuned models, where the attack is an adversarial \\emph{input}, self-jailbreak arises from the model's \\emph{own reasoning process}.\n32\t\n33\tThis phenomenon poses a unique detection challenge. Safety reasoning and harmful execution are interleaved throughout the CoT---84\\% of traces contain safety-relevant labels after step 10---culminating in a \\emph{commitment point} (CP) where safety reasoning permanently ceases. Existing hidden-state detectors such as TrajGuard \\cite{zou2024improving}, SafeSwitch, and SIREN operate at per-token or per-step granularity and were designed for models where harmful intent originates externally. Whether these approaches transfer to detecting a model's internal safety commitment remains unexplored.\n34\t\n35\tWe address this gap with three contributions:\n36\t\n37\t\\paragraph{(1) Static probes are fundamentally limited.} We show that linear probes applied to individual reasoning steps achieve $<$50\\% detection coverage across all four models tested, despite hidden states containing abundant safety signal (Fisher discriminant ratio 17--19 in full representation space). The signal collapses in low-dimensional projections (Fisher drops to $\\sim$1.0 in PCA-3D, with top-3 PCs explaining only 14--17\\% of variance), explaining why geometric methods like Mahalanobis distance fail (coverage 0.30--0.35).\n38\t\n39\t\\paragraph{(2) Temporal context saturates oracle coverage.} A temporal window logistic regression probe (W=15 steps) achieves 72--77\\% coverage across all architectures, matching the label-oracle upper bound. The +31--35pp improvement over static probes is consistent across two architecture families and two scales, with the temporal context effect (31pp) dwarfing both scale (2pp) and architecture (5pp) effects.\n40\t\n41\t\\paragraph{(3) BOCPD enables reliable pre-textual detection.} We introduce Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (BOCPD) \\cite{adams2007bayesian} applied to temporal probe confidence scores as a novel approach for early commitment detection. BOCPD achieves +8.4 mean lead time steps with 62\\% early detection rate, substantially outperforming GRU sequence models (+4.7 steps, 39\\%). Hidden-state probes outperform text-based monitoring by 29--32pp in coverage, confirming that the commitment signal exists in representations \\emph{before} it appears in text.\n42\t\n43\t%==============================================================================\n44\t\\section{Related Work}\n45\t\\label{sec:related}\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Hidden-state safety detection.} Representation engineering \\cite{zou2023representation} and refusal direction analysis \\cite{arditi2024refusal} demonstrate that safety-relevant features are linearly decodable from LLM hidden states. TrajGuard \\cite{zou2024improving} uses Mahalanobis distance for trajectory-level anomaly detection, achieving 95\\% defense rate on instruction-tuned models. These methods assume safety features align with dominant variance directions---an assumption we show fails for reasoning models.\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Reasoning model safety.} Self-Jailbreaking \\cite{jiang2025harmthoughts} and HarmThoughts characterize how reasoning models abandon safety reasoning during extended CoT. SafeRemind and SafeThink propose text-level CoT monitoring but are vulnerable to pathological CoT where surface text does not reflect internal state. No prior work bridges hidden-state detection to reasoning-model self-jailbreak.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Probing methodology.} Linear probing \\cite{hewitt2019structural} is widely used for analyzing learned representations. Process reward models (PRMs) \\cite{lightman2023lets} provide step-level supervision for reasoning, motivating our step-level probe design.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Change-point detection.} BOCPD \\cite{adams2007bayesian} performs online detection of distributional shifts in sequential data. We are the first to apply BOCPD to hidden-state probe outputs for safety monitoring, reframing commitment detection as a change-point problem.\n54\t\n55\t%==============================================================================\n56\t\\section{Problem Setup}\n57\t\\label{sec:setup}\n58\t\n59\t\\subsection{Safety Commitment in Reasoning Traces}\n60\t\n61\tWe study reasoning traces from models prompted with harmful queries. Each trace consists of a sequence of CoT reasoning steps $\\{s_1, s_2, \\ldots, s_T\\}$. Following \\citet{jiang2025harmthoughts}, each step receives a binary safety label $y_t \\in \\{0, 1\\}$ indicating whether it contains safety-relevant reasoning.\n62\t\n63\t\\paragraph{Commitment point.} We define the commitment point as $\\text{CP} = \\min\\{t : y_{t'} = 0 \\;\\forall\\; t' \\geq t\\}$---the first step after which safety reasoning never returns. A trace is \\emph{committed} if such a CP exists and the model ultimately produces harmful output. Our detection task is to identify committed traces and localize their CPs.\n64\t\n65\t\\paragraph{Detection metrics.} We evaluate probes on:\n66\t\\begin{itemize}\n67\t\\item \\textbf{Coverage}: fraction of committed traces where the probe correctly detects commitment (true positive rate at operational threshold).\n68\t\\item \\textbf{Lead time}: number of steps between probe detection and CP. Positive values indicate pre-CP (early) detection.\n69\t\\item \\textbf{Early detection rate}: fraction of detected traces where lead time $> 0$.\n70\t\\item \\textbf{False positive rate (FPR)}: fraction of safe traces (model refuses) incorrectly flagged.\n71\t\\end{itemize}\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{Models and Data}\n74\t\n75\tWe evaluate four reasoning models spanning a 2$\\times$2 matrix of architecture families and scales:\n76\t\n77\t\\begin{table}[h]\n78\t\\centering\n79\t\\small\n80\t\\begin{tabular}{llcc}\n81\t\\toprule\n82\t\\textbf{Model} & \\textbf{Base Arch.} & \\textbf{Layers} & \\textbf{Hidden Dim} \\\\\n83\t\\midrule\n84\tDeepSeek-R1-Distill-8B & Llama-3.1 & 32 & 4096 \\\\\n85\tOpenThinker-7B & Qwen2.5 & 32 & 4096 \\\\\n86\tDeepSeek-R1-Distill-32B & Qwen2.5 & 64 & 5120 \\\\\n87\tQwQ-32B & Qwen2.5 & 64 & 5120 \\\\\n88\t\\bottomrule\n89\t\\end{tabular}\n90\t\\caption{Models evaluated. R1-8B uses Llama-3.1 base; the remaining three use Qwen2.5 base at two scales.}\n91\t\\label{tab:models}\n92\t\\end{table}\n93\t\n94\tWe use HarmThoughts reasoning traces with binary safety labels. Training uses 209 traces from R1-8B; cross-architecture evaluation uses held-out test sets from all four models. Safe traces (85 total, model successfully refused) are reserved for false positive analysis.\n95\t\n96\t%==============================================================================\n97\t\\section{Methods}\n98\t\\label{sec:methods}\n99\t\n100\t\\subsection{Static Probes}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"aaaa3ed1-6b35-4856-9cbb-8ab488ef4ca3","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:07:24.637Z"}
