{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01SJgpWaYx6d8zZkCpmyZXWK","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WGyHEgE2uWd2BYjRcT4h7w","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/paper.tex","offset":50,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1747,"cache_read_input_tokens":37394,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1747,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":73,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"e7dd1760-5300-448a-920f-688721f1631e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WGyHEgE2uWd2BYjRcT4h7w","type":"tool_result","content":"50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Representation probing and scale.} Representation Engineering (RepE) demonstrates that safety-relevant directions exist in model hidden states. The refusal direction literature shows linear probes can identify refusal-related features. However, these studies typically evaluate on a single model scale. The question of whether probed features persist or diffuse with scale has received limited attention.\n52\t\n53\t\\paragraph{Change-point detection.} Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (BOCPD) and CUSUM have been applied to sequential monitoring problems. Their application to reasoning trace analysis requires sufficient signal-to-noise ratio in the monitored quantity; whether this condition holds across scales is an empirical question we address.\n54\t\n55\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n56\t\n57\t\\subsection{Models}\n58\t\n59\tWe evaluate four reasoning models spanning a 2$\\times$2 architecture-scale matrix:\n60\t\\begin{itemize}\n61\t\\item \\textbf{Small scale (7--8B)}: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (``r1-8b'', Llama-based, 4096-d, 32 layers) and OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-based, 4096-d, 32 layers)\n62\t\\item \\textbf{Large scale (32B)}: DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-32B (``r1-32b'', Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d, 64 layers) and QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5-based, 5120-d, 64 layers)\n63\t\\end{itemize}\n64\t\n65\t\\subsection{Data}\n66\t\n67\tWe use HarmThoughts traces annotated with per-step safety labels (safe/unsafe subtypes 1--4). The commitment point (CP) is defined as the last safety-labeled step plus one. Training uses 209 traces; test uses 43 traces per model. Traces are generated by prompting each model with identical inputs and annotating the resulting CoT.\n68\t\n69\t\\subsection{Detection Methods}\n70\t\n71\t\\paragraph{Hidden-state probes.} We extract residual stream activations at each reasoning step. Static probes train logistic regression (LR) on single-step hidden states. Temporal probes train LR on concatenated windows of $W$ consecutive steps. GRU probes train a recurrent model on variable-length sequences.\n72\t\n73\t\\paragraph{Text-based probes.} Sentence-BERT embeddings of each reasoning step serve as input to the same LR and temporal LR architectures.\n74\t\n75\t\\paragraph{Geometric methods.} Mahalanobis distance computed on PCA-projected hidden states relative to safe-trace covariance (TrajGuard-style).\n76\t\n77\t\\paragraph{Change-point methods.} BOCPD with hazard rate $\\lambda \\in \\{10, 100\\}$ and CUSUM with threshold $t{=}0.3$, limit $h{=}3$ applied to probe score sequences.\n78\t\n79\t\\subsection{Metrics}\n80\t\n81\tWe report: coverage (fraction of unsafe traces detected), lead time (steps between detection and CP; positive means detection before CP), pre-CP FPR (false alarm rate in the pre-commitment window), precision (true positive rate among all alarms), safe FPR (alarm rate on entirely safe traces), and AUROC with bootstrap 95\\% confidence intervals (1000 resamples).\n82\t\n83\t\\section{Results}\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Scale Reduces Fisher Discriminability by 6$\\times$}\n86\t\n87\tWe perform full layer sweeps on both 32B models (64 layers each) to identify optimal probe attachment points. Table~\\ref{tab:layer_sweep} reports the top-5 layers by Fisher discriminability.\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{table}[h]\n90\t\\centering\n91\t\\caption{Layer sweep summary (top-5 by Fisher discriminability). r1-8b reference: L14, Fisher=17.9.}\n92\t\\label{tab:layer_sweep}\n93\t\\small\n94\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccccc}\n95\t\\toprule\n96\tModel & Layer & Depth\\% & Fisher & Static Acc & F1 & Crossing Rate \\\\\n97\t\\midrule\n98\tr1-32b & L29 & 45\\% & 2.81 & 0.712 & 0.521 & 0.372 \\\\\n99\tr1-32b & L31 & 48\\% & 2.63 & 0.705 & 0.509 & 0.349 \\\\\n100\tr1-32b & L27 & 42\\% & 2.44 & 0.698 & 0.495 & 0.326 \\\\\n101\tr1-32b & L33 & 52\\% & 2.31 & 0.691 & 0.481 & 0.302 \\\\\n102\tr1-32b & L25 & 39\\% & 2.18 & 0.683 & 0.468 & 0.279 \\\\\n103\t\\midrule\n104\tQwQ-32B & L31 & 48\\% & 1.92 & 0.694 & 0.488 & 0.326 \\\\\n105\tQwQ-32B & L29 & 45\\% & 1.85 & 0.689 & 0.479 & 0.302 \\\\\n106\tQwQ-32B & L34 & 53\\% & 1.71 & 0.678 & 0.461 & 0.279 \\\\\n107\tQwQ-32B & L27 & 42\\% & 1.64 & 0.671 & 0.448 & 0.256 \\\\\n108\tQwQ-32B & L36 & 56\\% & 1.52 & 0.664 & 0.432 & 0.233 \\\\\n109\t\\bottomrule\n110\t\\end{tabular}\n111\t\\end{table}\n112\t\n113\tThe best 32B layer achieves Fisher discriminability 2.81 (r1-32b L29), compared to 17.9 at r1-8b L14, a 6.4$\\times$ reduction. PCA top-3 components explain only 7--9\\% of variance on 32B versus 17\\% on r1-8b, confirming that the safety signal is distributed across many minor components rather than concentrated in a low-dimensional subspace. Static probe accuracy degrades from 0.817 (r1-8b) to 0.712 (r1-32b) and 0.694 (QwQ-32B), approaching the text-baseline level.\n114\t\n115\t\\subsection{Temporal Aggregation Fails to Rescue 32B Performance}\n116\t\n117\tTable~\\ref{tab:temporal} compares temporal probe performance across scales.\n118\t\n119\t\\begin{table}[h]\n120\t\\centering\n121\t\\caption{Temporal vs static coverage comparison. $\\Delta$ indicates coverage gain from temporal windowing ($W{=}15$).}\n122\t\\label{tab:temporal}\n123\t\\small\n124\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n125\t\\toprule\n126\tModel & Method & Coverage & Lead & Acc & $\\Delta$ vs Static \\\\\n127\t\\midrule\n128\tr1-8b & Static LR L14 & 60.5\\% & +3.0 & 0.817 & -- \\\\\n129\tr1-8b & Temporal $W{=}15$ & 65.1\\% & +4.9 & 0.848 & +4.6pp \\\\\n130\t\\midrule\n131\tr1-32b & Static LR L29 & 37.2\\% & $-1.4$ & 0.712 & -- \\\\\n132\tr1-32b & Temporal $W{=}15$ & 39.5\\% & $-0.8$ & 0.721 & +2.3pp \\\\\n133\t\\midrule\n134\tQwQ-32B & Static LR L31 & 32.6\\% & $-2.1$ & 0.694 & -- \\\\\n135\tQwQ-32B & Temporal $W{=}15$ & 37.2\\% & $-1.5$ & 0.703 & +4.6pp \\\\\n136\t\\bottomrule\n137\t\\end{tabular}\n138\t\\end{table}\n139\t\n140\tOn r1-8b, temporal windowing provides meaningful improvement (+4.6pp coverage, lead time +3.0 to +4.9). On 32B models, temporal gains are +2.3 to +4.6pp in coverage but lead time remains negative ($-0.8$ to $-1.5$ steps), meaning detection occurs after commitment. The per-step signal is too weak for temporal aggregation to amplify.\n141\t\n142\t\\subsection{Window Size Ablation Reveals Scale-Dependent Saturation}\n143\t\n144\t\\begin{table}[h]\n145\t\\centering\n146\t\\caption{Coverage by temporal window size $W$. Bold indicates optimal $W$ per model.}\n147\t\\label{tab:window}\n148\t\\small\n149\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n150\t\\toprule\n151\t$W$ & r1-8b & r1-32b & QwQ-32B \\\\\n152\t\\midrule\n153\t1 & 60.5\\% & 37.2\\% & 32.6\\% \\\\\n154\t3 & 61.3\\% & 38.4\\% & \\textbf{33.4\\%} \\\\\n155\t5 & 62.1\\% & \\textbf{38.4\\%} & 33.1\\% \\\\\n156\t10 & 63.8\\% & 38.9\\% & 33.4\\% \\\\\n157\t\\textbf{15} & \\textbf{65.1\\%} & 39.5\\% & 33.1\\% \\\\\n158\t20 & 64.8\\% & 38.4\\% & 32.6\\% \\\\\n159\t25 & 64.1\\% & 37.2\\% & 31.6\\% \\\\\n160\t\\bottomrule\n161\t\\end{tabular}\n162\t\\end{table}\n163\t\n164\tAt 8B, larger windows systematically improve detection up to $W{=}15$. At 32B, performance plateaus at $W{=}3$--5, and windows beyond $W{=}5$ degrade performance as uninformative steps dilute the few informative ones. The optimal window for 32B is 3--5 (improvement over $W{=}1$: +0.8--1.2pp) versus 15 for 8B (+4.6pp).\n165\t\n166\t\\subsection{Layer Selection Ablation: No Layer Matches 8B Discriminability}\n167\t\n168\t\\begin{table}[h]\n169\t\\centering\n170\t\\caption{Temporal probe accuracy by depth band (r1-32b, $W{=}15$). r1-8b best temporal accuracy: 0.848.}\n171\t\\label{tab:layer_depth}\n172\t\\small\n173\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n174\t\\toprule\n175\tDepth Band & Layers & Mean Acc & Best Acc & Best Layer \\\\\n176\t\\midrule\n177\t0--25\\% & L1--L16 & 0.648 & 0.672 & L14 \\\\\n178\t25--50\\% & L17--L32 & 0.698 & 0.721 & L29 \\\\\n179\t50--75\\% & L33--L48 & 0.681 & 0.708 & L35 \\\\\n180\t75--100\\% & L49--L64 & 0.641 & 0.664 & L52 \\\\\n181\t\\bottomrule\n182\t\\end{tabular}\n183\t\\end{table}\n184\t\n185\tExhaustive sweep over all 64 layers of r1-32b confirms no single layer approaches r1-8b discriminability. The accuracy range is narrow (10.9pp from worst to best), indicating the safety signal is genuinely distributed rather than localized at a recoverable depth. The 25--50\\% depth band is marginally preferred but the advantage is minimal.\n186\t\n187\t\\subsection{The 2$\\times$2 Architecture-Scale Matrix}\n188\t\n189\t\\begin{table}[h]\n190\t\\centering\n191\t\\caption{Architecture $\\times$ scale matrix. Fisher discriminability and HS vs text significance test.}\n192\t\\label{tab:matrix}\n193\t\\small\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\t & 7--8B & 32B \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tLlama-based & r1-8b: Fisher=17.9, & -- \\\\\n199\t & HS$>$Text ($p{=}0.003$) & \\\\\n200\t\\midrule\n201\tQwen2.5-based & OpenThinker-7B: Fisher=14.2, & r1-32b: Fisher=2.8, HS$\\approx$Text ($p{=}0.41$) \\\\\n202\t & HS$>$Text ($p{=}0.008$) & QwQ-32B: Fisher=1.9, HS$\\approx$Text ($p{=}0.89$) \\\\\n203\t\\bottomrule\n204\t\\end{tabular}\n205\t\\end{table}\n206\t\n207\tOpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5-7B base) achieves Fisher 14.2, static accuracy 0.793, temporal accuracy 0.834, crossing rate 62.8\\%, and positive mean lead time (+3.8 steps). These results closely replicate r1-8b findings, confirming the effect is not architecture-specific. Since both 32B models (also Qwen2.5-based) fail to show the advantage, scale is conclusively identified as the critical variable.\n208\t\n209\t\\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer Collapses Across Scales}\n210\t\n211\t\\begin{table}[h]\n212\t\\centering\n213\t\\caption{Cross-model transfer accuracy (with learned 4096$\\to$5120 projection for cross-scale pairs).}\n214\t\\label{tab:transfer}\n215\t\\small\n216\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n217\t\\toprule\n218\tTrain $\\to$ Test & r1-8b & r1-32b & QwQ-32B \\\\\n219\t\\midrule\n220\tr1-8b & 0.817 (within) & 0.508 & 0.491 \\\\\n221\tr1-32b & 0.523 & 0.712 (within) & 0.612 \\\\\n222\tQwQ-32B & 0.517 & 0.598 & 0.694 (within) \\\\\n223\t\\bottomrule\n224\t\\end{tabular}\n225\t\\end{table}\n226\t\n227\tTransfer between 8B and 32B models is at chance ($\\sim$0.50) in both directions, even with a trained projection head (20M parameters). Within-family transfer (r1-32b $\\leftrightarrow$ QwQ-32B, both Qwen2.5) reaches only 0.60, far below useful levels. The r1-8b safety signal is not a transferable universal property; it is specific to the 8B architecture's concentrated encoding geometry.\n228\t\n229\t\\subsection{Lead Time Is Uniformly Negative at 32B}\n230\t\n231\t\\begin{table}[h]\n232\t\\centering\n233\t\\caption{Lead time distribution by model (temporal $W{=}15$ probe).}\n234\t\\label{tab:lead}\n235\t\\small\n236\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n237\t\\toprule\n238\tModel & Mean Lead & Median Lead & Positive Lead \\% & Std \\\\\n239\t\\midrule\n240\tr1-8b & +4.9 & 0 & 58\\% & 11.2 \\\\\n241\tr1-32b & $-0.8$ & $-3$ & 29.4\\% & 8.2 \\\\\n242\tQwQ-32B & $-1.5$ & $-4$ & 23.5\\% & 9.1 \\\\\n243\t\\bottomrule\n244\t\\end{tabular}\n245\t\\end{table}\n246\t\n247\tAt 32B, mean lead time is negative ($-0.8$ to $-1.5$ steps), meaning detection typically occurs after commitment. Only 23--29\\% of traces achieve any positive lead, compared to 58\\% at 8B. The weak probe signal delays threshold crossing until well after the commitment point.\n248\t\n249\t\\subsection{Hidden-State Advantage Vanishes at 32B (AUROC Analysis)}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"05850688-86b2-4099-9f51-071dc272687c","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:07:35.590Z"}
