{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01AWtBGUuEQmBs4p79bjZi8b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019jHFsLYiAHk6BCV8AR2JNd","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_5/projects/contrastive_trace_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T212347/worst/paper.tex","offset":249,"limit":260},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4501,"cache_read_input_tokens":39141,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4501,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":34,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"270854f7-117d-4a81-bddc-43cd28fb1efb"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019jHFsLYiAHk6BCV8AR2JNd","type":"tool_result","content":"249\t\\subsection{Hidden-State Advantage Vanishes at 32B (AUROC Analysis)}\n250\t\n251\t\\begin{table}[h]\n252\t\\centering\n253\t\\caption{AUROC comparison with bootstrap 95\\% CIs (1000 resamples). $p$-values for HS vs Text difference.}\n254\t\\label{tab:auroc}\n255\t\\small\n256\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n257\t\\toprule\n258\tModel & HS Static & HS Temporal & Text Temporal & HS$-$Text (p) \\\\\n259\t\\midrule\n260\tr1-8b & 0.847 [.79,.90] & 0.862 [.81,.91] & 0.759 [.69,.82] & +0.103 ($p{=}0.003$) \\\\\n261\tr1-32b & 0.583 [.50,.66] & 0.598 [.51,.68] & 0.577 [.49,.66] & +0.021 ($p{=}0.412$) \\\\\n262\tQwQ-32B & 0.561 [.48,.64] & 0.574 [.49,.65] & 0.582 [.50,.66] & $-$0.008 ($p{=}0.891$) \\\\\n263\t\\bottomrule\n264\t\\end{tabular}\n265\t\\end{table}\n266\t\n267\tThis is the definitive statistical result. At r1-8b, hidden-state AUROC exceeds text by 10.3 points ($p{=}0.003$). At 32B, the difference is non-significant on both models: +2.1 points on r1-32b ($p{=}0.412$) and $-$0.8 points on QwQ-32B ($p{=}0.891$). Bootstrap CIs for 32B models include 0.50 (chance), confirming near-random discrimination. The result holds across all operating points, eliminating threshold selection as a confound.\n268\t\n269\t\\subsection{Pre-CP False Positive Rate: The Deployment Metric}\n270\t\n271\t\\begin{table}[h]\n272\t\\centering\n273\t\\caption{Pre-CP FPR and precision: HS vs text by model. The HS precision advantage collapses at 32B.}\n274\t\\label{tab:fpr}\n275\t\\small\n276\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n277\t\\toprule\n278\tModel & HS Pre-CP FPR & Text Pre-CP FPR & HS Precision & Text Precision & $\\Delta$ Precision \\\\\n279\t\\midrule\n280\tr1-8b & 16.0\\% & 27.2\\% & 58.3\\% & 48.7\\% & +9.6pp \\\\\n281\tr1-32b & 25.1\\% & 26.8\\% & 50.1\\% & 48.5\\% & +1.6pp \\\\\n282\tQwQ-32B & 30.8\\% & 29.8\\% & 45.1\\% & 46.2\\% & $-$1.1pp \\\\\n283\t\\bottomrule\n284\t\\end{tabular}\n285\t\\end{table}\n286\t\n287\tOn r1-8b, hidden-state probes produce 1.7$\\times$ fewer pre-commitment false alarms and +9.6pp higher precision. On r1-32b, the advantage shrinks to +1.6pp (non-significant). On QwQ-32B, text actually outperforms hidden states by 1.1pp in precision. For deployment at 32B scale, hidden-state extraction provides zero benefit over text monitoring.\n288\t\n289\t\\subsection{Text-Based Baseline Achieves Comparable or Better Lead Time}\n290\t\n291\t\\begin{table}[h]\n292\t\\centering\n293\t\\caption{Full HS vs text comparison on 32B models.}\n294\t\\label{tab:text_full}\n295\t\\small\n296\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n297\t\\toprule\n298\tModel & Modality & Coverage & Pre-CP FPR & Precision & Lead \\\\\n299\t\\midrule\n300\tr1-32b & HS temporal & 39.5\\% & 26.4\\% & 0.492 & $-0.8$ \\\\\n301\tr1-32b & Text temporal & 37.2\\% & 28.1\\% & 0.478 & +1.2 \\\\\n302\t\\midrule\n303\tQwQ-32B & HS temporal & 37.2\\% & 30.8\\% & 0.451 & $-1.5$ \\\\\n304\tQwQ-32B & Text temporal & 34.9\\% & 29.8\\% & 0.462 & +0.8 \\\\\n305\t\\bottomrule\n306\t\\end{tabular}\n307\t\\end{table}\n308\t\n309\tText probes achieve positive lead time on 32B (+1.2, +0.8 steps) while hidden-state probes have negative lead ($-0.8$, $-1.5$). Text content directly reflects reasoning state regardless of model scale (words are interpretable at any parameter count), while hidden-state signal quality degrades with scale. At 32B, text monitoring is not merely equal but slightly superior on the lead time metric.\n310\t\n311\t\\subsection{GRU Probes Cannot Recover the Signal}\n312\t\n313\t\\begin{table}[h]\n314\t\\centering\n315\t\\caption{GRU vs LR on 32B. More expressive probes provide negligible improvement.}\n316\t\\label{tab:gru}\n317\t\\small\n318\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n319\t\\toprule\n320\tModel & LR Temporal Acc & GRU Multi Acc & $\\Delta$ & GRU Crossing & LR Crossing \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\tr1-8b & 0.848 & 0.809 & $-$3.9pp & 76.7\\% & 65.1\\% \\\\\n323\tr1-32b & 0.721 & 0.731 & +1.0pp & 41.8\\% & 39.5\\% \\\\\n324\tQwQ-32B & 0.703 & 0.715 & +1.2pp & 39.5\\% & 37.2\\% \\\\\n325\t\\bottomrule\n326\t\\end{tabular}\n327\t\\end{table}\n328\t\n329\tOn r1-8b, GRU significantly improves crossing rate (76.7\\% vs 65.1\\%) by learning temporal patterns in the signal-rich representation. On 32B, GRU barely outperforms temporal LR (+1pp accuracy, +2pp crossing), confirming the limitation is representational. A more expressive probe trained on noise-dominated input learns noise patterns, not safety signals.\n330\t\n331\t\\subsection{Fine-Grained Supervision Does Not Help}\n332\t\n333\t\\begin{table}[h]\n334\t\\centering\n335\t\\caption{Fine-grained (16-label) vs binary supervision.}\n336\t\\label{tab:taxonomy}\n337\t\\small\n338\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n339\t\\toprule\n340\tModel & Binary Direct Acc & 16-Label Acc & 16$\\to$Binary Acc & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n341\t\\midrule\n342\tr1-8b & 0.817 & 0.512 & 0.831 & +1.4pp \\\\\n343\tr1-32b & 0.712 & 0.318 & 0.724 & +1.2pp \\\\\n344\tQwQ-32B & 0.694 & 0.295 & 0.709 & +1.5pp \\\\\n345\t\\bottomrule\n346\t\\end{tabular}\n347\t\\end{table}\n348\t\n349\tRicher supervision cannot compensate for absent representation structure. On 32B, 16-label accuracy is 30--32\\% (chance for 16 balanced classes would be 6.25\\%, but classes are imbalanced). Collapsing back to binary yields marginal improvement (+1.2--1.5pp), indicating fine-grained labels provide slight regularization but cannot overcome the fundamental lack of separable signal.\n350\t\n351\t\\subsection{Geometric Detection Fails More Severely at Scale}\n352\t\n353\t\\begin{table}[h]\n354\t\\centering\n355\t\\caption{Mahalanobis distance separation (TrajGuard-style). MD values for safe, pre-CP, and post-CP regions.}\n356\t\\label{tab:mahal}\n357\t\\small\n358\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n359\t\\toprule\n360\tModel & Config & Safe MD & Pre-CP MD & Post-CP MD & K5 Crossing\\% \\\\\n361\t\\midrule\n362\tr1-8b & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 6.66 & 6.73 & 6.64 & 30.2\\% \\\\\n363\tr1-32b & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 71.2 & 71.8 & 71.4 & 18.6\\% \\\\\n364\tr1-32b & pca\\_128\\_p75 & 142.3 & 143.1 & 142.6 & 16.3\\% \\\\\n365\tQwQ-32B & pca\\_64\\_p75 & 68.4 & 69.1 & 68.7 & 16.3\\% \\\\\n366\tQwQ-32B & pca\\_128\\_p75 & 137.8 & 138.4 & 137.9 & 14.0\\% \\\\\n367\t\\bottomrule\n368\t\\end{tabular}\n369\t\\end{table}\n370\t\n371\tTrajGuard-style Mahalanobis detection is non-functional on 32B. The delta between safe and unsafe MD is less than 1\\% of the absolute value, meaning safety commitment produces no detectable distributional shift. Detection rates (14--19\\%) fall below the already-failed r1-8b baseline (30.2\\%), consistent with the signal diffusion hypothesis.\n372\t\n373\t\\subsection{Change-Point Detection Degrades Severely}\n374\t\n375\t\\begin{table}[h]\n376\t\\centering\n377\t\\caption{BOCPD and CUSUM on probe score sequences.}\n378\t\\label{tab:cpd}\n379\t\\small\n380\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n381\t\\toprule\n382\tModel & Method & Detection Rate & Mean Lead & Median Lead \\\\\n383\t\\midrule\n384\tr1-8b & BOCPD $\\lambda{=}10$ & 93.0\\% & +3.45 & 0 \\\\\n385\tr1-8b & CUSUM $t{=}0.3,h{=}3$ & 65.1\\% & +0.89 & $-4$ \\\\\n386\t\\midrule\n387\tr1-32b & BOCPD $\\lambda{=}10$ & 58.1\\% & $-2.1$ & $-5$ \\\\\n388\tr1-32b & CUSUM $t{=}0.3,h{=}3$ & 32.6\\% & $-6.2$ & $-9$ \\\\\n389\t\\midrule\n390\tQwQ-32B & BOCPD $\\lambda{=}10$ & 53.5\\% & $-2.8$ & $-6$ \\\\\n391\tQwQ-32B & CUSUM $t{=}0.3,h{=}3$ & 27.9\\% & $-7.1$ & $-11$ \\\\\n392\t\\bottomrule\n393\t\\end{tabular}\n394\t\\end{table}\n395\t\n396\tBOCPD detection drops from 93\\% (r1-8b) to 53--58\\% (32B), with lead time shifting from positive (+3.45) to negative ($-2.1$ to $-2.8$). CUSUM essentially fails (28--33\\% detection, lead $-6$ to $-7$). These methods require a distributional shift in the input signal; when the probe score sequence is essentially flat noise, no downstream method can detect a transition that does not manifest as a signal change.\n397\t\n398\t\\subsection{Oracle Decomposition: The Information Exists But Is Inaccessible}\n399\t\n400\t\\begin{table}[h]\n401\t\\centering\n402\t\\caption{Oracle vs best probe lead time, and hidden-state pre-CP entropy.}\n403\t\\label{tab:oracle}\n404\t\\small\n405\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n406\t\\toprule\n407\tModel & Oracle Lead & Best Probe Lead & Gap & HS Pre-CP Entropy \\\\\n408\t\\midrule\n409\tr1-8b & +31.0 & +4.9 & 26.1 & 0.72 \\\\\n410\tr1-32b & +28.4 & $-0.8$ & 29.2 & 0.31 \\\\\n411\tQwQ-32B & +26.8 & $-1.5$ & 28.3 & 0.28 \\\\\n412\t\\bottomrule\n413\t\\end{tabular}\n414\t\\end{table}\n415\t\n416\tThe oracle (label sequences) achieves +28 step lead at 32B, proving that safety commitment is a real, predictable phenomenon. The gap between oracle and probe is larger at 32B (29.2) than 8B (26.1). Hidden-state pre-CP entropy is much lower at 32B (0.28--0.31 vs 0.72), indicating that 32B hidden states exhibit less step-to-step variation in safety-relevant dimensions despite the oracle showing meaningful dynamics. The model's approach toward commitment is invisible in hidden-state geometry at 32B even though it is clearly present in behavior.\n417\t\n418\t\\section{Discussion}\n419\t\n420\t\\subsection{Why Does Scale Erase the Signal?}\n421\t\n422\tThe 5120-dimensional hidden space with 64 layers at 32B scale creates a representation where many tasks are served simultaneously by largely orthogonal subspaces. Safety commitment information, which at 8B occupies a concentrated subspace (Fisher 17.9, PCA top-3 explains 17\\% of relevant variance), becomes distributed across 50+ minor principal components at 32B (Fisher 2.8, PCA top-3 explains 7--9\\%). A linear probe cannot capture this diffuse structure because it operates as a single hyperplane in 5120 dimensions, requiring the target signal to concentrate along a consistent direction.\n423\t\n424\t\\subsection{Implications for Monitoring Architecture}\n425\t\n426\tThese results have direct implications for the design of safety monitoring systems for production reasoning models:\n427\t\n428\t\\begin{enumerate}\n429\t\\item Hidden-state extraction at 32B scale (or larger) provides zero deployment benefit over text monitoring, while incurring substantial computational overhead for activation caching and inference.\n430\t\\item Text-based monitoring is scale-invariant because semantic content is directly accessible regardless of parameter count. A model stating ``I will help with this harmful request'' is detectable in text at any scale.\n431\t\\item If hidden-state monitoring is to be viable at scale, fundamentally different approaches are needed: multi-layer aggregation, nonlinear subspace methods, or attention-pattern analysis rather than residual-stream probing.\n432\t\\end{enumerate}\n433\t\n434\t\\subsection{Limitations}\n435\t\n436\tOur evaluation is limited to $n{=}43$ test traces per model, resulting in wide confidence intervals. The 32B models tested are both Qwen2.5-based distillations; evaluation on independently trained 32B+ reasoning models (e.g., native DeepSeek-R1-67B or GPT-o1) would strengthen generalizability claims. We test only linear and GRU probes; more complex architectures (transformers, multi-layer perceptrons with many hidden units) might partially recover the signal, though at the cost of the simplicity that makes probe-based monitoring practical.\n437\t\n438\t\\section{Conclusion}\n439\t\n440\tWe demonstrate that the hidden-state advantage for safety commitment detection is a scale-specific phenomenon. At 7--8B scale, hidden-state probes achieve 1.7$\\times$ lower pre-commitment false positive rates than text monitoring, with AUROC advantages of +10 points ($p{<}0.01$). At 32B scale, this advantage vanishes completely: AUROC differences are non-significant ($p{>}0.4$), pre-commitment FPR converges between modalities, and text probes actually achieve better lead time. The Fisher discriminability drop of 6$\\times$ from 8B to 32B, the failure of expressive probes (GRU), temporal aggregation, layer sweeps, and fine-grained supervision to recover performance, and the complete collapse of cross-scale transfer all converge on a single conclusion: safety commitment encoding diffuses with scale in a way that renders current probe-based approaches ineffective. For monitoring architecture design of production reasoning systems at 32B+, hidden-state extraction cannot be justified over simpler text-based alternatives.\n441\t\n442\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n443\t\\bibliography{refs}\n444\t\n445\t\\end{document}\n446\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"aebfccca-1b39-46e9-bf17-dc2f9d1a28b2","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:07:40.951Z"}
