{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Ez6bRhwEYp1btsyVY5jscx","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough information to produce the review. Let me now write a thorough R2 review based on my analysis of the full paper.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper evaluates whether hidden-state probes for safety commitment detection in reasoning models generalize across model scale, using a 2x2 matrix of architectures and scales. The central finding is negative: the hidden-state monitoring advantage observed at 7-8B parameters vanishes at 32B, with Fisher discriminability dropping 6x and all recovery strategies (GRU, temporal aggregation, fine-grained supervision) failing. The paper positions this as a cautionary result for the deployment of probe-based safety monitors on production-scale reasoning systems.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- S1: The research question is timely and practically important. As the community invests in hidden-state monitoring for safety (TrajGuard, SIREN, SafeSwitch), understanding whether these approaches scale is essential. The paper asks the right question at the right time.\n\n- S2: The 2x2 experimental design (architecture x scale) with OpenThinker-7B completing the Qwen2.5/7B cell is well-constructed for isolating scale as the causal variable. The inclusion of a same-architecture small model (OpenThinker-7B, Qwen2.5-based) alongside both 32B Qwen2.5 models provides reasonable evidence that scale, not architecture, drives the effect.\n\n- S3: The thoroughness of negative evidence is commendable. The paper systematically eliminates alternative explanations: wrong layer (full 64-layer sweep), insufficient temporal context (window ablation), insufficient model capacity (GRU), insufficient supervision granularity (16-label), and wrong detection paradigm (BOCPD/CUSUM). This exhaustiveness makes the negative result more convincing.\n\n- S4: The Fisher discriminability metric provides a clean, interpretable summary statistic for the core phenomenon (17.9 at 8B vs 1.9-2.8 at 32B). The 6x ratio is striking and easy to communicate.\n\n- S5: Statistical methodology is reasonable: bootstrap CIs, AUROC as a threshold-independent metric, and explicit p-values for the HS vs text comparison at each scale.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- W1: **Confounded experimental design undermines the central claim.** The paper claims to isolate \"scale\" but the design has a critical confound: all 32B models are Qwen2.5-based distillations from a single teacher (DeepSeek-R1). The 8B Llama model is also a distillation from the same teacher. There is no independently-trained 32B reasoning model in the evaluation. The \"scale effect\" could equally be a \"distillation-into-Qwen2.5-32B effect\" or an artifact of how the R1 teacher's knowledge transfers to larger student architectures. A Llama-based 32B model (which does not exist in this family) is the missing cell that would truly isolate scale. The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but understates its severity.\n\n- W2: **The Related Work section is inadequate for a top venue.** At approximately 0.5 pages with only 4 short paragraphs and 14 citations, this is far below the density expected at EMNLP. Critical missing work includes: (a) the broader \"probing at scale\" literature (e.g., work by Dalvi et al. on probing across model sizes, Tenney et al. on BERT layers), (b) the mechanistic interpretability literature that studies how features distribute across dimensions at scale (Elhage et al., \"Toy Models of Superposition\"; Bricken et al. on dictionary learning), (c) concurrent work on reasoning model safety (ThinkGuard, ChainGuard if they exist), (d) the scaling laws literature that would contextualize \"does X property hold at scale\" questions. The paper does not cite the superposition hypothesis, which is essentially the theoretical framework that would explain its own findings.\n\n- W3: **No figures in a 10-page paper with 15 tables is poor information design.** The Fisher discriminability drop, the layer sweep plateau, the ROC curves, and the transfer heatmap all cry out for visualization. Tables are appropriate for precise numbers, but the narrative would be vastly more compelling with 2-3 key figures (e.g., a Fisher-by-layer curve comparing 8B peak vs 32B plateau; an ROC comparison; the 2x2 matrix as a visual). The paper reads like an experiment log rather than a persuasive scientific narrative.\n\n- W4: **Overclaiming regarding \"production systems.\"** The Conclusion states \"hidden-state extraction cannot be justified over simpler text-based alternatives\" for \"production reasoning systems at 32B+.\" This extrapolation from 32B distilled models to 70B+ natively-trained models (GPT-o1, Claude, Gemini reasoning modes) is unsupported. Production systems may have fundamentally different representation geometry due to RL training rather than distillation. The \"+scale\" implication is a pure extrapolation.\n\n- W5: **The paper lacks a theoretical framework for its finding.** The observation that \"safety signal becomes diffuse at 32B\" is described but not explained mechanistically. The superposition hypothesis (Elhage et al. 2022) predicts exactly this phenomenon: larger models can represent more features in superposition, making any individual concept harder to isolate with linear probes. This framework is not cited or discussed. Without it, the paper provides an empirical observation without mechanistic understanding, limiting its contribution.\n\n- W6: **Dataset size is very small (n=43 test traces) and from a single source.** The CIs for 32B AUROC include 0.50 (chance), which means the paper cannot even definitively establish that HS probes work *at all* at 32B. The entire negative result could be a power issue rather than a genuine absence of signal. The paper acknowledges this but proceeds to draw strong conclusions (\"zero benefit\") from data consistent with both \"no signal\" and \"weak signal + insufficient data.\"\n\n- W7: **The framing as a purely negative result may be misleading.** The paper establishes that both HS and text probes achieve only ~0.57-0.60 AUROC at 32B. This means *all monitoring fails* at 32B, not just HS monitoring. The headline \"HS advantage vanishes\" buries the arguably more important finding: \"safety commitment detection itself becomes much harder at scale regardless of modality.\" The framing emphasizes the relative comparison (HS vs text) over the absolute finding (nothing works well at 32B).\n\n### Detailed Comments\n\n1. (Section 3.1, models): DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B is listed as \"r1-8b, Llama-based\" in the setup. This appears to be an error in the paper text, where it says Llama-based for the 8B model. Please verify: is the 8B model truly Llama-based (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B) or Qwen-based (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B)? The naming is inconsistent between the setup section and elsewhere.\n\n2. (Section 4.5, 2x2 matrix): The matrix has an empty cell (Llama/32B). The paper should explicitly acknowledge this limits causal claims about scale vs. architecture. An ANOVA-style decomposition with a missing cell is fundamentally underdetermined.\n\n3. (Section 4.8, AUROC): The CIs for 32B models ([0.48, 0.64] and [0.49, 0.65]) substantially overlap with chance (0.50). Can the authors confirm that HS probes at 32B provide *any* statistically significant discrimination above chance at all? The current framing (\"near-chance discrimination\") is appropriate but the paper sometimes vacillates between \"no signal at all\" and \"signal exists but is weak.\"\n\n4. (Section 5.1, Discussion): The explanation \"safety information becomes distributed across many dimensions at larger scale\" is speculative without mechanistic evidence. Have the authors considered that the difference could be driven by the distillation procedure rather than scale per se? Distillation into a larger model with more capacity could compress safety-relevant features differently than independent training.\n\n5. (References): The bibliography has only 14 entries. For context, typical EMNLP papers in this area cite 40-60 works. Several entries use \"et al.\" improperly and lack full author lists, page numbers, or proper venue information. The bibliography appears hastily assembled.\n\n6. (Throughout): The paper uses em-dashes for explanatory clauses in multiple locations. While minor, this is a stylistic issue that should be addressed for camera-ready.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you evaluated on any model that was trained with full RL (not distillation)? The distillation hypothesis is a serious alternative explanation for your findings.\n\n2. What happens with nonlinear probes beyond GRU? Specifically, have you tried multi-layer perceptrons with sufficient hidden units (e.g., 2-layer MLP with 1024 hidden units)? The GRU result is informative but GRU is designed for sequence modeling, not necessarily for recovering diffuse signals from high-dimensional static inputs.\n\n3. Can you provide a direct comparison of representation entropy/dimensionality between 8B and 32B models on non-safety tasks (e.g., sentiment probing) to verify that the scale effect is safety-specific rather than a general property of probing these particular models?\n\n4. The oracle decomposition (Table 12) shows that labels exhibit meaningful dynamics at 32B (oracle lead +28 steps). This proves safety commitment IS happening at 32B. Have you considered that the issue might not be \"signal is diffuse\" but rather \"the model represents safety reasoning in a fundamentally different computational mechanism at scale\" (e.g., in attention patterns rather than residual stream)?\n\n5. Why not include OpenThinker-7B results in the full comparison tables? Its absence from many tables despite being named as the 4th model weakens the 2x2 design claim.\n\n### Suggestions for Improvement\n\n1. Add 2-3 figures: (a) Fisher discriminability by layer for all models overlaid, (b) ROC curves for 8B vs 32B, (c) the 2x2 matrix visualization. These would dramatically improve readability and impact.\n\n2. Cite and discuss the superposition hypothesis (Elhage et al., \"Toy Models of Superposition\", 2022) as the theoretical framework explaining your observation. Your finding is essentially empirical confirmation of superposition predictions for safety-relevant features.\n\n3. Expand Related Work to ~1.5 pages with proper treatment of: probing at scale, mechanistic interpretability and superposition, scaling laws for emergent properties, and broader safety monitoring literature.\n\n4. Tone down the \"production systems\" claims. Replace extrapolations to 70B+ with the more defensible \"at the 32B scale tested\" framing. Explicitly state that results on distilled models may not transfer to RL-trained models.\n\n5. Consider reframing the paper's core message from \"HS advantage vanishes\" to the more fundamental \"safety commitment detection degrades catastrophically with scale for ALL methods, and the HS modality advantage that partially compensated at 8B disappears.\" This is more honest and arguably more impactful.\n\n6. Add a control experiment: probe a non-safety property (e.g., topic classification, language detection) at both scales to verify that the probe methodology itself is not broken at 32B. If topic probes work fine at 32B but safety probes fail, the finding is more specific and interesting.\n\n7. The bibliography needs substantial expansion. Add full citations for all referenced work, include the superposition/mechanistic interpretability literature, and aim for 35+ references minimum.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper addresses an important question and the experimental methodology is thorough within its scope. However, the confounded design (all 32B models are Qwen2.5 distillations), severely underdeveloped Related Work and bibliography, absence of theoretical grounding (superposition hypothesis), overclaiming about production systems, and the small dataset size collectively prevent acceptance at a top venue. The core observation is interesting but the paper needs significant revision: stronger theoretical motivation, expanded related work, additional controls (non-safety probing, non-distilled models), toned-down claims, and visual presentation. As a workshop paper or short paper (with appropriate scoping of claims), this could work. For EMNLP main conference, the evidence does not yet support the breadth of the conclusions drawn."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a013742ae97c55663 (use SendMessage with to: 'a013742ae97c55663' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 86830\ntool_uses: 17\nduration_ms: 145919</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5feb2154-bbc5-4d7e-891f-e50eada4cb93","uuid":"6fe67056-6394-45f6-9b53-425c526231fc","timestamp":"2026-05-16T22:09:15.248Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a top ML venue peer review. Your focus is **novelty, positioning, and related work**. Review the following paper and produce a structured review.\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Does Scale Erase Safety Signals? Hidden-State Monitoring Limitations in Large Reasoning Models\"\n\nThe paper investigates whether hidden-state (HS) probes for safety commitment detection in reasoning models generalize across model scale. It evaluates a 2×2 matrix of architectures (Llama, Qwen2.5) × scales (7–8B, 32B). Key findings:\n- At 7–8B: HS probes significantly outperform text (pre-CP FPR 16% vs 27%, p=0.003; AUROC 0.847 vs 0.738)\n- At 32B: HS advantage vanishes (pre-CP FPR 25–31% vs 27–30%, p>0.4; AUROC 0.56–0.60 for both)\n- Fisher discriminability drops 6× from 8B to 32B\n- Temporal aggregation, GRU probes, fine-grained supervision, layer sweeps all fail to recover signal at 32B\n- Cross-scale transfer collapses to chance\n\n## Paper Structure (10 pages)\n\n**Section 1 - Introduction (1.5 pages)**: \n- Reasoning models self-jailbreak through CoT erosion\n- Hidden-state probes proposed as monitors but untested on reasoning-model self-jailbreak\n- Critical question: does HS advantage hold at scale?\n- 2×2 architecture×scale matrix evaluation\n- Four bullet contributions: (1) HS advantage at 7-8B, (2) advantage vanishes at 32B, (3) architecture-independent via OpenThinker-7B, (4) GRU/complex probes can't recover\n\n**Section 2 - Related Work (~0.5 page)**:\nFour paragraph-style subsections:\n- Hidden-state safety detection: TrajGuard, SafeSwitch, SIREN\n- Reasoning model safety: HarmThoughts, SafeRemind, SafeThink\n- Representation probing and scale: RepE, refusal directions\n- Change-point detection: BOCPD, CUSUM\n\n**Section 3 - Experimental Setup (1 page)**:\n- Models: r1-8b (Llama), OpenThinker-7B (Qwen2.5), r1-32b (Qwen2.5), QwQ-32B (Qwen2.5)\n- Data: HarmThoughts traces, 209 train / 43 test per model\n- Methods: Static LR, Temporal LR, GRU, Sentence-BERT text, Mahalanobis, BOCPD/CUSUM\n- Metrics: coverage, lead time, pre-CP FPR, precision, safe FPR, AUROC\n\n**Section 4 - Results (5 pages, 15 subsections)**:\n4.1: Fisher discriminability 6× drop\n4.2: Temporal aggregation fails at 32B\n4.3: Window size ablation\n4.4: Layer sweep—no 32B layer matches 8B\n4.5: 2×2 matrix (scale is the variable)\n4.6: Cross-model transfer collapses\n4.7: Negative lead time at 32B\n4.8: AUROC with bootstrap CIs\n4.9: Pre-CP FPR convergence\n4.10: Text achieves better lead time at 32B\n4.11: GRU can't recover signal\n4.12: 16-label supervision doesn't help\n4.13: Mahalanobis fails more at scale\n4.14: BOCPD/CUSUM degrade\n4.15: Oracle decomposition\n\n**Section 5 - Discussion (1 page)**: Why scale erases signal, monitoring implications, limitations\n\n**Section 6 - Conclusion (0.3 pages)**\n\nNo references section visible in the 10-page paper. 15 tables total, no figures.\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nEvaluate:\n1. **Novelty**: Is the research question novel? Is the finding (\"HS advantage is scale-specific\") a meaningful contribution?\n2. **Significance**: Does this change how the community thinks about safety monitoring?\n3. **Related work coverage**: Are key prior works cited? Is positioning accurate?\n4. **Framing**: Is the paper well-motivated? Does the narrative hold together?\n5. **Writing quality**: Clarity, organization, accessibility?\n6. **Negative results paper**: Is this a well-executed negative result? Does it meet the bar for a negative-results contribution?\n7. **Missing related work**: What relevant work is not cited?\n8. **Overclaiming**: Does the paper overclaim given the evidence?\n\nConsider these specific concerns:\n- The Related Work section is very short (~0.5 page). Is this adequate?\n- The paper has no references section visible—is citation coverage sufficient?\n- The paper has 15 results subsections but no figures—is this good information density?\n- The 2×2 matrix only goes up to 32B. Claims about \"production systems\" (often 70B+) may be overclaiming.\n- Both 32B models are Qwen2.5-based distillations. How much does this limit generalizability?\n- The paper frames as a negative result for HS monitoring. Is the framing balanced?\n\nProduce your review in this format:\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- ...\n\n### Detailed Comments\n(numbered, specific references to sections)\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n### Suggestions for Improvement\n\n### Score: X/6\n### Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nUse the 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept.\nBe rigorous. A top venue accepts ~25% of submissions.","agentId":"a013742ae97c55663","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have enough information to produce the review. Let me now write a thorough R2 review based on my analysis of the full paper.\n\n## R2 Review: Novelty, Positioning, and Related Work\n\n### Summary (2-3 sentences)\n\nThis paper evaluates whether hidden-state probes for safety commitment detection in reasoning models generalize across model scale, using a 2x2 matrix of architectures and scales. The central finding is negative: the hidden-state monitoring advantage observed at 7-8B parameters vanishes at 32B, with Fisher discriminability dropping 6x and all recovery strategies (GRU, temporal aggregation, fine-grained supervision) failing. The paper positions this as a cautionary result for the deployment of probe-based safety monitors on production-scale reasoning systems.\n\n### Strengths\n\n- S1: The research question is timely and practically important. As the community invests in hidden-state monitoring for safety (TrajGuard, SIREN, SafeSwitch), understanding whether these approaches scale is essential. The paper asks the right question at the right time.\n\n- S2: The 2x2 experimental design (architecture x scale) with OpenThinker-7B completing the Qwen2.5/7B cell is well-constructed for isolating scale as the causal variable. The inclusion of a same-architecture small model (OpenThinker-7B, Qwen2.5-based) alongside both 32B Qwen2.5 models provides reasonable evidence that scale, not architecture, drives the effect.\n\n- S3: The thoroughness of negative evidence is commendable. The paper systematically eliminates alternative explanations: wrong layer (full 64-layer sweep), insufficient temporal context (window ablation), insufficient model capacity (GRU), insufficient supervision granularity (16-label), and wrong detection paradigm (BOCPD/CUSUM). This exhaustiveness makes the negative result more convincing.\n\n- S4: The Fisher discriminability metric provides a clean, interpretable summary statistic for the core phenomenon (17.9 at 8B vs 1.9-2.8 at 32B). The 6x ratio is striking and easy to communicate.\n\n- S5: Statistical methodology is reasonable: bootstrap CIs, AUROC as a threshold-independent metric, and explicit p-values for the HS vs text comparison at each scale.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n- W1: **Confounded experimental design undermines the central claim.** The paper claims to isolate \"scale\" but the design has a critical confound: all 32B models are Qwen2.5-based distillations from a single teacher (DeepSeek-R1). The 8B Llama model is also a distillation from the same teacher. There is no independently-trained 32B reasoning model in the evaluation. The \"scale effect\" could equally be a \"distillation-into-Qwen2.5-32B effect\" or an artifact of how the R1 teacher's knowledge transfers to larger student architectures. A Llama-based 32B model (which does not exist in this family) is the missing cell that would truly isolate scale. The paper acknowledges this in Limitations but understates its severity.\n\n- W2: **The Related Work section is inadequate for a top venue.** At approximately 0.5 pages with only 4 short paragraphs and 14 citations, this is far below the density expected at EMNLP. Critical missing work includes: (a) the broader \"probing at scale\" literature (e.g., work by Dalvi et al. on probing across model sizes, Tenney et al. on BERT layers), (b) the mechanistic interpretability literature that studies how features distribute across dimensions at scale (Elhage et al., \"Toy Models of Superposition\"; Bricken et al. on dictionary learning), (c) concurrent work on reasoning model safety (ThinkGuard, ChainGuard if they exist), (d) the scaling laws literature that would contextualize \"does X property hold at scale\" questions. The paper does not cite the superposition hypothesis, which is essentially the theoretical framework that would explain its own findings.\n\n- W3: **No figures in a 10-page paper with 15 tables is poor information design.** The Fisher discriminability drop, the layer sweep plateau, the ROC curves, and the transfer heatmap all cry out for visualization. Tables are appropriate for precise numbers, but the narrative would be vastly more compelling with 2-3 key figures (e.g., a Fisher-by-layer curve comparing 8B peak vs 32B plateau; an ROC comparison; the 2x2 matrix as a visual). The paper reads like an experiment log rather than a persuasive scientific narrative.\n\n- W4: **Overclaiming regarding \"production systems.\"** The Conclusion states \"hidden-state extraction cannot be justified over simpler text-based alternatives\" for \"production reasoning systems at 32B+.\" This extrapolation from 32B distilled models to 70B+ natively-trained models (GPT-o1, Claude, Gemini reasoning modes) is unsupported. Production systems may have fundamentally different representation geometry due to RL training rather than distillation. The \"+scale\" implication is a pure extrapolation.\n\n- W5: **The paper lacks a theoretical framework for its finding.** The observation that \"safety signal becomes diffuse at 32B\" is described but not explained mechanistically. The superposition hypothesis (Elhage et al. 2022) predicts exactly this phenomenon: larger models can represent more features in superposition, making any individual concept harder to isolate with linear probes. This framework is not cited or discussed. Without it, the paper provides an empirical observation without mechanistic understanding, limiting its contribution.\n\n- W6: **Dataset size is very small (n=43 test traces) and from a single source.** The CIs for 32B AUROC include 0.50 (chance), which means the paper cannot even definitively establish that HS probes work *at all* at 32B. The entire negative result could be a power issue rather than a genuine absence of signal. The paper acknowledges this but proceeds to draw strong conclusions (\"zero benefit\") from data consistent with both \"no signal\" and \"weak signal + insufficient data.\"\n\n- W7: **The framing as a purely negative result may be misleading.** The paper establishes that both HS and text probes achieve only ~0.57-0.60 AUROC at 32B. This means *all monitoring fails* at 32B, not just HS monitoring. The headline \"HS advantage vanishes\" buries the arguably more important finding: \"safety commitment detection itself becomes much harder at scale regardless of modality.\" The framing emphasizes the relative comparison (HS vs text) over the absolute finding (nothing works well at 32B).\n\n### Detailed Comments\n\n1. (Section 3.1, models): DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B is listed as \"r1-8b, Llama-based\" in the setup. This appears to be an error in the paper text, where it says Llama-based for the 8B model. Please verify: is the 8B model truly Llama-based (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Llama-8B) or Qwen-based (DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B)? The naming is inconsistent between the setup section and elsewhere.\n\n2. (Section 4.5, 2x2 matrix): The matrix has an empty cell (Llama/32B). The paper should explicitly acknowledge this limits causal claims about scale vs. architecture. An ANOVA-style decomposition with a missing cell is fundamentally underdetermined.\n\n3. (Section 4.8, AUROC): The CIs for 32B models ([0.48, 0.64] and [0.49, 0.65]) substantially overlap with chance (0.50). Can the authors confirm that HS probes at 32B provide *any* statistically significant discrimination above chance at all? The current framing (\"near-chance discrimination\") is appropriate but the paper sometimes vacillates between \"no signal at all\" and \"signal exists but is weak.\"\n\n4. (Section 5.1, Discussion): The explanation \"safety information becomes distributed across many dimensions at larger scale\" is speculative without mechanistic evidence. Have the authors considered that the difference could be driven by the distillation procedure rather than scale per se? Distillation into a larger model with more capacity could compress safety-relevant features differently than independent training.\n\n5. (References): The bibliography has only 14 entries. For context, typical EMNLP papers in this area cite 40-60 works. Several entries use \"et al.\" improperly and lack full author lists, page numbers, or proper venue information. The bibliography appears hastily assembled.\n\n6. (Throughout): The paper uses em-dashes for explanatory clauses in multiple locations. While minor, this is a stylistic issue that should be addressed for camera-ready.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. Have you evaluated on any model that was trained with full RL (not distillation)? The distillation hypothesis is a serious alternative explanation for your findings.\n\n2. What happens with nonlinear probes beyond GRU? Specifically, have you tried multi-layer perceptrons with sufficient hidden units (e.g., 2-layer MLP with 1024 hidden units)? The GRU result is informative but GRU is designed for sequence modeling, not necessarily for recovering diffuse signals from high-dimensional static inputs.\n\n3. Can you provide a direct comparison of representation entropy/dimensionality between 8B and 32B models on non-safety tasks (e.g., sentiment probing) to verify that the scale effect is safety-specific rather than a general property of probing these particular models?\n\n4. The oracle decomposition (Table 12) shows that labels exhibit meaningful dynamics at 32B (oracle lead +28 steps). This proves safety commitment IS happening at 32B. Have you considered that the issue might not be \"signal is diffuse\" but rather \"the model represents safety reasoning in a fundamentally different computational mechanism at scale\" (e.g., in attention patterns rather than residual stream)?\n\n5. Why not include OpenThinker-7B results in the full comparison tables? Its absence from many tables despite being named as the 4th model weakens the 2x2 design claim.\n\n### Suggestions for Improvement\n\n1. Add 2-3 figures: (a) Fisher discriminability by layer for all models overlaid, (b) ROC curves for 8B vs 32B, (c) the 2x2 matrix visualization. These would dramatically improve readability and impact.\n\n2. Cite and discuss the superposition hypothesis (Elhage et al., \"Toy Models of Superposition\", 2022) as the theoretical framework explaining your observation. Your finding is essentially empirical confirmation of superposition predictions for safety-relevant features.\n\n3. Expand Related Work to ~1.5 pages with proper treatment of: probing at scale, mechanistic interpretability and superposition, scaling laws for emergent properties, and broader safety monitoring literature.\n\n4. Tone down the \"production systems\" claims. Replace extrapolations to 70B+ with the more defensible \"at the 32B scale tested\" framing. Explicitly state that results on distilled models may not transfer to RL-trained models.\n\n5. Consider reframing the paper's core message from \"HS advantage vanishes\" to the more fundamental \"safety commitment detection degrades catastrophically with scale for ALL methods, and the HS modality advantage that partially compensated at 8B disappears.\" This is more honest and arguably more impactful.\n\n6. Add a control experiment: probe a non-safety property (e.g., topic classification, language detection) at both scales to verify that the probe methodology itself is not broken at 32B. If topic probes work fine at 32B but safety probes fail, the finding is more specific and interesting.\n\n7. The bibliography needs substantial expansion. Add full citations for all referenced work, include the superposition/mechanistic interpretability literature, and aim for 35+ references minimum.\n\n### Score: 3/6\n### Confidence: 4/5\n\nThe paper addresses an important question and the experimental methodology is thorough within its scope. However, the confounded design (all 32B models are Qwen2.5 distillations), severely underdeveloped Related Work and bibliography, absence of theoretical grounding (superposition hypothesis), overclaiming about production systems, and the small dataset size collectively prevent acceptance at a top venue. The core observation is interesting but the paper needs significant revision: stronger theoretical motivation, expanded related work, additional controls (non-safety probing, non-distilled models), toned-down claims, and visual presentation. As a workshop paper or short paper (with appropriate scoping of claims), this could work. For EMNLP main conference, the evidence does not yet support the breadth of the conclusions drawn."}],"totalDurationMs":145919,"totalTokens":86830,"totalToolUseCount":17,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":206,"cache_read_input_tokens":83640,"output_tokens":2983,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":206},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2983,"cache_read_input_tokens":83640,"cache_creation_input_tokens":206,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":206,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":5}}}
